(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1b.82fa2956fa.js"]={"7":[{"id":"sdl07_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply the renal clearance equation to calculate the volume of plasma cleared of a substance per unit time, evaluating how dynamic changes in plasma concentration and urine flow rate affect the clearance of glomerular markers.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the correct renal clearance equation for a substance x?","choices":{"A":"Cx = [P]x / ([U]x × urine flow rate)","B":"Cx = ([U]x + [P]x) × urine flow rate","C":"Cx = ([U]x × [P]x) × urine flow rate","D":"Cx = urine flow rate / ([U]x × [P]x)","E":"Cx = ([U]x × urine flow rate) / [P]x"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Renal clearance equals the urinary excretion rate of a substance, urine concentration times urine flow rate, divided by its plasma concentration, giving units of volume per unit time (e.g., mL/min).","boardPrep":"Clearance = ([U] × V̇) / [P] — the plasma concentration always sits alone in the denominator, never multiplied or added.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — this exact formula, applied specifically to inulin, is what defines GFR itself in Objective 3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply the renal clearance equation to calculate the volume of plasma cleared of a substance per unit time, evaluating how dynamic changes in plasma concentration and urine flow rate affect the clearance of glomerular markers.","batch":1,"stem":"For a fixed plasma concentration of a substance, how does its renal clearance change as urinary excretion rate increases?","choices":{"A":"Clearance rises, but more slowly than excretion rate","B":"Clearance increases proportionally with excretion rate","C":"Clearance remains constant as excretion rate rises","D":"Clearance becomes undefined at high excretion rates","E":"Clearance rises briefly, then plateaus at a maximum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Renal clearance is the ratio of urinary excretion rate to plasma concentration, so for a fixed plasma concentration, clearance rises directly in proportion to any increase in urinary excretion rate.","boardPrep":"Clearance tracks excretion rate directly (for fixed plasma concentration) — more excreted per minute always means more plasma cleared per minute.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — this proportionality is exactly what keeps inulin clearance (GFR) constant even as urine flow rate changes, per the worked hydration example","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply the renal clearance equation to calculate the volume of plasma cleared of a substance per unit time, evaluating how dynamic changes in plasma concentration and urine flow rate affect the clearance of glomerular markers.","batch":1,"stem":"A person drinks a large volume of water, and urine flow rate rises from 1 mL/min to 5 mL/min. Urine inulin concentration falls proportionally by dilution. What happens to calculated GFR?","choices":{"A":"GFR stays unchanged since the formula adjusts for the concentration drop","B":"GFR increases fivefold, tracking the same fivefold rise seen in urine flow rate","C":"The diluted inulin concentration causes the formula to calculate a reduced GFR","D":"GFR becomes impossible to calculate without a corresponding plasma inulin sample","E":"GFR increases temporarily before returning to baseline"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because urine inulin concentration falls by dilution in exact proportion to the rise in urine flow rate, the product of the two (urinary inulin excretion) stays the same, so calculated GFR (inulin clearance) remains constant despite the large change in urine flow rate. Because inulin is filtered but neither reabsorbed nor secreted, its clearance tracks GFR alone, so no transient rise in calculated GFR follows the water load.","boardPrep":"Increased water intake dilutes urine concentration proportionally to the flow-rate increase, leaving the calculated clearance of a purely filtered marker unchanged — a classic \"trick\" scenario on exams.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — this same excretion-rate-times-concentration invariance is the mathematical reason clearance calculations are robust to hydration status","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the clearance ratio of a substance relative to inulin clearance to determine whether a solute undergoes net tubular reabsorption, net tubular secretion, or is handled strictly as a glomerular marker.","batch":1,"stem":"A substance has a clearance ratio (Cx / Cinulin) of exactly 1.0. What does this indicate about its renal handling?","choices":{"A":"It is filtered and partially reabsorbed by the tubule","B":"It is filtered and partially secreted by the tubule","C":"It is too large to cross the glomerular filtration barrier","D":"It is filtered but neither reabsorbed nor secreted","E":"It is filtered and secreted at a rate exceeding its reabsorption"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A clearance ratio of exactly 1.0 means the substance's clearance equals inulin's clearance, which only occurs when the substance is freely filtered and then neither reabsorbed nor secreted by the renal tubule, making it a glomerular marker just like inulin.","boardPrep":"Clearance ratio = 1.0 defines a glomerular marker: filtered, and nothing more happens to it in the tubule.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — this exact glomerular-marker property is what makes inulin (ratio always 1.0 by definition) the reference standard used throughout Objective 1","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the clearance ratio of a substance relative to inulin clearance to determine whether a solute undergoes net tubular reabsorption, net tubular secretion, or is handled strictly as a glomerular marker.","batch":1,"stem":"A substance has a clearance ratio of 0.3. What does this indicate about its renal handling?","choices":{"A":"It is filtered and then partially secreted","B":"It is not filtered across the glomerular capillaries","C":"It is filtered and then partially reabsorbed","D":"It undergoes net secretion exceeding filtration","E":"It is neither filtered nor reabsorbed nor secreted"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A clearance ratio below 1.0 means the substance's clearance is lower than inulin's, which occurs when a filtered substance is subsequently reabsorbed by the tubule, removing some of the filtered load before it reaches the urine. Net secretion would give a clearance higher than inulin's, a ratio above 1.0, as with PAH.","boardPrep":"Clearance ratio < 1.0 signals net tubular reabsorption; the further below 1.0, the greater the fraction reabsorbed.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — this reabsorption-indicating ratio is exactly the pattern seen for Na+, glucose, and amino acids in the worked clearance-ratio examples","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the clearance ratio of a substance relative to inulin clearance to determine whether a solute undergoes net tubular reabsorption, net tubular secretion, or is handled strictly as a glomerular marker.","batch":1,"stem":"A substance has a clearance ratio of 4.5, higher than inulin's. What does this indicate about its renal handling?","choices":{"A":"It is filtered and then secreted by the tubule","B":"It is filtered and then reabsorbed by the tubule","C":"It is not filtered across the glomerular capillaries","D":"It is reabsorbed and secreted in exactly equal amounts","E":"It bypasses renal handling and undergoes hepatic clearance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A clearance ratio greater than 1.0 means the substance's clearance exceeds inulin's, which can only occur if the renal tubule adds additional substance to the urine beyond what was filtered, meaning the substance undergoes net tubular secretion.","boardPrep":"Clearance ratio > 1.0 signals net tubular secretion — organic acids like PAH and organic bases are the classic examples with the highest ratios.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — this secretion-indicating ratio is the basis for using PAH clearance to estimate renal plasma flow, since it approaches complete single-pass extraction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the physiological properties of an ideal exogenous glomerular marker (such as inulin) with clinical endogenous markers (such as creatinine and blood urea nitrogen), evaluating the clinical convenience and the specific physiological limitations of using creatinine clearance to estimate GFR.","batch":1,"stem":"Why is inulin, rather than creatinine, considered the ideal exogenous marker for measuring GFR?","choices":{"A":"Inulin is produced endogenously through skeletal muscle metabolism processes","B":"Inulin undergoes no reabsorption or secretion after filtration","C":"Inulin can be measured in timed urine samples but is undetectable in plasma","D":"Inulin persists in the plasma for a notably shorter duration","E":"Inulin undergoes substantial reabsorption after filtration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Inulin is freely filtered and then neither reabsorbed nor secreted, making its clearance exactly equal to GFR, whereas creatinine is freely filtered but also secreted to a small extent by the renal tubule, causing creatinine clearance to slightly overestimate true GFR.","boardPrep":"Inulin clearance = GFR exactly; creatinine clearance slightly overestimates GFR because of its small secreted component.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — this same small secretion-driven overestimate is the specific limitation clinicians accept in exchange for creatinine's practical convenience","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the physiological properties of an ideal exogenous glomerular marker (such as inulin) with clinical endogenous markers (such as creatinine and blood urea nitrogen), evaluating the clinical convenience and the specific physiological limitations of using creatinine clearance to estimate GFR.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the main practical advantage of using creatinine clearance over inulin clearance to estimate GFR in clinical practice?","choices":{"A":"Creatinine clearance is consistently more accurate than inulin clearance","B":"Creatinine is filtered but is not secreted by the tubule","C":"Creatinine measurement requires a specialized blood assay","D":"Creatinine clearance stays fixed despite changes in GFR","E":"Creatinine is endogenous and does not require infusion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Creatinine is produced endogenously by muscle metabolism at a roughly constant rate, so its plasma concentration can be measured without infusing an exogenous substance, unlike inulin, which must be continuously infused to maintain a steady plasma level.","boardPrep":"The clinical tradeoff: inulin is the accurate but impractical exogenous marker; creatinine is the convenient endogenous marker with a small, accepted overestimate.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — this same endogenous convenience is why creatinine, not inulin, is the marker used in Objective 4's BUN/creatinine ratio calculations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the physiological properties of an ideal exogenous glomerular marker (such as inulin) with clinical endogenous markers (such as creatinine and blood urea nitrogen), evaluating the clinical convenience and the specific physiological limitations of using creatinine clearance to estimate GFR.","batch":1,"stem":"Both BUN and serum creatinine are used clinically to estimate GFR. What property do they share that makes this possible?","choices":{"A":"Both are actively secreted by the proximal tubule","B":"Both are synthesized directly within the glomerulus","C":"Both freely cross cell membranes throughout the body","D":"Both are cleared from plasma through glomerular filtration","E":"Both are reabsorbed in full by the proximal tubule"},"correct":"D","explanation":"BUN and serum creatinine can both be used to estimate GFR because both urea and creatinine are filtered across the glomerular capillaries, so a decrease in GFR reduces their filtration and causes both to accumulate in the plasma.","boardPrep":"BUN and creatinine both rise with falling GFR because both depend on glomerular filtration to be cleared — the difference between them lies in what happens after filtration.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — this shared filtration-dependence is the premise behind interpreting the BUN/creatinine ratio in Objective 4","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Utilize the plasma blood urea nitrogen to creatinine ratio to clinically differentiate prerenal azotemia (ratio greater than 20 to 1) from intrinsic renal parenchymal disease (ratio less than 15 to 1), tracing these distinct biochemical profiles to proximal tubular urea reabsorption driven by volume contraction.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with volume contraction from severe vomiting has a BUN/creatinine ratio of 25:1. What type of azotemia does this represent?","choices":{"A":"Intrinsic renal azotemia from parenchymal damage","B":"Prerenal azotemia from hypoperfusion","C":"Postrenal azotemia from urinary tract obstruction","D":"Combined prerenal and intrinsic renal azotemia","E":"No azotemia, since the ratio is within normal limits"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A BUN/creatinine ratio greater than 20:1, combined with a history of volume contraction, is the classic signature of prerenal azotemia, in which decreased renal perfusion drives increased proximal tubular reabsorption of urea disproportionately more than creatinine.","boardPrep":"BUN/creatinine ratio > 20:1 with a volume-contraction history = prerenal azotemia — one of the most frequently tested lab-ratio patterns on boards.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — this elevated ratio is the direct biochemical signature of the proximal urea reabsorption mechanism traced in this same objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Utilize the plasma blood urea nitrogen to creatinine ratio to clinically differentiate prerenal azotemia (ratio greater than 20 to 1) from intrinsic renal parenchymal disease (ratio less than 15 to 1), tracing these distinct biochemical profiles to proximal tubular urea reabsorption driven by volume contraction.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with chronic kidney disease has both an elevated BUN and an elevated serum creatinine, but the BUN/creatinine ratio remains around 12:1. What does this ratio pattern suggest?","choices":{"A":"Intrinsic renal parenchymal dysfunction","B":"Prerenal azotemia from volume contraction","C":"Postrenal obstruction of the urinary tract","D":"A laboratory measurement error","E":"Resolving prerenal azotemia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A BUN/creatinine ratio below 15:1 despite elevations in both values reflects intrinsic renal parenchymal disease, where reduced GFR raises both urea and creatinine together without the disproportionate proximal urea reabsorption seen in volume-contraction states. Postrenal obstruction gives a variable ratio and needs evidence of blocked outflow, a laboratory error would not produce matching chronic rises in both values, and prerenal azotemia, resolving or not, is defined by a ratio above 20:1.","boardPrep":"BUN/creatinine ratio < 15:1 with both values elevated = intrinsic renal disease — the ratio distinguishes the mechanism, not just the presence, of azotemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — the absence of disproportionate urea reabsorption here is what separates intrinsic renal disease from prerenal azotemia's volume-driven mechanism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Utilize the plasma blood urea nitrogen to creatinine ratio to clinically differentiate prerenal azotemia (ratio greater than 20 to 1) from intrinsic renal parenchymal disease (ratio less than 15 to 1), tracing these distinct biochemical profiles to proximal tubular urea reabsorption driven by volume contraction.","batch":1,"stem":"In prerenal azotemia from volume contraction, why does BUN increase more than serum creatinine, despite both being filtered at the glomerulus?","choices":{"A":"Creatinine is actively secreted during volume contraction, offsetting its filtered-load decrease","B":"Urea is produced in far larger quantities during states of volume contraction","C":"Proximal tubular reabsorption disproportionately affects urea relative to creatinine","D":"Creatinine is metabolized into a nontoxic byproduct during hypovolemia","E":"Urea filtration rises while creatinine filtration simultaneously falls"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Volume contraction increases proximal tubular reabsorption of essentially all filtered solutes, and because urea (unlike creatinine) is substantially reabsorbed under normal conditions, this enhanced reabsorption raises BUN disproportionately more than serum creatinine, widening the ratio.","boardPrep":"The BUN/creatinine ratio widens in volume contraction specifically because urea, not creatinine, is subject to reabsorption that intensifies with hypovolemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — this same enhanced-proximal-reabsorption mechanism is the direct downstream consequence of the volume-contraction states studied in SDL 6","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply the renal clearance equation to calculate the volume of plasma cleared of a substance per unit time, evaluating how dynamic changes in plasma concentration and urine flow rate affect the clearance of glomerular markers.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man is enrolled in a phase 1 study of an investigational drug, substance x. He is healthy and takes no other medications. Blood pressure is 122/78 mm Hg, and his serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). During a timed collection at steady state, he produces 2 mL/min of urine with a substance x concentration of 60 mg/mL, and his plasma concentration of substance x is 2 mg/mL. What is the renal clearance of substance x?","choices":{"A":"30 mL/min","B":"60 mL/min","C":"120 mL/min","D":"15 mL/min","E":"2 mL/min"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Clearance equals urine concentration times urine flow rate, divided by plasma concentration: (60 mg/mL × 2 mL/min) / 2 mg/mL = 120 mg/min / 2 mg/mL = 60 mL/min.","boardPrep":"Step 1: clearance = (U × V)/P; compute the excretion rate in mg/min, divide by plasma concentration, and keep units in mL/min. COMLEX: the same formula with inulin gives GFR.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 1 — this same calculation, applied specifically to inulin, becomes the GFR measurement developed further in Objective 3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply the renal clearance equation to calculate the volume of plasma cleared of a substance per unit time, evaluating how dynamic changes in plasma concentration and urine flow rate affect the clearance of glomerular markers.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman volunteers for a renal physiology study. She is healthy and takes no medications. Blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. Laboratory studies show a serum glucose of 88 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), serum albumin of 4.4 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL), and creatinine of 0.7 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). The renal clearances of both albumin and glucose are measured as approximately zero. Do these two findings reflect the same underlying mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because both substances fail to cross the glomerular filtration barrier","B":"Yes, because both substances undergo full reabsorption after filtration","C":"No, because albumin is reabsorbed after filtration while glucose is not filtered at the glomerulus","D":"No, because albumin is not filtered while glucose is filtered and then reabsorbed","E":"No, because both are secreted at rates that exactly cancel their filtration"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Albumin's near-zero clearance reflects the fact that it is almost entirely restricted from filtration across the glomerular capillaries by size and charge, whereas glucose's near-zero clearance reflects a different mechanism: glucose is freely filtered but then completely reabsorbed by the proximal tubule under normal plasma concentrations.","boardPrep":"A clearance near zero does not always mean the same thing — always ask whether the substance failed to be filtered, or was filtered and then fully reabsorbed.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — glucosuria appears once plasma glucose exceeds the renal threshold of about 200 mg/dL, before whole-kidney Tm is reached","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply the renal clearance equation to calculate the volume of plasma cleared of a substance per unit time, evaluating how dynamic changes in plasma concentration and urine flow rate affect the clearance of glomerular markers.","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman is evaluated as a potential living kidney donor for her brother. She is healthy and takes no medications. Blood pressure is 116/74 mm Hg, and her serum creatinine is 0.7 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). To measure her GFR, she receives a continuous inulin infusion and produces 1.44 L of urine over 24 hours. Her urine inulin concentration is 150 mg/mL, and her plasma inulin concentration is 1 mg/mL. What is her GFR?","choices":{"A":"144 mL/min","B":"100 mL/min","C":"120 mL/min","D":"1.5 mL/min","E":"150 mL/min"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Urine flow rate is 1.44 L over 1440 minutes, or 1.0 mL/min; GFR equals inulin clearance, (150 mg/mL × 1.0 mL/min) / 1 mg/mL = 150 mL/min.","boardPrep":"Always convert a 24-hour urine volume to mL/min (divide by 1440 minutes) before plugging it into the clearance equation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — inulin clearance, equal to GFR, is the denominator of every clearance ratio","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the clearance ratio of a substance relative to inulin clearance to determine whether a solute undergoes net tubular reabsorption, net tubular secretion, or is handled strictly as a glomerular marker.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man is evaluated as a potential living kidney donor. He is healthy, eats a high-salt diet, and takes no medications. Blood pressure is 120/76 mm Hg. His GFR measured by inulin clearance is 150 mL/min. His plasma sodium is 140 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), his urine sodium is 200 mEq/L (reference range: varies with intake), and his urine flow rate is 1.0 mL/min. Which of the following best describes his Na+ clearance ratio?","choices":{"A":"About 1.4, indicating that Na+ is secreted by the tubule","B":"About 0.01, indicating that 99% of filtered Na+ is reabsorbed","C":"About 0.5, indicating that half of filtered Na+ is reabsorbed","D":"About 1.0, indicating that Na+ is handled as a pure glomerular marker","E":"About 0.1, indicating that 90% of filtered Na+ is reabsorbed"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Na+ clearance is (200 mEq/L × 1.0 mL/min) / 140 mEq/L ≈ 1.43 mL/min; dividing by the inulin clearance of 150 mL/min gives a clearance ratio of about 0.01, meaning only 1% of the filtered Na+ load is excreted and 99% must have been reabsorbed by the tubule.","boardPrep":"A clearance ratio near 0.01 for a heavily reabsorbed solute like Na+ translates directly to \"about 99% reabsorbed\" — always subtract the excreted fraction from 100% to state the reabsorbed fraction correctly.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — this same 1% filtered-Na+-excretion figure is the quantitative basis for fractional excretion of sodium (FENa) used clinically to assess volume status","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the clearance ratio of a substance relative to inulin clearance to determine whether a solute undergoes net tubular reabsorption, net tubular secretion, or is handled strictly as a glomerular marker.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man is enrolled in a phase 1 study of a new antibiotic. He is healthy and takes no other medications. Blood pressure is 124/80 mm Hg, his hematocrit is 45% (reference range: 41–53%), and his serum creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). On the same day, the renal clearance of the antibiotic is found to be approximately equal to that of para-aminohippuric acid (PAH). What can be concluded about this drug's renal handling?","choices":{"A":"It is filtered and secreted with near-complete extraction from plasma","B":"It is freely filtered with no significant tubular reabsorption or secretion","C":"It is filtered and reabsorbed to a similar degree as glucose","D":"It is metabolized within the renal tubule before excretion","E":"It bypasses filtration and is secreted directly by the collecting duct"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PAH is filtered and secreted so efficiently that it is nearly completely extracted from plasma in a single pass, so a drug whose clearance approaches that of PAH must also be filtered and secreted with near-complete extraction, and its clearance approximates renal plasma flow. A clearance equal to inulin's would indicate filtration without net tubular handling, reabsorption would lower clearance, and tubular metabolism or collecting duct secretion cannot produce a clearance near renal plasma flow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PAH is a near-maximal secretion benchmark whose clearance approximates renal plasma flow; a drug with a similar clearance is handled by both filtration and highly efficient secretion. COMLEX: Reinforces why PAH, not inulin, is used to estimate renal plasma flow.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — PAH's near-complete single-pass extraction is exactly the property that makes its clearance a surrogate measure of renal plasma flow","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the clearance ratio of a substance relative to inulin clearance to determine whether a solute undergoes net tubular reabsorption, net tubular secretion, or is handled strictly as a glomerular marker.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man is enrolled in a dose-escalation study of an investigational solute. He is healthy, with a blood pressure of 118/72 mm Hg and a serum creatinine of 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL); his GFR remains constant throughout the study. As the infusion rate and his plasma concentration of the solute rise, its clearance ratio increases from 0.4 to 0.7, remaining well below 1.0 throughout. What does this pattern most likely indicate?","choices":{"A":"The solute switches from reabsorption to secretion as plasma concentration rises","B":"The solute stops being filtered once its plasma concentration rises past a threshold","C":"The solute undergoes reabsorption by a saturable process, less complete as concentration rises","D":"The solute's clearance ratio reflects measurement error, since clearance ratios stay constant","E":"The solute becomes a pure glomerular marker once its plasma concentration is high"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A clearance ratio that stays below 1.0 but rises toward 1.0 as plasma concentration increases is consistent with a saturable tubular reabsorption transporter that reabsorbs a smaller fraction of the filtered load as that load grows, similar in principle to the glucose transport system.","boardPrep":"A clearance ratio moving toward 1.0 with rising plasma concentration, while staying below 1.0, points to a saturable reabsorptive transporter, not a switch to secretion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — this same saturable-transporter logic explains the glucose titration curve and the transport maximum concept covered alongside GFR marker physiology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the physiological properties of an ideal exogenous glomerular marker (such as inulin) with clinical endogenous markers (such as creatinine and blood urea nitrogen), evaluating the clinical convenience and the specific physiological limitations of using creatinine clearance to estimate GFR.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes mellitus comes to the clinic for evaluation of her kidney function. Blood pressure is 134/82 mm Hg. Her serum creatinine is 0.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and urinalysis shows no protein. Her physician orders a 24-hour urine collection to measure creatinine clearance as an estimate of her GFR, rather than an inulin clearance study. How will her measured creatinine clearance compare with her true GFR?","choices":{"A":"Creatinine clearance will slightly underestimate the true GFR value","B":"Creatinine clearance will slightly overestimate the true GFR value","C":"Creatinine clearance will grossly overestimate the true GFR value","D":"Creatinine clearance will exactly equal the true GFR value","E":"Creatinine clearance carries too much tubular secretion error to serve as a GFR estimate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because creatinine is filtered and also secreted to a small extent, creatinine clearance slightly overestimates true GFR; this small, predictable error is accepted in clinical practice because creatinine does not require infusion, unlike inulin.","boardPrep":"Creatinine clearance overestimates GFR by a small, well-characterized margin — \"slightly\" and \"overestimate\" are the two words to anchor on when this concept is tested.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — this same creatinine-based estimate is what clinicians rely on when interpreting the BUN/creatinine ratio in prerenal versus intrinsic renal azotemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the physiological properties of an ideal exogenous glomerular marker (such as inulin) with clinical endogenous markers (such as creatinine and blood urea nitrogen), evaluating the clinical convenience and the specific physiological limitations of using creatinine clearance to estimate GFR.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman volunteers for a study comparing a new exogenous filtration marker with inulin. She is healthy, with a blood pressure of 110/70 mm Hg and a serum creatinine of 0.7 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). The new marker is freely filtered at the glomerulus but, in contrast to inulin, is partially reabsorbed by the proximal tubule. How would her clearance of this marker compare with her true GFR?","choices":{"A":"Its clearance would overestimate the true GFR value","B":"Its clearance would exactly equal the true GFR value","C":"Its clearance would be zero because reabsorption abolishes excretion","D":"Its clearance would underestimate the true GFR value","E":"Its clearance would be unpredictable and difficult to interpret"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A marker that is filtered and then partially reabsorbed will have a clearance lower than the true GFR, since some of the filtered load never reaches the urine; this makes it a less accurate glomerular marker than inulin, which is filtered and neither reabsorbed nor secreted.","boardPrep":"Any reabsorption after filtration pulls a marker's clearance below true GFR — inulin remains the gold standard specifically because it lacks this reabsorptive error.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — this same reabsorption-causes-underestimation logic is why substances like Na+ and glucose have clearance ratios well below 1.0 in Objective 2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the physiological properties of an ideal exogenous glomerular marker (such as inulin) with clinical endogenous markers (such as creatinine and blood urea nitrogen), evaluating the clinical convenience and the specific physiological limitations of using creatinine clearance to estimate GFR.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with metastatic pancreatic cancer comes to the clinic for follow-up. Over the past 6 months he has lost 14 kg (31 lb), mostly from visible muscle wasting. Blood pressure is 118/72 mm Hg. Serum albumin is 3.0 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). His GFR measured by a radionuclide method is unchanged from 6 months ago. Assuming creatinine production falls in proportion to muscle mass, what is the expected effect on his serum creatinine and creatinine clearance?","choices":{"A":"Serum creatinine falls while creatinine clearance stays a similarly accurate GFR estimate","B":"Serum creatinine rises while less creatinine becomes available for filtration","C":"Serum creatinine and creatinine clearance both stay unchanged since GFR is stable","D":"Serum creatinine falls while creatinine clearance underestimates GFR because tubular secretion of creatinine drops","E":"Serum creatinine rises while creatinine clearance falls in direct proportion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because creatinine is produced by muscle at a roughly constant rate proportional to muscle mass, a substantial decline in muscle mass reduces creatinine production and lowers serum creatinine even though GFR itself is unchanged. Creatinine clearance, calculated from serum and urine creatinine, still tracks the unchanged GFR because urinary creatinine excretion falls in step with the serum value, and the secretory contribution is a small, stable fraction of excretion rather than something that changes with muscle mass. The clinically important change is therefore the low absolute serum creatinine, which can mask a real decline in renal function.","boardPrep":"Muscle mass, not just GFR, determines the absolute serum creatinine value — a low-muscle-mass patient can have a deceptively low or \"normal-looking\" creatinine even when GFR is only borderline, which can mask a real decline in renal function on future labs; this is a classic clinical pitfall in cirrhosis, malnutrition, and chronic wasting illness.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — this muscle-mass dependence is exactly why creatinine, unlike inulin, is not a universally reliable marker across all patient populations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Utilize the plasma blood urea nitrogen to creatinine ratio to clinically differentiate prerenal azotemia (ratio greater than 20 to 1) from intrinsic renal parenchymal disease (ratio less than 15 to 1), tracing these distinct biochemical profiles to proximal tubular urea reabsorption driven by volume contraction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department after 4 days of diarrhea and poor oral intake. Pulse is 108/min, and blood pressure is 98/60 mm Hg with a postural drop. Her mucous membranes are dry. Blood urea nitrogen is 50 mg/dL (reference range: 7–18 mg/dL), and serum creatinine is 1.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), giving a BUN/creatinine ratio near 28:1. Two months ago, her creatinine was within the reference range. What is the most likely underlying mechanism of her laboratory findings?","choices":{"A":"Intrinsic glomerular damage is filtering urea and creatinine at different rates","B":"A urinary tract obstruction is preventing both urea and creatinine excretion equally","C":"Increased dietary protein intake is independently raising both BUN and creatinine","D":"A defect in the countercurrent multiplication system is concentrating both solutes equally","E":"Decreased renal perfusion is driving disproportionate proximal urea reabsorption"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The clinical history of poor intake and diarrhea points to volume contraction, and a BUN/creatinine ratio above 20:1 reflects decreased renal perfusion driving increased proximal tubular reabsorption of urea disproportionately more than creatinine, the classic mechanism of prerenal azotemia. Countercurrent multiplication concentrates the urine in the medulla; it does not raise plasma urea out of proportion to creatinine, and a defect would impair concentration rather than cause this ratio.","boardPrep":"Whenever a vignette pairs a volume-contraction history (vomiting, diarrhea, poor intake, hemorrhage) with a BUN/creatinine ratio above 20:1, the mechanism is prerenal, not intrinsic renal.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — this same volume-contraction mechanism is the clinical endpoint of the isosmotic, hyperosmotic and hyposmotic contraction states studied quantitatively in SDL 6","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Utilize the plasma blood urea nitrogen to creatinine ratio to clinically differentiate prerenal azotemia (ratio greater than 20 to 1) from intrinsic renal parenchymal disease (ratio less than 15 to 1), tracing these distinct biochemical profiles to proximal tubular urea reabsorption driven by volume contraction.","batch":2,"stem":"Two patients with diabetic kidney disease are seen in the nephrology clinic on the same day; neither has gastrointestinal bleeding, is receiving corticosteroids, or has a change in protein intake. Both have a serum creatinine of 3.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Patient A, a 66-year-old man, has a blood urea nitrogen of 65 mg/dL (reference range: 7–18 mg/dL), a ratio of about 22:1. Patient B, a 64-year-old woman, has a blood urea nitrogen of 33 mg/dL (reference range: 7–18 mg/dL), a ratio of about 11:1. Which mechanism best distinguishes the two?","choices":{"A":"Patient A has superimposed volume contraction; Patient B has isolated intrinsic renal disease","B":"Patient A has isolated intrinsic renal disease; Patient B has superimposed volume contraction","C":"Both patients have identical underlying mechanisms since their creatinine values match","D":"Patient A has postrenal obstruction; Patient B has classic prerenal azotemia","E":"The ratio difference reflects measurement variability between the two labs"},"correct":"A","explanation":"With identical elevated creatinine values reflecting a similar reduction in GFR, Patient A's elevated BUN/creatinine ratio above 20:1 suggests an additional volume-contraction component driving disproportionate urea reabsorption, whereas Patient B's ratio near 11:1 is consistent with intrinsic renal disease raising both values together without that disproportionate effect.","boardPrep":"When creatinine is equal between two patients, the BUN/creatinine ratio is what reveals whether volume contraction is also present — a key exam technique for layered renal pathology.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — this side-by-side ratio comparison is the clinical application of the same reabsorption-versus-filtration distinction established in Objective 2's clearance ratios","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Utilize the plasma blood urea nitrogen to creatinine ratio to clinically differentiate prerenal azotemia (ratio greater than 20 to 1) from intrinsic renal parenchymal disease (ratio less than 15 to 1), tracing these distinct biochemical profiles to proximal tubular urea reabsorption driven by volume contraction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with long-standing chronic kidney disease from hypertension is seen in the nephrology clinic. He feels tired but has been eating and drinking normally. Blood pressure is 148/90 mm Hg, pulse is 78/min, and he has trace ankle edema. Blood urea nitrogen is 80 mg/dL (reference range: 7–18 mg/dL), and serum creatinine is 6.5 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), giving a ratio near 12:1. His daughter asks whether aggressive intravenous fluids would be expected to normalize these values. What is the most accurate response?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because the elevated values reflect volume contraction that fluids would reverse","B":"Yes, because fluids would restore proximal urea reabsorption and lower the ratio","C":"No, because intrinsic renal dysfunction explains this ratio and fluids would not correct it","D":"No, because the ratio points to a urinary obstruction that should be relieved by drainage","E":"No, because fluids would paradoxically raise both BUN and creatinine further"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A BUN/creatinine ratio near 12:1, within the normal-to-low range despite both values being markedly elevated, indicates intrinsic renal parenchymal disease rather than volume contraction, so intravenous fluids would not correct the underlying reduced GFR from chronic kidney disease.","boardPrep":"A low-normal BUN/creatinine ratio with elevated absolute values argues against a volume-contraction component — fluids treat prerenal azotemia, not intrinsic renal disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — this same ratio-based reasoning is the clinical decision point separating fluid resuscitation from renal-replacement-focused management in the nephrology block ahead","isHighYield":true}],"8":[{"id":"sdl08_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze how extrinsic vasoactive mediators and hormones adjust afferent and efferent renal arteriolar resistances to regulate renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate, utilizing the opposing vasomotor actions of Atrial Natriuretic Peptide (ANP) as a model.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the net effect of atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) on afferent arteriolar tone, efferent arteriolar tone, and overall renal blood flow?","choices":{"A":"ANP constricts the afferent arteriole, dilates the efferent arteriole, and decreases renal blood flow overall","B":"ANP dilates both the afferent and efferent arterioles equally, leaving overall renal blood flow essentially unchanged","C":"ANP constricts both the afferent and efferent arterioles, decreasing overall renal blood flow substantially","D":"ANP dilates the afferent arteriole, constricts the efferent arteriole, and increases renal blood flow overall","E":"ANP dilates the efferent arteriole modestly, leaving afferent arteriolar tone essentially unaffected"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ANP causes afferent arteriolar dilation and efferent arteriolar constriction; because the dilatory effect on the afferent vessel outweighs the constrictor effect on the efferent vessel, overall renal vascular resistance falls and renal blood flow rises.","boardPrep":"ANP = afferent dilation + efferent constriction, net resistance DOWN, RBF UP — the opposite arteriolar pairing from angiotensin II.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — this ANP-driven resistance pattern is why volume-expanded states can raise GFR even as renal vascular resistance falls","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze how extrinsic vasoactive mediators and hormones adjust afferent and efferent renal arteriolar resistances to regulate renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate, utilizing the opposing vasomotor actions of Atrial Natriuretic Peptide (ANP) as a model.","batch":1,"stem":"Which extrinsic mediator constricts both the afferent and efferent arterioles, but has a preferential constrictor effect on the efferent arteriole at low circulating levels?","choices":{"A":"Atrial natriuretic peptide","B":"Prostaglandin E2","C":"Nitric oxide","D":"Bradykinin","E":"Angiotensin II"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Angiotensin II constricts both arterioles, but the efferent arteriole is more sensitive to it; at low levels this preferential efferent constriction raises glomerular capillary pressure and protects GFR even as renal blood flow falls. Atrial natriuretic peptide dilates the afferent arteriole while only mildly constricting the efferent, and prostaglandin E2, nitric oxide and bradykinin are renal vasodilators.","boardPrep":"Low-dose angiotensin II = efferent-predominant constriction = GFR protected, RBF down; this is the basis for ACE-inhibitor/ARB effects on GFR in renal artery stenosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> future block — this dose-dependent efferent selectivity is the physiologic basis for ACE-inhibitor-induced acute kidney injury in bilateral renal artery stenosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze how extrinsic vasoactive mediators and hormones adjust afferent and efferent renal arteriolar resistances to regulate renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate, utilizing the opposing vasomotor actions of Atrial Natriuretic Peptide (ANP) as a model.","batch":1,"stem":"A low dose of dopamine is administered to a hemorrhaging patient specifically for its effect on which vascular beds?","choices":{"A":"Renal, cerebral, cardiac, and splanchnic arterioles are dilated","B":"Skeletal muscle and cutaneous arterioles are dilated","C":"Systemic arterioles throughout the body are constricted uniformly","D":"Renal arterioles are the single vascular bed affected, while other vascular beds remain unchanged","E":"Renal arterioles are constricted while cerebral arterioles are dilated"},"correct":"A","explanation":"At low levels, dopamine selectively dilates renal, cerebral, cardiac, and splanchnic arterioles while constricting skeletal muscle and cutaneous arterioles, which is why low-dose dopamine has historically been used to protect flow to critical organs during hemorrhage.","boardPrep":"Low-dose dopamine = selective dilation of renal/cerebral/cardiac/splanchnic beds — protects the organs that matter most during shock.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — dopamine's selective renal vasodilation is a classic (though now largely abandoned in practice) example of extrinsic hemodynamic manipulation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) on the afferent arteriole, deconstructing the pathophysiological mechanisms by which nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) block this safety buffer to precipitate acute kidney injury in hypoperfused states.","batch":1,"stem":"Locally produced renal prostaglandins such as PGE2 and PGI2 primarily protect renal blood flow during states of high sympathetic and angiotensin II activity by what mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Directly inhibiting angiotensin-converting enzyme activity in endothelial cells","B":"Suppressing renin release directly from the juxtaglomerular apparatus cells","C":"Increasing tubular sodium reabsorption to expand circulating plasma volume levels","D":"Vasodilating the afferent arteriole to counteract afferent vasoconstriction","E":"Stimulating aldosterone secretion directly from the adrenal cortex gland"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The same stimuli that activate the sympathetic nervous system and raise angiotensin II during hemorrhage constrict the afferent arteriole and also trigger local renal prostaglandin production; these prostaglandins vasodilate the afferent arteriole, attenuating that constriction and protecting glomerular blood flow from a profound, potentially injurious reduction. Prostaglandins do not suppress renin, since macula densa prostaglandins help signal juxtaglomerular renin release, and they reduce rather than increase sodium reabsorption by inhibiting Na+-K+-2Cl- transport in the thick ascending limb.","boardPrep":"Prostaglandins are the afferent-arteriole counterweight to sympathetic/angiotensin II vasoconstriction — remove that counterweight (NSAIDs) and unopposed afferent constriction can precipitate renal failure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — this counterbalancing mechanism is exactly what NSAIDs disable, setting up the classic hypoperfusion-plus-NSAID acute kidney injury scenario","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) on the afferent arteriole, deconstructing the pathophysiological mechanisms by which nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) block this safety buffer to precipitate acute kidney injury in hypoperfused states.","batch":1,"stem":"A hypovolemic patient with high circulating angiotensin II and sympathetic tone is given an NSAID. What is the expected renal consequence?","choices":{"A":"Renal blood flow rises because prostaglandin-mediated vasoconstriction is removed","B":"Renal blood flow is unaffected because prostaglandins play no meaningful role during hypovolemia","C":"Renal blood flow falls further, risking acute kidney injury","D":"Renal blood flow falls in the efferent arteriole while the afferent arteriole stays unaffected","E":"Renal blood flow briefly falls, then rebounds above baseline"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In a hypovolemic patient, angiotensin II and sympathetic tone are already producing substantial renal vasoconstriction that is normally buffered by local prostaglandin-mediated vasodilation; NSAIDs block prostaglandin synthesis, removing this protective buffer and leaving the vasoconstriction unopposed, which can drop renal blood flow enough to precipitate acute kidney injury.","boardPrep":"NSAID + volume depletion (or heart failure, cirrhosis, ACE-inhibitor/ARB use) = classic prerenal AKI setup — always ask about NSAID use in a volume-depleted patient with rising creatinine.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — this exact clinical scenario is the highest-yield teaching point in this SDL","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) on the afferent arteriole, deconstructing the pathophysiological mechanisms by which nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) block this safety buffer to precipitate acute kidney injury in hypoperfused states.","batch":1,"stem":"By what mechanism do NSAIDs interfere with the renal protective response to hemorrhage or volume depletion?","choices":{"A":"They directly block angiotensin II receptors, preventing arteriolar constriction","B":"They stimulate excess renin release, amplifying the renin-angiotensin system","C":"They increase sympathetic outflow, worsening arteriolar vasoconstriction","D":"They inhibit prostaglandin synthesis, removing a vasodilatory buffer","E":"They inhibit aldosterone-mediated sodium reabsorption, reducing extracellular volume"},"correct":"D","explanation":"NSAIDs inhibit cyclooxygenase and therefore prostaglandin synthesis; because renal prostaglandins are the local vasodilatory buffer that normally offsets sympathetic- and angiotensin II-driven vasoconstriction during hemorrhage or volume depletion, their loss allows unopposed vasoconstriction to reduce renal blood flow.","boardPrep":"NSAID mechanism of renal harm = COX inhibition -> lost prostaglandin vasodilation -> unopposed vasoconstriction, not a direct arteriolar receptor effect.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — this is the pharmacologic mechanism underlying the AKI risk tested throughout this objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the twin intrinsic autoregulatory mechanisms that maintain constant renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate, contrasting the physical stretch-activated calcium biophysics of the myogenic hypothesis in afferent smooth muscle with the chemosensitive, solute-tracking tubuloglomerular feedback (TGF) mechanism at the macula densa (including its pharmacologic blockade by loop diuretics).","batch":1,"stem":"The myogenic hypothesis of renal autoregulation explains constant renal blood flow across a range of arterial pressures by what cellular mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Increased arterial pressure directly inhibits renin release, which substantially lowers renal vascular resistance","B":"Pressure-induced stretch opens calcium channels in afferent arteriolar smooth muscle, causing reflex contraction","C":"Stretch of the efferent arteriole triggers macula densa cells to release adenosine locally","D":"Baroreceptors located in the renal artery wall signal the medulla to adjust sympathetic outflow","E":"Increased arterial pressure reduces intracellular calcium concentration in vascular smooth muscle cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The myogenic hypothesis holds that increased arterial pressure stretches the afferent arteriolar wall, opening stretch-activated calcium channels in vascular smooth muscle; the resulting calcium influx produces reflex contraction, raising afferent arteriolar resistance to balance the pressure increase and keep renal blood flow constant.","boardPrep":"Myogenic mechanism = stretch -> calcium channels open -> smooth muscle contracts -> resistance rises to match the pressure rise, all within the afferent arteriole itself.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — this stretch-activated calcium mechanism is a general vascular smooth muscle principle, not unique to the kidney","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the twin intrinsic autoregulatory mechanisms that maintain constant renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate, contrasting the physical stretch-activated calcium biophysics of the myogenic hypothesis in afferent smooth muscle with the chemosensitive, solute-tracking tubuloglomerular feedback (TGF) mechanism at the macula densa (including its pharmacologic blockade by loop diuretics).","batch":1,"stem":"In the tubuloglomerular feedback mechanism, what ionic event at the macula densa directly precedes adenosine release?","choices":{"A":"Increased intracellular calcium following Na+-2Cl-K+ cotransporter-mediated depolarization","B":"Decreased intracellular chloride concentration following reduced Na+-2Cl-K+ cotransporter activity","C":"Direct calcium influx through stretch-activated channels in macula densa cell membranes","D":"Hyperpolarization of the basolateral membrane caused by potassium efflux through open channels","E":"Direct binding of filtered angiotensin II to receptors on macula densa cell membranes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Increased GFR raises Na+ and Cl− delivery to the macula densa, which are taken up via the Na+-2Cl-K+ cotransporter; the resulting rise in intracellular chloride depolarizes the basolateral membrane, opening calcium channels and raising intracellular calcium, which then triggers adenosine release and paracrine afferent arteriolar constriction.","boardPrep":"TGF sequence: increased delivered Na+/Cl− -> cotransporter uptake -> depolarization -> calcium channels open -> adenosine release -> afferent constriction -> GFR falls back to normal.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — this exact signaling cascade is the mechanistic basis for SGLT2 inhibitors lowering GFR in diabetic hyperfiltration","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the twin intrinsic autoregulatory mechanisms that maintain constant renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate, contrasting the physical stretch-activated calcium biophysics of the myogenic hypothesis in afferent smooth muscle with the chemosensitive, solute-tracking tubuloglomerular feedback (TGF) mechanism at the macula densa (including its pharmacologic blockade by loop diuretics).","batch":1,"stem":"Loop diuretics blunt tubuloglomerular feedback primarily by inhibiting which transporter at the macula densa?","choices":{"A":"The Na+-glucose cotransporter protein","B":"The Na+/H+ exchanger protein","C":"The epithelial Na+ channel protein","D":"The Na+/K+-ATPase pump protein","E":"The Na+-2Cl-K+ cotransporter"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The macula densa senses tubular Na+ and Cl− delivery through the same Na+-2Cl-K+ cotransporter targeted by loop diuretics; blocking this transporter prevents the macula densa from sensing increased delivered load, blunting the afferent arteriolar constriction that would otherwise occur. The Na+/H+ exchanger works mainly in the proximal tubule, the epithelial Na+ channel sits in principal cells of the late distal tubule and collecting duct, and Na+/K+-ATPase is a basolateral pump rather than the luminal sensor that loop diuretics block.","boardPrep":"Loop diuretics blunt TGF by blocking the macula densa's own Na+-2Cl-K+ cotransporter sensor — the same target responsible for their diuretic action in the thick ascending limb.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — this pharmacologic blockade of TGF sensing is a favorite integration point linking diuretic pharmacology to renal hemodynamics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the principles of renal clearance and hematocrit scaling to calculate effective renal plasma flow and total renal blood flow from clinical para-aminohippuric acid (PAH) extraction data.","batch":1,"stem":"Which property of para-aminohippuric acid (PAH) makes it suitable for measuring effective renal plasma flow by the clearance method?","choices":{"A":"It is freely filtered by the glomerulus but neither secreted nor reabsorbed","B":"It is almost completely extracted from renal arterial blood by filtration plus secretion","C":"It is reabsorbed by the proximal tubule in direct proportion to plasma concentration","D":"It is metabolized extensively by renal tubular cells before urinary excretion","E":"It is bound almost entirely to plasma proteins and therefore poorly filtered"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PAH is neither synthesized nor metabolized by the kidney and is removed from renal arterial blood almost completely by the combined processes of glomerular filtration and proximal tubular secretion, leaving renal vein PAH concentration nearly zero; this near-complete extraction is what allows its clearance to approximate renal plasma flow.","boardPrep":"PAH clearance ≈ effective RPF only because PAH is almost completely extracted in one pass — a substance like glucose, with zero net extraction, cannot be used this way.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — this near-complete single-pass extraction is the same property that makes PAH clearance a measure of renal plasma flow rather than GFR","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the principles of renal clearance and hematocrit scaling to calculate effective renal plasma flow and total renal blood flow from clinical para-aminohippuric acid (PAH) extraction data.","batch":1,"stem":"Effective renal plasma flow, calculated from PAH clearance using only a peripheral venous plasma sample and a urine sample, underestimates true renal plasma flow by approximately how much?","choices":{"A":"10%","B":"1%","C":"25%","D":"50%","E":"75%"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Effective RPF assumes renal vein PAH concentration is zero, but a small fraction of renal plasma flow perfuses tissue not involved in filtration or secretion of PAH, such as renal fat and the renal capsule, so renal vein PAH is nearly, but not exactly, zero; this makes effective RPF underestimate true RPF by roughly 10%.","boardPrep":"Effective RPF (from PAH clearance) ≈ true RPF, off by about 10%, because a small non-extracting fraction of renal blood flow never contacts filtering/secreting tissue.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — remembering this 10% figure distinguishes \"effective\" from \"true\" RPF on exam questions that test this exact nuance","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the principles of renal clearance and hematocrit scaling to calculate effective renal plasma flow and total renal blood flow from clinical para-aminohippuric acid (PAH) extraction data.","batch":1,"stem":"Once effective renal plasma flow is calculated from PAH clearance, what additional value is needed to calculate renal blood flow?","choices":{"A":"The plasma protein concentration","B":"The glomerular filtration rate","C":"The patient's hematocrit","D":"The renal filtration fraction","E":"The mean arterial pressure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Renal blood flow equals renal plasma flow divided by the quantity one minus the hematocrit, since hematocrit represents the fraction of blood volume occupied by red blood cells and 1 minus hematocrit represents the plasma fraction that PAH clearance actually measures. Neither the glomerular filtration rate nor the mean arterial pressure gives the plasma fraction of blood, so neither converts plasma flow into blood flow.","boardPrep":"RBF = RPF / (1 − Hct) — always convert plasma flow to blood flow by dividing out the red-cell fraction, never by multiplying.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — this hematocrit-scaling step is the final arithmetic bridge between the PAH-based plasma flow measurement and true whole-blood renal blood flow","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze how extrinsic vasoactive mediators and hormones adjust afferent and efferent renal arteriolar resistances to regulate renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate, utilizing the opposing vasomotor actions of Atrial Natriuretic Peptide (ANP) as a model.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with chronic heart failure is admitted with worsening dyspnea and leg swelling. Blood pressure is 86/58 mm Hg, and pulse is 112/min. He has jugular venous distention, bibasilar crackles, and cool extremities. Serum sodium is 127 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and creatinine has risen from 1.2 to 2.4 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL) over 3 days. His circulating angiotensin II concentration is now markedly elevated, far above the mild elevation found during his compensated outpatient visits. Given that the two glomerular resistance vessels differ in their sensitivity to angiotensin II, how does his glomerular filtration rate at this level compare with that at a mild elevation?","choices":{"A":"GFR falls, because a very high concentration of angiotensin II constricts both afferent and efferent arterioles substantially","B":"GFR rises further, because efferent arteriolar constriction continues to dominate the resistance response","C":"GFR stays unchanged, because arteriolar sensitivity to angiotensin II does not vary with its concentration","D":"GFR falls because the afferent arteriole stops responding while the efferent arteriole stays reactive","E":"GFR rises transiently after the initial change, then returns to baseline as renal autoregulation compensates"},"correct":"A","explanation":"At low angiotensin II levels, the efferent arteriole's greater sensitivity means preferential efferent constriction raises glomerular capillary pressure and protects or increases GFR; at high levels, both afferent and efferent arterioles are substantially constricted, and the resulting large rise in overall resistance drops GFR along with renal blood flow.","boardPrep":"Angiotensin II and GFR: low level protects/raises GFR (efferent-predominant), high level drops GFR (both arterioles constrict) — dose determines the direction of the GFR effect.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> future block — this dose-dependent switch explains why severe heart failure or hemorrhage can still cause a falling GFR despite angiotensin II normally being GFR-protective at lower levels","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze how extrinsic vasoactive mediators and hormones adjust afferent and efferent renal arteriolar resistances to regulate renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate, utilizing the opposing vasomotor actions of Atrial Natriuretic Peptide (ANP) as a model.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with newly diagnosed hypertension comes to the clinic to start treatment. She has no other medical conditions. Blood pressure is 156/96 mm Hg, and pulse is 74/min. Serum creatinine is 0.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and potassium is 4.2 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). She begins a drug that inhibits conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II. Based on the arteriolar effects of angiotensin II, what is the expected acute change in her renal plasma flow and glomerular filtration rate?","choices":{"A":"Renal plasma flow falls sharply while glomerular filtration rate stays at its baseline","B":"Renal plasma flow rises while glomerular filtration rate falls","C":"Both renal plasma flow and glomerular filtration rate rise together","D":"Both values decline together whenever systemic blood pressure falls low enough to reduce overall renal perfusion","E":"Renal plasma flow stays essentially unchanged while glomerular filtration rate falls sharply"},"correct":"B","explanation":"ACE inhibitors block angiotensin II production, removing its preferential efferent arteriolar constriction; efferent arteriolar dilation lowers overall renal vascular resistance and raises renal plasma flow, but it also lowers glomerular capillary pressure, which drops the glomerular filtration rate.","boardPrep":"ACE inhibitors/ARBs: efferent dilation -> RPF up, GFR down — this is why creatinine can rise modestly (an expected, tolerable change) after starting these drugs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> future block — a large or progressive creatinine rise after starting an ACE inhibitor should prompt evaluation for bilateral renal artery stenosis rather than reassurance","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze how extrinsic vasoactive mediators and hormones adjust afferent and efferent renal arteriolar resistances to regulate renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate, utilizing the opposing vasomotor actions of Atrial Natriuretic Peptide (ANP) as a model.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old man is brought to the emergency department 30 minutes after a stab wound to the thigh with brisk bleeding. He is anxious and diaphoretic. Pulse is 124/min, and blood pressure is 98/64 mm Hg. Serum creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). His urine output over the next hour is 15 mL. His elevated sympathetic tone is activating renal alpha-1 adrenergic receptors. Which mechanism explains why his glomerular filtration rate falls together with his renal blood flow?","choices":{"A":"Alpha-1 receptor density is greater on the efferent arteriole, so sympathetic activation preferentially raises efferent resistance","B":"Sympathetic activation dilates the afferent arteriole to preserve glomerular capillary pressure during hypovolemia","C":"Sympathetic nerves constrict the renal vein and raise glomerular capillary pressure through venous back-pressure","D":"Alpha-1 receptor density is greater on the afferent arteriole, so sympathetic activation preferentially raises afferent resistance","E":"Sympathetic nerves act directly on the glomerular capillary wall itself, reducing its overall filtering surface area"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because afferent arterioles carry more alpha-1 receptors than efferent arterioles, sympathetic activation during hemorrhage preferentially constricts the afferent vessel; this reduces glomerular capillary pressure along with renal blood flow, so both renal blood flow and GFR fall together, in contrast to the efferent-predominant pattern seen with low-level angiotensin II that protects GFR.","boardPrep":"Sympathetic activation = afferent-predominant constriction = both RBF and GFR fall together, unlike angiotensin II's efferent-predominant, GFR-protective pattern at low levels.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — contrasting the afferent-predominant sympathetic pattern with the efferent-predominant low-level angiotensin II pattern is a classic board distinction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) on the afferent arteriole, deconstructing the pathophysiological mechanisms by which nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) block this safety buffer to precipitate acute kidney injury in hypoperfused states.","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old woman with chronic heart failure is seen in the clinic because her serum creatinine has risen from 1.0 to 2.1 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL) over 2 weeks. Three weeks ago, she began taking ibuprofen daily for osteoarthritis of the knees. Pulse is 92/min, and arterial pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. She has 2+ pitting edema of the ankles. Serum urea nitrogen is 46 mg/dL (reference range: 7–18 mg/dL), and urinalysis shows no protein, hematuria, or casts. What is the most likely mechanism of her rising creatinine?","choices":{"A":"Ibuprofen blocks protective prostaglandin vasodilation, allowing unopposed vasoconstriction to reduce renal blood flow","B":"Ibuprofen directly damages the glomerular basement membrane, causing significant protein loss and severely reduced filtration","C":"Ibuprofen stimulates excess renin release, raising circulating angiotensin II above its usual protective range","D":"Ibuprofen blocks tubular creatinine secretion directly, raising serum creatinine without any change in true GFR","E":"Ibuprofen causes afferent arteriolar dilation that raises glomerular capillary pressure excessively high"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In a patient with reduced effective circulating volume, such as one with heart failure, renal perfusion depends heavily on prostaglandin-mediated vasodilation to counteract already-elevated angiotensin II and sympathetic tone; an NSAID like ibuprofen inhibits this prostaglandin synthesis, removing the protective buffer and allowing unopposed vasoconstriction to drop renal blood flow and precipitate a prerenal rise in creatinine.","boardPrep":"Heart failure + NSAID + rising creatinine = classic prostaglandin-buffer-loss prerenal AKI, one of the most frequently tested drug-induced kidney injury scenarios.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — heart failure, cirrhosis, and true volume depletion are the three classic settings where the prostaglandin buffer is already maximally recruited before an NSAID is even added","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) on the afferent arteriole, deconstructing the pathophysiological mechanisms by which nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) block this safety buffer to precipitate acute kidney injury in hypoperfused states.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old man comes to the clinic for a routine visit. Yesterday he took a single dose of ibuprofen for a tension headache, which resolved. He has no medical conditions, drinks plenty of fluids, and takes no other medications. Blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg, and pulse is 64/min. Serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and blood urea nitrogen is 12 mg/dL (reference range: 7–18 mg/dL). Why is acute kidney injury unlikely in this patient compared with a volume-depleted patient taking the same drug?","choices":{"A":"Ibuprofen does not inhibit prostaglandin synthesis at low single-dose exposure","B":"Young adults lack the renal prostaglandin receptors needed for this drug interaction","C":"Prostaglandin vasodilation is not heavily recruited when volume status is normal","D":"A single dose is metabolized and cleared before it ever reaches renal tissue","E":"Prostaglandins act on the efferent arteriole when volume status is well hydrated"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In a euvolemic, well-perfused kidney, sympathetic and angiotensin II tone are not elevated, so the prostaglandin vasodilatory buffer is not being heavily called upon to protect renal blood flow; removing that buffer with an NSAID therefore has little hemodynamic consequence, whereas in a volume-depleted or hypoperfused state the buffer is essential and its loss is far more dangerous.","boardPrep":"NSAID nephrotoxicity risk tracks with how much the prostaglandin buffer is already being used — high-risk in volume depletion, heart failure, cirrhosis, or ACE-inhibitor use; low-risk in a euvolemic healthy patient.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — this risk-stratification logic is exactly why NSAID warnings target volume-depleted and chronically ill patients rather than the general population","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the protective vasodilatory role of local renal prostaglandins (PGE2 and PGI2) on the afferent arteriole, deconstructing the pathophysiological mechanisms by which nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) block this safety buffer to precipitate acute kidney injury in hypoperfused states.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with chronic kidney disease from diabetes takes lisinopril. Ten days ago, he began taking naproxen for a gout flare. He now feels fatigued and has noticed less urine output. Blood pressure is 122/76 mm Hg, and pulse is 80/min. Serum creatinine has risen from 1.8 to 3.2 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and potassium is 5.7 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Urinalysis shows no blood or casts. How do the two drugs' mechanisms combine to produce his kidney injury?","choices":{"A":"The ACE inhibitor and NSAID both act on the same vascular receptor type, producing an additive but mechanistically redundant effect","B":"The NSAID reverses the ACE inhibitor effect on angiotensin II production, which restores normal efferent arteriolar tone","C":"The ACE inhibitor affects tubular sodium handling while the NSAID affects glomerular capillary permeability","D":"The combination raises systemic blood pressure high enough to cause acute hypertensive glomerular capillary damage","E":"ACE inhibitor dilation of the efferent arteriole plus NSAID loss of afferent prostaglandins collapses glomerular pressure from both sides"},"correct":"E","explanation":"ACE inhibitors lower glomerular capillary pressure by dilating the efferent arteriole (removing angiotensin II's constrictor effect), while NSAIDs remove the prostaglandin-mediated afferent vasodilation that normally sustains blood flow into the glomerulus; used together, efferent pressure support and afferent blood delivery are lost simultaneously, and glomerular filtration pressure can collapse far more than with either drug alone.","boardPrep":"ACE inhibitor + NSAID = a classic dual hit on glomerular hemodynamics from opposite arterioles — a well-known cause of acute kidney injury, especially with added volume depletion (the \"triple whammy\" with diuretics).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — recognizing this two-drug (or three-drug, with a diuretic) combination is one of the most frequently tested acute kidney injury vignettes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the twin intrinsic autoregulatory mechanisms that maintain constant renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate, contrasting the physical stretch-activated calcium biophysics of the myogenic hypothesis in afferent smooth muscle with the chemosensitive, solute-tracking tubuloglomerular feedback (TGF) mechanism at the macula densa (including its pharmacologic blockade by loop diuretics).","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes mellitus comes to the clinic for follow-up. Blood pressure is 132/80 mm Hg, and BMI is 34 kg/m2. Hemoglobin A1c is 8.4% (reference range: 4.0–5.6%), serum creatinine is 0.6 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and her measured GFR is elevated at 148 mL/min, consistent with early glomerular hyperfiltration. She is started on a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 (SGLT2) inhibitor. Over the next 2 weeks her GFR falls. How does tubuloglomerular feedback mediate the drug's effect on her GFR?","choices":{"A":"The drug directly constricts the efferent arteriole, acting independently of macula densa sensing","B":"Blocking the cotransporter raises macula densa Na+ delivery, triggering afferent constriction that lowers GFR","C":"The drug blocks the Na+-2Cl-K+ cotransporter at the macula densa, the exact same target used by loop diuretics","D":"Blocking the cotransporter increases proximal Na+ reabsorption, decreasing delivery to the macula densa","E":"Blocking the cotransporter lowers GFR through a direct effect on the glomerular capillary wall, bypassing feedback"},"correct":"B","explanation":"SGLT2 inhibitors block Na+-glucose cotransport in the proximal convoluted tubule, increasing downstream Na+ delivery to the macula densa; through the normal tubuloglomerular feedback pathway, this increased delivered load triggers adenosine release and afferent arteriolar constriction, lowering GFR and offsetting the damaging hyperfiltration seen in diabetes mellitus.","boardPrep":"SGLT2 inhibitors lower GFR via intact tubuloglomerular feedback (more Na+ to macula densa -> afferent constriction), not via direct arteriolar action — this is the renoprotective mechanism in diabetic kidney disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — this is one of the highest-yield clinical applications of tubuloglomerular feedback physiology tested on current board exams","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the twin intrinsic autoregulatory mechanisms that maintain constant renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate, contrasting the physical stretch-activated calcium biophysics of the myogenic hypothesis in afferent smooth muscle with the chemosensitive, solute-tracking tubuloglomerular feedback (TGF) mechanism at the macula densa (including its pharmacologic blockade by loop diuretics).","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man comes to the transplant clinic for a routine visit 1 year after a deceased-donor kidney transplant for polycystic kidney disease. He feels well. Blood pressure is 128/80 mm Hg, and serum creatinine is 1.1 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). As part of a research protocol, studies of his allograft show that its renal blood flow stays nearly constant as his mean arterial pressure varies across the normal range, even though the transplanted kidney has no autonomic nerve supply. What does this observation demonstrate about the underlying mechanisms?","choices":{"A":"Renal autoregulation depends on autonomic innervation for its stretch and feedback responses","B":"The transplanted kidney regenerates its sympathetic nerve supply within hours of transplantation","C":"Autoregulation in a transplanted kidney relies on circulating angiotensin II acting on both arterioles","D":"Renal autoregulation does not require intact autonomic innervation to operate normally","E":"Denervation abolishes tubuloglomerular feedback but spares the myogenic mechanism"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because a denervated kidney autoregulates renal blood flow just as well as an intact one, the autonomic nervous system cannot be responsible for autoregulation; instead, the myogenic mechanism (an intrinsic property of vascular smooth muscle) and tubuloglomerular feedback (a local paracrine mechanism at the macula densa) are entirely local, kidney-intrinsic processes that do not require nerve input.","boardPrep":"Autoregulation is intrinsic to the kidney itself — the classic evidence is that transplanted (denervated) kidneys autoregulate normally, ruling out a required autonomic component.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — this denervation evidence is a frequently quoted proof-of-concept for the intrinsic, local nature of both autoregulatory mechanisms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the twin intrinsic autoregulatory mechanisms that maintain constant renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate, contrasting the physical stretch-activated calcium biophysics of the myogenic hypothesis in afferent smooth muscle with the chemosensitive, solute-tracking tubuloglomerular feedback (TGF) mechanism at the macula densa (including its pharmacologic blockade by loop diuretics).","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man who trains for bodybuilding competitions comes to the clinic for a routine visit. He eats several very high-protein meals each day. Blood pressure is 120/76 mm Hg, and pulse is 58/min. Serum creatinine is 1.1 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and blood urea nitrogen is 22 mg/dL (reference range: 7–18 mg/dL). His physician explains how a high-protein meal changes kidney function. Through the tubuloglomerular feedback pathway, what is the expected effect of such a meal on his glomerular filtration rate?","choices":{"A":"GFR falls, because increased amino acid delivery stimulates macula densa adenosine release","B":"GFR is unchanged, because dietary protein does not alter tubuloglomerular feedback sensitivity","C":"GFR rises, because increased proximal Na+ and Cl− reabsorption reduces delivery to the macula densa","D":"GFR falls, because a high-protein meal increases Na+ and Cl− delivery to the macula densa","E":"GFR rises transiently, then falls below baseline as feedback overcorrects"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A high-protein diet increases proximal tubular Na+ and Cl− reabsorption, which reduces the amount of Na+ and Cl− delivered downstream to the macula densa; with less delivered load to sense, tubuloglomerular feedback generates less afferent arteriolar constriction, allowing GFR to rise above its usual set point.","boardPrep":"High-protein diet -> more proximal Na+/Cl− reabsorption -> less delivery to macula densa -> less TGF-mediated constriction -> GFR rises — a classic example of TGF sensitivity being altered by diet.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — this dietary modulation of TGF sensitivity is a clinically relevant nuance distinguishing TGF from a fixed, unmodifiable set point","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the principles of renal clearance and hematocrit scaling to calculate effective renal plasma flow and total renal blood flow from clinical para-aminohippuric acid (PAH) extraction data.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman is evaluated as a potential living kidney donor for her sister. She is healthy and takes no medications. Blood pressure is 114/72 mm Hg, and her serum creatinine is 0.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). During a constant infusion of para-aminohippuric acid (PAH), her urine flow rate is 1 mL/min, her plasma PAH concentration is 1 mg/100 mL, and her urine PAH concentration is 600 mg/100 mL. What is her effective renal plasma flow?","choices":{"A":"600 mL/min","B":"60 mL/min","C":"1000 mL/min","D":"6 mL/min","E":"1200 mL/min"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Effective RPF equals the clearance of PAH, calculated as urine PAH concentration times urine flow rate divided by plasma PAH concentration: (600 mg/100 mL × 1 mL/min) / (1 mg/100 mL) = 600 mL/min. Values of 1000 or 1200 mL/min would need a urine PAH of 1000 or 1200 mg/100 mL at this urine flow, not 600.","boardPrep":"Effective RPF = ([U]PAH × V̇) / [P]PAH — plug in urine concentration times flow rate, divide by plasma concentration, done.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — this is the identical calculation structure used for any clearance calculation, just applied specifically to PAH","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the principles of renal clearance and hematocrit scaling to calculate effective renal plasma flow and total renal blood flow from clinical para-aminohippuric acid (PAH) extraction data.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man is evaluated as a potential living kidney donor for his son. He is healthy and takes no medications. Blood pressure is 122/78 mm Hg. Serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and hematocrit is 45% (reference range: 41–53%). His effective renal plasma flow, measured by para-aminohippuric acid clearance, is 600 mL/min. What is his effective renal blood flow?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 270 mL/min","B":"Approximately 600 mL/min","C":"Approximately 833 mL/min","D":"Approximately 1333 mL/min","E":"Approximately 1091 mL/min"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Renal blood flow equals renal plasma flow divided by one minus the hematocrit: 600 mL/min / (1 − 0.45) = 600 mL/min / 0.55 ≈ 1091 mL/min. About 270 mL/min multiplies plasma flow by the hematocrit, 600 mL/min is the plasma flow left unconverted, about 1333 mL/min divides by the hematocrit instead of the plasma fraction, and about 833 mL/min does not follow from either the hematocrit or the plasma fraction.","boardPrep":"RBF = RPF / (1 − Hct); with Hct 0.45, divide RPF by 0.55 — always larger than RPF itself, since blood includes red cells that plasma flow alone does not measure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — pairing this hematocrit-scaling step with the PAH clearance calculation is the complete two-step quantitative workflow tested in this objective","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl08_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the principles of renal clearance and hematocrit scaling to calculate effective renal plasma flow and total renal blood flow from clinical para-aminohippuric acid (PAH) extraction data.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man is evaluated as a potential living renal donor. He is healthy, with a blood pressure of 118/76 mm Hg and a serum creatinine of 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). During a constant infusion of para-aminohippuric acid (PAH), a timed urine collection and a peripheral venous blood sample are analyzed for PAH; no catheter is placed in the renal vein. Why can his true renal plasma flow not be calculated from these data?","choices":{"A":"True RPF requires a full 24-hour urine collection, and a single spot urine sample cannot substitute for that requirement","B":"True RPF needs concentration values from both the vessel entering the kidney and the vessel draining it afterward","C":"True RPF calculations require a separately measured hematocrit value, and no hematocrit value was reported anywhere in this workup","D":"A peripheral venous blood sample differs enough from the true arterial blood to invalidate any renal clearance calculation","E":"True RPF cannot be calculated for any substance that undergoes tubular secretion in addition to glomerular filtration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Fick principle formula for true RPF is urine PAH excretion divided by the difference between renal artery and renal vein PAH concentrations; because renal vein blood requires invasive catheterization and is not routinely sampled, only the simplified effective RPF (which assumes renal vein PAH is zero and substitutes an easily obtained peripheral venous sample for the renal artery concentration) can actually be calculated in practice.","boardPrep":"True RPF needs a renal vein sample (invasive catheterization); effective RPF is the practical, clinically calculable approximation, and it is what \"PAH clearance\" almost always refers to on exams.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — recognizing which formula (true vs. effective RPF) a question is actually asking for is the key discriminator in this objective's calculation questions","isHighYield":false}],"9":[{"id":"sdl09_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the structural and chemical layers of the glomerular filtration barrier (fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane, and podocyte slit diaphragms), evaluating how size and negative charge selectivity (heparan sulfate glycoproteins) prevent protein filtration and how their disruption leads to selective proteinuria (such as in minimal change disease).","batch":1,"stem":"Which layer of the glomerular capillary wall is considered the most significant barrier to filtration of plasma proteins based on its multilayered structure?","choices":{"A":"The basement membrane","B":"The fenestrated endothelium","C":"The podocyte foot processes","D":"The filtration slit diaphragms","E":"The mesangial matrix"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The basement membrane has three layers (lamina rara interna, lamina densa, and lamina rara externa) and does not permit filtration of plasma proteins, making it the most significant physical barrier of the glomerular capillary wall, even though the endothelium and epithelium also contribute size restriction. The mesangial matrix lies between the capillary loops as support for the mesangial cells and is not one of the layers that filtrate crosses.","boardPrep":"Three layers, one barrier hierarchy: endothelium (large pores, freely permeable) -> basement membrane (the main protein barrier) -> podocyte slit diaphragms (fine size restriction).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — this layered barrier structure is what a renal biopsy electron micrograph is showing when it demonstrates basement membrane thickening or podocyte foot process effacement","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the structural and chemical layers of the glomerular filtration barrier (fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane, and podocyte slit diaphragms), evaluating how size and negative charge selectivity (heparan sulfate glycoproteins) prevent protein filtration and how their disruption leads to selective proteinuria (such as in minimal change disease).","batch":1,"stem":"The negatively charged heparan sulfate glycoproteins lining the glomerular filtration barrier primarily restrict the filtration of which type of solute?","choices":{"A":"Small cations such as Na+ and K+, too small for charge to matter","B":"Small anions such as Cl- and HCO3-, small enough that charge barely slows them","C":"Large anionic plasma proteins such as albumin, restricted by both size and charge","D":"Water molecules, passing through the endothelial fenestrae unimpeded","E":"Neutral solutes such as glucose, filtered without any size or charge restriction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Small solutes such as Na+, K+, Cl-, and glucose are freely filtered regardless of charge because their tiny molecular diameter makes electrostatic repulsion irrelevant, but large solutes such as plasma proteins have a molecular diameter similar to the pore and slit sizes, so their net negative charge at physiologic pH causes electrostatic repulsion by the barrier's fixed negative charges, adding to size-based restriction.","boardPrep":"Charge selectivity only matters for large solutes whose size already puts them near the filtration cutoff — small ions and glucose are filtered freely no matter their charge.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — losing this negative-charge barrier, without a change in the size-based barrier, is exactly the mechanism of minimal change disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the structural and chemical layers of the glomerular filtration barrier (fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane, and podocyte slit diaphragms), evaluating how size and negative charge selectivity (heparan sulfate glycoproteins) prevent protein filtration and how their disruption leads to selective proteinuria (such as in minimal change disease).","batch":1,"stem":"A pediatric patient develops selective proteinuria (loss of albumin without loss of larger plasma proteins) with normal-appearing glomeruli on light microscopy. What barrier defect best explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Complete destruction of the basement membrane's size-restricting layers, with the charge barrier also compromised secondarily","B":"Loss of the barrier's fixed negative charges, with its size-restricting structure remaining intact","C":"Formation of new large pores in the endothelium, with both size and charge selectivity lost","D":"Loss of podocyte foot processes, with the charge-based barrier remaining intact","E":"Deposition of immune complexes that mechanically obstruct the filtration slits, with the charge barrier left intact"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Minimal change disease classically presents with selective proteinuria and normal light microscopy because the defect is a loss of the barrier's fixed negative charges (not visible on light microscopy; electron microscopy shows podocyte foot process effacement), which selectively increases filtration of the negatively charged, moderately sized albumin while the size-based barrier remains largely intact, restricting larger proteins.","boardPrep":"Selective proteinuria (albumin only) with normal light microscopy = minimal change disease = charge-barrier loss, not a structural/size-barrier lesion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — contrast this with nonselective proteinuria (loss of both albumin and larger proteins), which points to a structural size-barrier lesion instead","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply the Starling equation to calculate net ultrafiltration pressure across the glomerular capillaries, explaining why Bowman space oncotic pressure is physiologically considered zero.","batch":1,"stem":"Why is the oncotic pressure of Bowman space treated as zero in the Starling equation for glomerular filtration?","choices":{"A":"Because Bowman space has no fluid present under normal conditions","B":"Because Bowman space hydrostatic pressure equals capillary hydrostatic pressure","C":"Because the basement membrane actively pumps protein back into the capillary","D":"Because oncotic pressure develops in fluid compartments containing cells","E":"Because protein passage into Bowman space is normally negligible"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Oncotic pressure arises from protein concentration, and because the glomerular barrier normally filters only a negligible amount of protein into Bowman space, the fluid there has essentially no oncotic pressure, allowing this term to be dropped from the Starling equation for glomerular capillaries.","boardPrep":"Bowman space oncotic pressure = 0 only because normal filtrate is essentially protein-free — this assumption breaks down in proteinuric disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — this same assumption is why an increase in filtered protein in glomerular disease can itself further alter the net ultrafiltration pressure calculation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply the Starling equation to calculate net ultrafiltration pressure across the glomerular capillaries, explaining why Bowman space oncotic pressure is physiologically considered zero.","batch":1,"stem":"At the beginning of the glomerular capillary, hydrostatic pressure in the capillary is 45 mm Hg, hydrostatic pressure in Bowman space is 10 mm Hg, and oncotic pressure in the capillary is 19 mm Hg. What is the net ultrafiltration pressure at this point?","choices":{"A":"+35 mm Hg, favoring filtration","B":"+16 mm Hg, favoring filtration","C":"-16 mm Hg, favoring absorption","D":"0 mm Hg, at filtration equilibrium","E":"+26 mm Hg, favoring filtration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Net ultrafiltration pressure equals glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure minus Bowman space hydrostatic pressure minus glomerular capillary oncotic pressure: 45 − 10 − 19 = +16 mm Hg, a positive value that strongly favors filtration, consistent with the beginning of the glomerular capillary before oncotic pressure has risen. A value of −16 mm Hg flips the sign: the hydrostatic gradient of 35 mm Hg exceeds the 19 mm Hg oncotic pressure, so the net force favors filtration, not absorption.","boardPrep":"Net UF pressure = PGC − PBS − πGC; at the start of the glomerular capillary this is a strongly positive +16 mm Hg.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — this exact arithmetic setup reappears in Objective 3's discussion of how this positive value falls to zero by the end of the capillary","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply the Starling equation to calculate net ultrafiltration pressure across the glomerular capillaries, explaining why Bowman space oncotic pressure is physiologically considered zero.","batch":1,"stem":"Which combination of properties of the glomerular capillary wall accounts for the fact that its filtration coefficient (Kf) is more than 100-fold that of typical systemic capillaries?","choices":{"A":"A much thinner basement membrane than found in systemic capillary walls","B":"A higher hydrostatic pressure driving fluid directly through the capillary wall","C":"Much higher water permeability paired with much greater surface area","D":"Active transport of water molecules across the entire endothelial layer","E":"A lower oncotic pressure present in the surrounding renal interstitium"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Kf reflects hydraulic conductance, which depends on both the water permeability per unit surface area and the total surface area available for filtration; glomerular capillaries have a combination of much higher intrinsic water permeability and much greater total surface area than systemic capillaries, together producing their dramatically higher Kf and correspondingly high GFR. Interstitial oncotic pressure is a Starling force, not part of Kf, which depends only on the wall's water permeability and surface area.","boardPrep":"Kf = permeability × surface area — glomerular capillaries win on both factors simultaneously, which is why GFR (180 L/day) dwarfs systemic capillary filtration.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — a reduced Kf (from a lower functioning nephron mass or a diseased basement membrane) directly lowers GFR even when the net ultrafiltration pressure itself is unchanged","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the dynamic concept of filtration equilibrium, analyzing how the progressive rise in glomerular capillary oncotic pressure along the length of the capillary eventually halts filtration under normal and altered states.","batch":1,"stem":"Which Starling pressure changes progressively along the length of the glomerular capillary to eventually produce filtration equilibrium?","choices":{"A":"Glomerular capillary oncotic pressure, which rises as protein-free fluid is filtered out","B":"Glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, which falls progressively along the capillary length","C":"Bowman space hydrostatic pressure, which rises steadily as filtrate accumulates downstream","D":"Bowman space oncotic pressure, which rises as protein leaks progressively into the filtrate","E":"Systemic arterial pressure, which falls steadily as blood traverses the capillary bed"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Unlike systemic capillaries, glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure remains essentially constant along the entire capillary length because efferent arteriolar resistance prevents its decline; instead, it is the glomerular capillary oncotic pressure that rises progressively as protein-free fluid is filtered out and protein is left behind in an ever-smaller plasma volume, eventually driving net ultrafiltration pressure to zero.","boardPrep":"Filtration equilibrium is caused by rising πGC, not falling PGC — the efferent arteriole is what keeps PGC constant along the glomerular capillary, unlike any systemic capillary bed.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — this same rising oncotic pressure becomes the peritubular capillary's high oncotic pressure that drives proximal tubular reabsorption immediately downstream","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the dynamic concept of filtration equilibrium, analyzing how the progressive rise in glomerular capillary oncotic pressure along the length of the capillary eventually halts filtration under normal and altered states.","batch":1,"stem":"Under normal physiologic conditions, at what point along the glomerular capillary does filtration equilibrium typically occur?","choices":{"A":"Immediately at the very beginning of the glomerular capillary","B":"After blood has already entered the efferent arteriole","C":"It does not occur under normal conditions; true equilibrium requires a pathologic state","D":"Right at the very end of the glomerular capillary","E":"Precisely at the midpoint of the glomerular capillary length"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Under normal conditions, net ultrafiltration pressure starts strongly positive at the beginning of the glomerular capillary and progressively falls as glomerular capillary oncotic pressure rises, reaching zero (filtration equilibrium) right at the end of the capillary, which conveniently means filtration occurs efficiently along virtually the entire capillary length before stopping. Filtration happens only across the glomerular capillary wall, so once blood enters the efferent arteriole there is no filtering surface on which equilibrium could occur.","boardPrep":"Normal filtration equilibrium occurs right at the capillary's end — filtration is essentially continuous along the whole glomerular capillary length under normal conditions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — a pathologic state that shifts equilibrium earlier along the capillary effectively shortens the functional filtering length and can lower GFR","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the dynamic concept of filtration equilibrium, analyzing how the progressive rise in glomerular capillary oncotic pressure along the length of the capillary eventually halts filtration under normal and altered states.","batch":1,"stem":"A reduction in renal plasma flow, without any change in the filtration coefficient, tends to shift the point of filtration equilibrium in which direction along the glomerular capillary?","choices":{"A":"Later along the capillary, since less fluid needs to be filtered to reach equilibrium","B":"Earlier along the capillary, since oncotic pressure rises more quickly per unit of blood flow","C":"The equilibrium point stays fixed because it is set by glomerular hydrostatic pressure","D":"Equilibrium is no longer reached, and filtration continues along the entire capillary","E":"Earlier along the capillary, because glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure falls"},"correct":"B","explanation":"With reduced renal plasma flow, a smaller total volume of plasma passes through the glomerular capillary per unit time, so the same absolute amount of fluid filtration produces a proportionally larger rise in protein concentration and oncotic pressure sooner along the capillary length, shifting filtration equilibrium earlier and potentially reducing the effective filtering surface actually used.","boardPrep":"Low RPF states can shift filtration equilibrium earlier along the capillary, one mechanism by which low renal blood flow states can reduce GFR beyond what falling PGC alone would predict.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — this RPF-dependence of the equilibrium point is part of why the filtration fraction (GFR/RPF) is itself a hemodynamically meaningful ratio, not just an arbitrary quotient","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare the hemodynamic consequences of selective afferent versus efferent arteriolar constriction on glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, renal plasma flow, glomerular filtration rate, and the filtration fraction.","batch":1,"stem":"Constriction of the afferent arteriole produces what combined effect on renal plasma flow, glomerular filtration rate, and filtration fraction?","choices":{"A":"RPF falls from reduced inflow, yet GFR climbs higher, pushing filtration fraction upward as well","B":"RPF unexpectedly climbs upward while GFR falls instead, dragging filtration fraction downward with it","C":"RPF falls from reduced inflow, and GFR falls even further in proportion, so filtration fraction drops","D":"RPF falls from reduced inflow, and GFR falls in proportion with it, so filtration fraction stays unchanged","E":"RPF stays essentially flat overall while GFR falls on its own accord, again lowering filtration fraction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Afferent arteriolar constriction reduces blood flow into the glomerular capillary, decreasing both renal plasma flow and glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure; because GFR falls in the same proportion as RPF, the filtration fraction (GFR divided by RPF) remains unchanged.","boardPrep":"Afferent constriction: RPF down, GFR down, filtration fraction unchanged — both numerator and denominator fall together.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — sympathetic activation and high-level angiotensin II both produce this afferent-predominant pattern discussed in SDL 8","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare the hemodynamic consequences of selective afferent versus efferent arteriolar constriction on glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, renal plasma flow, glomerular filtration rate, and the filtration fraction.","batch":1,"stem":"Constriction of the efferent arteriole produces what combined effect on renal plasma flow, glomerular filtration rate, and filtration fraction?","choices":{"A":"RPF falls from reduced inflow instead, and GFR falls right along with it in proportion, leaving filtration fraction unchanged","B":"RPF unexpectedly climbs upward alongside GFR, so filtration fraction stays essentially flat","C":"RPF falls while GFR climbs much higher, yet filtration fraction still somehow ends up lower","D":"RPF stays essentially flat overall while GFR climbs on its own accord, still raising filtration fraction","E":"RPF falls from restricted outflow, yet GFR climbs because capillary pressure rises, so filtration fraction increases"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Efferent arteriolar constriction restricts blood from leaving the glomerular capillary, decreasing renal plasma flow but raising glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure and therefore increasing GFR; because GFR rises while RPF falls, the filtration fraction (GFR divided by RPF) increases.","boardPrep":"Efferent constriction: RPF down, GFR up, filtration fraction up — GFR and RPF move in opposite directions, the signature pattern of efferent-selective vasoconstriction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — low-level angiotensin II and ACE inhibitors both act through this efferent-selective pathway, just in opposite directions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare the hemodynamic consequences of selective afferent versus efferent arteriolar constriction on glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, renal plasma flow, glomerular filtration rate, and the filtration fraction.","batch":1,"stem":"A decrease in plasma protein concentration, such as occurs in nephrotic syndrome from urinary protein loss, is expected to have what effect on GFR and filtration fraction, assuming RPF is unchanged?","choices":{"A":"Both GFR and filtration fraction increase together","B":"Both GFR and filtration fraction decline together","C":"GFR climbs even as filtration fraction actually falls","D":"GFR falls even as filtration fraction actually climbs","E":"Neither GFR nor filtration fraction shows any change"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A decrease in plasma protein concentration lowers glomerular capillary oncotic pressure, which increases net ultrafiltration pressure and therefore increases GFR; since RPF is assumed unchanged, the filtration fraction (GFR divided by RPF) increases along with GFR.","boardPrep":"Low plasma protein (as in nephrotic syndrome or liver failure) -> low oncotic pressure -> higher net ultrafiltration pressure -> GFR and filtration fraction both rise, opposite to the effect of high plasma protein.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — this oncotic-pressure-driven change in GFR is independent of the arteriolar resistance mechanisms and can be tested as a distractor against afferent/efferent effects","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the structural and chemical layers of the glomerular filtration barrier (fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane, and podocyte slit diaphragms), evaluating how size and negative charge selectivity (heparan sulfate glycoproteins) prevent protein filtration and how their disruption leads to selective proteinuria (such as in minimal change disease).","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man who is being evaluated as a living kidney donor volunteers for a research study of glomerular permselectivity. He is healthy, with a blood pressure of 118/74 mm Hg, a serum creatinine of 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and no proteinuria. He receives an infusion of three dextran preparations of identical molecular radius but different net charge (cationic, neutral, and anionic), and the filtration of each is measured. Which result would be expected if his glomerular barrier's charge selectivity is intact?","choices":{"A":"Cationic dextran is filtered most readily, anionic dextran least readily, at the same molecular radius","B":"The three dextrans are filtered identically, since charge does not affect filtration at any molecular size","C":"Anionic dextran is filtered most readily, cationic dextran least readily, at the same molecular radius","D":"Neutral dextran is filtered most readily, with both charged forms filtered equally less","E":"Cationic and anionic dextran are filtered identically, while neutral dextran is filtered least"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because the glomerular barrier carries fixed negative charges, cationic (positively charged) solutes are electrostatically attracted and filtered more readily, while anionic (negatively charged) solutes of the same molecular radius are repelled and filtered less readily; this classic dextran experiment is the direct experimental demonstration of charge-selective filtration.","boardPrep":"Dextran sieving experiment: same size, different charge -> cationic filters best, anionic filters worst — the classic proof that the glomerular barrier discriminates by charge, not just size.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — this experimental principle is exactly why albumin (net negative at physiologic pH) is filtered far less than its size alone would predict","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the structural and chemical layers of the glomerular filtration barrier (fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane, and podocyte slit diaphragms), evaluating how size and negative charge selectivity (heparan sulfate glycoproteins) prevent protein filtration and how their disruption leads to selective proteinuria (such as in minimal change disease).","batch":2,"stem":"A 56-year-old man with a 20-year history of poorly controlled type 2 diabetes mellitus comes to the clinic because of leg swelling. Blood pressure is 152/90 mm Hg, and he has 2+ pitting edema to the knees. A 24-hour urine collection contains 5.4 g of protein (reference range: 0–0.15 g/24 h), and urine protein electrophoresis shows albumin together with large amounts of IgG and other high-molecular-weight proteins. Serum albumin is 2.6 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). A kidney biopsy shows mesangial nodules and diffuse thickening of the glomerular capillary walls on light microscopy. Compared with a patient with minimal change disease, what does this pattern suggest about the underlying barrier defect?","choices":{"A":"An isolated loss of fixed negative charge identical to minimal change disease","B":"A defect limited to the endothelial fenestrae, with the basement membrane and slit diaphragms unaffected","C":"An architectural, size-based barrier defect affecting the basement membrane or podocyte slit diaphragms","D":"An intact filtration barrier, with the protein loss arising from impaired tubular reabsorption of filtered protein","E":"A defect that selectively targets the podocyte cell body while sparing the filtration diaphragms"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Nonselective proteinuria, in which both albumin and larger plasma proteins are lost, indicates that the size-based barrier itself (the basement membrane and/or podocyte slit diaphragms) has been structurally disrupted, in contrast to minimal change disease, where the size barrier remains intact and only the charge-based restriction is lost, producing selective loss of the moderately sized, negatively charged albumin alone.","boardPrep":"Selective proteinuria (albumin-only) = charge-barrier loss (minimal change disease); nonselective proteinuria (albumin + larger proteins) = structural size-barrier disruption — a key clinical distinction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — visible light-microscopy changes are also a distinguishing feature, since minimal change disease is defined precisely by its normal light-microscopic appearance despite significant proteinuria","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the structural and chemical layers of the glomerular filtration barrier (fenestrated endothelium, basement membrane, and podocyte slit diaphragms), evaluating how size and negative charge selectivity (heparan sulfate glycoproteins) prevent protein filtration and how their disruption leads to selective proteinuria (such as in minimal change disease).","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman comes to the clinic for a routine health maintenance examination. She feels well and takes no medications. Blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. Hemoglobin is 13.4 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL), leukocyte count is 6,800/mm3 (reference range: 4,500–11,000/mm3), and serum creatinine is 0.7 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Urinalysis shows no red or white blood cells. Why do red and white blood cells not appear in her glomerular filtrate, despite the relatively large 70- to 100-nm pores of the glomerular endothelium?","choices":{"A":"Blood cells are actively pumped back into the capillary lumen by podocytes","B":"Blood cells carry a strong negative charge that repels them from the barrier","C":"Blood cells are destroyed by mechanical shear forces before reaching the barrier","D":"Blood cells are far larger than the largest openings in the filtration barrier","E":"Blood cells are retained by a separate cellular filter located upstream of the glomerulus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Although the endothelial fenestrae are relatively large at 70 to 100 nm, they are still far smaller than red or white blood cells, and the downstream basement membrane and podocyte filtration slits (25 to 60 nm) impose an even smaller effective size limit; blood cells are simply too large to pass through any layer of the barrier, with no active transport or charge-based mechanism required to exclude them.","boardPrep":"Blood cells are excluded by simple size, not charge or active transport — even the largest barrier pores are orders of magnitude smaller than a blood cell.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — hematuria in glomerular disease therefore implies a breach in the barrier, since intact layers exclude cells by size","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply the Starling equation to calculate net ultrafiltration pressure across the glomerular capillaries, explaining why Bowman space oncotic pressure is physiologically considered zero.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man comes to the clinic for a routine health maintenance examination. He feels well and takes no medications. Blood pressure is 120/78 mm Hg. Serum albumin is 4.4 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL), and creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). At the efferent end of his glomerular capillaries, the hydrostatic pressure in the capillary is estimated at 45 mm Hg, the hydrostatic pressure in Bowman space at 10 mm Hg, and the capillary oncotic pressure, which has risen along the capillary, at 35 mm Hg. Which of the following best describes the net ultrafiltration pressure at this point?","choices":{"A":"0 mm Hg, indicating complete filtration equilibrium has been reached","B":"+16 mm Hg, representing the same strong filtration drive as the start of the capillary","C":"-10 mm Hg, representing net fluid absorption into the capillary","D":"+35 mm Hg, representing maximal net ultrafiltration pressure","E":"-35 mm Hg, representing complete reversal of the normal filtration direction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Net ultrafiltration pressure equals glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure minus Bowman space hydrostatic pressure minus glomerular capillary oncotic pressure: 45 − 10 − 35 = 0 mm Hg; a value of zero means no further net filtration can occur, which is the definition of filtration equilibrium, and this normally occurs right at the end of the glomerular capillary.","boardPrep":"Net UF pressure falls from +16 mm Hg at the start to 0 mm Hg at the end of the glomerular capillary — entirely because oncotic pressure rises from 19 to 35 mm Hg as protein-free fluid is filtered out.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — net ultrafiltration pressure falls from about +16 mm Hg at the afferent end to 0 at filtration equilibrium as capillary oncotic pressure rises","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply the Starling equation to calculate net ultrafiltration pressure across the glomerular capillaries, explaining why Bowman space oncotic pressure is physiologically considered zero.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of 6 hours of severe, colicky left flank pain that radiates to the groin. Temperature is 37.1°C (98.8°F), pulse is 104/min, and blood pressure is 146/88 mm Hg. Urinalysis shows 30 red blood cells per high-power field (reference range: 0–2/hpf). Serum creatinine is 1.1 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). CT shows an 8-mm stone at the left ureterovesical junction completely obstructing the ureter, with dilation of the ureter and collecting system above it. Using the Starling equation, what is the direct effect on net ultrafiltration pressure and GFR in the obstructed kidney?","choices":{"A":"Net ultrafiltration pressure increases because glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure rises to compensate, and GFR increases","B":"Net ultrafiltration pressure is unaffected because Bowman space pressure is not a term in the Starling equation, and GFR is unchanged","C":"Net ultrafiltration pressure decreases because glomerular capillary oncotic pressure rises in response, and GFR decreases","D":"Net ultrafiltration pressure increases because Bowman space oncotic pressure falls to zero, and GFR increases","E":"Net ultrafiltration pressure decreases because Bowman space hydrostatic pressure rises, and GFR decreases"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Bowman space hydrostatic pressure is a term that opposes filtration in the Starling equation; when urine outflow is obstructed, pressure backs up as far as Bowman space, raising this opposing pressure, which directly lowers net ultrafiltration pressure and therefore decreases GFR in the obstructed kidney.","boardPrep":"Ureteral obstruction raises Bowman space pressure -> lowers net UF pressure -> lowers GFR, a mechanism distinct from any arteriolar resistance change and worth recognizing as its own Starling-force pathway.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — this is the classic postrenal mechanism of acute kidney injury, contrasted with the prerenal (hemodynamic) and intrinsic mechanisms discussed elsewhere in this block","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply the Starling equation to calculate net ultrafiltration pressure across the glomerular capillaries, explaining why Bowman space oncotic pressure is physiologically considered zero.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman with chronic kidney disease from childhood reflux nephropathy comes to the clinic for follow-up. Blood pressure is 136/84 mm Hg. Serum creatinine is 2.1 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and ultrasound shows two small, scarred kidneys. Her disease has chronically reduced her number of functioning nephrons, lowering her total glomerular filtration coefficient (Kf), while the net ultrafiltration pressure in each remaining nephron is normal. What is the expected effect on her overall GFR?","choices":{"A":"Overall GFR stays normal, since per-nephron ultrafiltration pressure sets GFR","B":"Overall GFR rises, since remaining nephrons compensate by increasing their Kf","C":"Overall GFR falls whenever net ultrafiltration pressure also happens to fall","D":"Overall GFR falls, since GFR is the product of Kf and net ultrafiltration pressure","E":"Overall GFR is unaffected until fewer than half of the nephrons are lost"},"correct":"D","explanation":"GFR equals Kf multiplied by the net ultrafiltration pressure; because a chronic reduction in functioning nephron mass lowers the total Kf (the combined hydraulic conductance and surface area across all glomeruli) even when the net ultrafiltration pressure per remaining nephron is unchanged, overall GFR falls in direct proportion to the loss of functioning filtering surface area.","boardPrep":"GFR = Kf × net ultrafiltration pressure — losing functioning nephron mass lowers GFR through the Kf term, which is a major way loss of nephron mass lowers GFR in chronic kidney disease, although remaining nephrons partly compensate by hyperfiltration.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — this Kf-driven decline in GFR is the physiologic basis for using GFR itself as the primary staging criterion in chronic kidney disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the dynamic concept of filtration equilibrium, analyzing how the progressive rise in glomerular capillary oncotic pressure along the length of the capillary eventually halts filtration under normal and altered states.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman, gravida 1, comes to the clinic for a routine prenatal visit at 14 weeks' gestation. She feels well. Blood pressure is 108/66 mm Hg. Serum creatinine is 0.5 mg/dL (reference range in pregnancy: 0.4–0.8 mg/dL). Her renal plasma flow has increased substantially since conception. Assuming her glomerular filtration coefficient (Kf) and arteriolar resistances are unchanged, how does the position of filtration equilibrium along her glomerular capillaries most likely shift?","choices":{"A":"Earlier along the capillary, since more total protein is delivered to the capillary per minute","B":"Later along the capillary, or equilibrium may not be reached before the capillary ends","C":"The equilibrium position stays fixed because it is set by the filtration coefficient","D":"Earlier along the capillary, because increased renal plasma flow raises glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure enough to accelerate oncotic pressure buildup","E":"Later along the capillary, because glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure falls"},"correct":"B","explanation":"With increased renal plasma flow, a larger total volume of plasma passes through the glomerular capillary per unit time, so the same absolute amount of fluid filtered produces a smaller proportional rise in protein concentration and oncotic pressure at any given point along the capillary, shifting the point of filtration equilibrium later along the capillary or preventing it from being reached before the capillary ends, which increases the effective filtering surface used.","boardPrep":"High RPF states (like pregnancy) can push filtration equilibrium later or eliminate it entirely, contributing to the physiologic rise in GFR seen in those states.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — this is the opposite scenario from a low-RPF state, where equilibrium shifts earlier and can limit GFR below what Kf and pressures alone would predict","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the dynamic concept of filtration equilibrium, analyzing how the progressive rise in glomerular capillary oncotic pressure along the length of the capillary eventually halts filtration under normal and altered states.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman comes to the clinic because of ankle swelling at the end of each day. She has varicose veins and no history of heart, liver, or kidney disease. Blood pressure is 124/78 mm Hg. Examination shows 1+ pitting edema of both ankles. Serum albumin is 4.2 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL), creatinine is 0.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and urinalysis is normal. Her physician explains that hydrostatic pressure falls progressively along her leg capillaries but stays nearly constant along the length of a glomerular capillary. Which feature best explains the nearly constant glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure?","choices":{"A":"The efferent arteriole adds a second downstream resistance that maintains glomerular pressure","B":"The glomerular capillary wall is rigid and resists distending the way systemic capillary walls do","C":"Systemic capillaries generally lack any arteriolar resistance vessels controlling their flow","D":"Glomerular capillary blood flow is too slow for any pressure gradient to ever develop along its length","E":"The kidney actively regulates glomerular capillary pressure through neural reflexes acting on the vasculature"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Systemic capillaries have only a single upstream arteriole controlling inflow, so hydrostatic pressure falls progressively along the capillary as resistance is encountered downstream; glomerular capillaries are unique in having a second arteriole, the efferent arteriole, positioned downstream, whose resistance prevents the pressure decline that would otherwise occur as fluid is filtered out along the capillary length.","boardPrep":"The efferent arteriole is the anatomic feature unique to the glomerulus that keeps PGC constant along its length — no other capillary bed in the body has this two-arteriole arrangement.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — this same efferent arteriole is the site of action for angiotensin II's GFR-protective effect discussed in SDL 8","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate the dynamic concept of filtration equilibrium, analyzing how the progressive rise in glomerular capillary oncotic pressure along the length of the capillary eventually halts filtration under normal and altered states.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man comes to the clinic for a routine health maintenance examination. He feels well and takes no medications. Vital signs are within normal limits. Serum albumin is 4.5 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL), creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and urinalysis shows no protein. In his glomeruli, filtration equilibrium is reached by the time the blood arrives at the efferent end of each tuft. Which statement correctly describes the blood leaving through his efferent arterioles?","choices":{"A":"Its oncotic pressure is lower than that of plasma entering the glomerulus","B":"Its oncotic pressure stays unchanged from the afferent arteriolar value","C":"Its oncotic pressure equals capillary hydrostatic pressure minus Bowman space pressure","D":"It contains measurable amounts of filtered protein that would not otherwise ever be present","E":"Its hydrostatic pressure is unchanged from the glomerular capillary value in the peritubular capillaries"},"correct":"C","explanation":"By definition, filtration equilibrium is reached once glomerular capillary oncotic pressure has risen enough to equal the net hydrostatic driving pressure, so net ultrafiltration pressure falls to zero. The blood leaving through the efferent arteriole therefore carries an oncotic pressure equal to capillary hydrostatic pressure minus Bowman space pressure, higher than that of afferent plasma because filtration concentrated the protein, and this elevated oncotic pressure becomes the driving force for peritubular capillary reabsorption downstream. A lower or unchanged oncotic pressure, filtered protein in the efferent blood, and an unchanged glomerular-level hydrostatic pressure downstream are all incompatible with this.","boardPrep":"Step 1: At filtration equilibrium, oncotic pressure equals PGC minus PBS; that value carries into the peritubular capillary as the driving force for reabsorption, the direct physiologic link between glomerular filtration and proximal tubular reabsorption.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — the high oncotic pressure of efferent blood leaving at filtration equilibrium becomes peritubular oncotic pressure, which sets proximal reabsorption","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl09_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare the hemodynamic consequences of selective afferent versus efferent arteriolar constriction on glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, renal plasma flow, glomerular filtration rate, and the filtration fraction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man with hypertension that is resistant to three medications comes to the clinic for follow-up. He has smoked 1 pack of cigarettes daily for 45 years. He is hypertensive at 172/94 mm Hg, and bruits are heard on both sides of the upper abdomen. Duplex ultrasound shows high-grade stenosis of the main artery to each kidney. Lisinopril is added to his regimen. One week later, his serum creatinine has risen from 1.3 to 2.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and potassium is 5.4 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Which mechanism best explains his acute kidney injury?","choices":{"A":"Blocking angiotensin II causes disproportionate afferent arteriolar constriction that directly reduces renal blood flow while efferent tone stays unchanged","B":"Blocking angiotensin II removes the efferent constriction that was maintaining glomerular pressure despite low renal blood flow","C":"ACE inhibitors directly damage the glomerular basement membrane, independent of any hemodynamic effect on the arterioles","D":"ACE inhibitors cause systemic hypotension severe enough to trigger prerenal azotemia in most treated patients","E":"Blocking angiotensin II increases aldosterone secretion enough to cause dangerous systemic volume overload"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In bilateral renal artery stenosis, reduced renal perfusion pressure activates the renin-angiotensin system, and the resulting angiotensin II preferentially constricts the efferent arteriole to maintain glomerular capillary pressure and GFR despite low renal blood flow; an ACE inhibitor removes this compensatory efferent constriction, causing glomerular capillary pressure and GFR to collapse even though renal blood flow itself may not change much further.","boardPrep":"Bilateral renal artery stenosis + ACE inhibitor/ARB = classic acute kidney injury because the kidney was relying on angiotensin II's efferent constriction just to maintain GFR at a low perfusion pressure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — this scenario is the clinical endpoint of the same efferent-selective angiotensin II mechanism first introduced in SDL 8","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare the hemodynamic consequences of selective afferent versus efferent arteriolar constriction on glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, renal plasma flow, glomerular filtration rate, and the filtration fraction.","batch":2,"stem":"Two 40-year-old men in a renal physiology study each receive an infusion that reduces renal plasma flow by 20% through arteriolar constriction. Both have a baseline blood pressure of 120/78 mm Hg and a serum creatinine of 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). In Patient A, the infused agent constricts only the afferent arteriole; in Patient B, it constricts only the efferent arteriole. Which patient has the higher filtration fraction during the infusion?","choices":{"A":"Patient B, since efferent constriction raises GFR while afferent constriction lowers it proportionally","B":"Patient A, because afferent constriction raises glomerular capillary pressure more than efferent constriction does","C":"Both patients have identical filtration fractions, since renal plasma flow is the same in each","D":"Patient A, because afferent constriction preserves a normal net ultrafiltration pressure","E":"Neither patient's filtration fraction can be determined without knowing absolute renal blood flow"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Filtration fraction equals GFR divided by RPF; afferent constriction lowers both RPF and GFR proportionally, leaving filtration fraction unchanged, while efferent constriction lowers RPF but raises GFR by increasing glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, so Patient B's filtration fraction (GFR up, RPF down) is higher than Patient A's (both down proportionally).","boardPrep":"Same RPF change, different GFR direction: afferent constriction keeps filtration fraction flat, efferent constriction pushes filtration fraction up — a favorite conceptual comparison question.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — recognizing this filtration-fraction divergence is the fastest way to distinguish an afferent-predominant from an efferent-predominant vasoconstrictor state on an exam vignette","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare the hemodynamic consequences of selective afferent versus efferent arteriolar constriction on glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, renal plasma flow, glomerular filtration rate, and the filtration fraction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department after a car crash with a lacerated spleen. She is pale and diaphoretic. Pulse is 132/min, and blood pressure is 84/52 mm Hg. Hemoglobin is 8.6 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL), and serum creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Her elevated sympathetic tone, which constricts mainly the afferent arteriole, and her elevated angiotensin II, which constricts mainly the efferent arteriole, are acting at the same time. What is the net expected effect on her GFR compared with her renal plasma flow?","choices":{"A":"RPF and GFR fall by the same proportion, since the two arteriolar effects cancel each other out","B":"RPF is relatively protected while GFR falls substantially, lowering the filtration fraction","C":"Both RPF and GFR rise, since the two mechanisms produce opposing effects that net to an increase","D":"GFR falls to zero immediately, since both arterioles are simultaneously maximally constricted","E":"RPF falls substantially while GFR is relatively protected, falling proportionally less"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sympathetic tone constricts the afferent arteriole (lowering both RPF and GFR), while angiotensin II preferentially constricts the efferent arteriole (lowering RPF while supporting glomerular capillary pressure); acting together, both mechanisms substantially reduce RPF, but the efferent-predominant angiotensin II effect partially offsets the afferent-driven fall in GFR, so GFR is relatively protected and falls proportionally less than RPF, raising the filtration fraction even as absolute GFR still declines somewhat.","boardPrep":"In compensated hemorrhage, RPF typically falls more than GFR because angiotensin II's efferent-predominant action partially protects GFR — this is why filtration fraction typically rises in hypovolemic states.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — this combined afferent-plus-efferent mechanism is the physiologic reason a patient can maintain a deceptively preserved GFR early in shock despite a large fall in renal blood flow","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl09_q01","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Compare the hemodynamic consequences of selective afferent versus efferent arteriolar constriction on glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure, renal plasma flow, glomerular filtration rate, and the filtration fraction.","batch":4,"stem":"In a physiology study, a healthy 24-year-old man receives a continuous intravenous infusion of angiotensin II at a low dose that raises his mean arterial pressure by 4 mm Hg. Inulin and para-aminohippuric acid (PAH) clearances are measured before and during the infusion to estimate the glomerular filtration rate (GFR) and renal plasma flow (RPF). Which of the following sets of changes during the infusion, compared with baseline, is most likely?","grid":{"headers":["GFR","RPF","Filtration fraction"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↓","↔"],"B":["↑","↔","↑"],"C":["↓","↓","↑"],"D":["↑","↓","↑"],"E":["↓","↔","↓"]},"correct":"D","explanation":"At low concentrations angiotensin II preferentially constricts the efferent arteriole, so the pattern (↑ GFR, ↓ RPF, ↑ filtration fraction) is correct. Efferent constriction raises glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure (P_GC), which increases net ultrafiltration pressure, so GFR rises slightly or is maintained, while the added resistance in series reduces RPF; with GFR up and RPF down, the filtration fraction (GFR/RPF) rises. The pattern (↓ GFR, ↓ RPF, ↔ filtration fraction) is afferent arteriolar constriction: P_GC falls, and GFR and RPF fall together, leaving the filtration fraction unchanged. The pattern (↑ GFR, ↔ RPF, ↑ filtration fraction) follows a fall in plasma protein concentration, which lowers glomerular capillary oncotic pressure and raises GFR without changing RPF. The pattern (↓ GFR, ↓ RPF, ↑ filtration fraction) is severe efferent constriction: RPF falls so far that the rise in glomerular oncotic pressure outweighs the rise in P_GC, and GFR declines while the filtration fraction keeps rising; a low dose stays in the moderate range. The pattern (↓ GFR, ↔ RPF, ↓ filtration fraction) follows a rise in plasma protein concentration, which raises glomerular capillary oncotic pressure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: efferent constriction raises GFR and filtration fraction and lowers RPF; afferent constriction lowers GFR and RPF together, leaving filtration fraction unchanged. COMLEX: ACE inhibitors and ARBs remove the efferent tone that angiotensin II supplies, so GFR can fall sharply in bilateral renal artery stenosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — angiotensin II and sympathetic nerves set afferent and efferent arteriolar resistance","isHighYield":true}],"10":[{"id":"sdl10_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the kinetics of renal carrier-mediated transport systems, analyzing the concepts of transport maximum, threshold, and splay in saturable reabsorptive pathways (utilizing the glucose titration curve) versus saturable secretory pathways (utilizing the para-aminohippuric acid titration curve).","batch":1,"stem":"On the glucose titration curve, what does the term \"splay\" describe?","choices":{"A":"The portion of the curve where reabsorption nears saturation but is not yet fully saturated","B":"The portion of the curve where no glucose reabsorption occurs at any plasma concentration","C":"The specific plasma concentration at which glucose first begins appearing in the urine","D":"The maximum rate of glucose reabsorption reached once carriers become fully saturated","E":"The portion of the curve where glucose filtration exceeds glucose reabsorption at any concentration"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Splay is the region of the glucose titration curve near the transport maximum where reabsorption is still increasing but has not yet reached its plateau; because of splay, glucose begins appearing in the urine (at threshold) at a plasma concentration lower than the one at which reabsorption actually levels off at Tm.","boardPrep":"Splay = the \"not-quite-saturated-yet\" zone near Tm — it explains why glucosuria threshold occurs below the true Tm value, not exactly at it.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — splay has two explanations: low-affinity Na+-glucose cotransporters near Tm, and heterogeneity of Tm values across individual nephrons","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the kinetics of renal carrier-mediated transport systems, analyzing the concepts of transport maximum, threshold, and splay in saturable reabsorptive pathways (utilizing the glucose titration curve) versus saturable secretory pathways (utilizing the para-aminohippuric acid titration curve).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the fundamental difference between the glucose titration curve and the para-aminohippuric acid (PAH) titration curve?","choices":{"A":"Glucose has no transport maximum, while PAH has a well-defined transport maximum","B":"Glucose is reabsorbed by paracellular diffusion, while PAH is secreted by primary active transport","C":"Glucose illustrates a saturable reabsorptive pathway, while PAH illustrates a saturable secretory pathway","D":"Glucose excretion rises linearly with plasma level, while PAH excretion plateaus once Tm is reached","E":"Glucose is filtered but not reabsorbed, while PAH is reabsorbed but not secreted"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The glucose titration curve tracks a carrier-mediated reabsorptive pathway (Na+-glucose cotransport) that becomes saturated at Tm, causing excretion to rise sharply once Tm is exceeded, whereas the PAH titration curve tracks a carrier-mediated secretory pathway that becomes saturated at its own Tm, causing excretion to rise steeply below Tm (filtration plus secretion) and then less steeply above Tm (filtration alone, since secretion is already saturated).","boardPrep":"Same saturation-kinetics logic, opposite direction: glucose's Tm limits how much can be reabsorbed; PAH's Tm limits how much can be secreted.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — recognizing which curve shape belongs to a reabsorptive versus a secretory pathway is the fastest way to identify an unlabeled titration curve on an exam","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the kinetics of renal carrier-mediated transport systems, analyzing the concepts of transport maximum, threshold, and splay in saturable reabsorptive pathways (utilizing the glucose titration curve) versus saturable secretory pathways (utilizing the para-aminohippuric acid titration curve).","batch":1,"stem":"Above the transport maximum (Tm) for PAH, why does excretion continue to rise with increasing plasma PAH concentration, even though secretion has already saturated?","choices":{"A":"Because secretion capacity somehow increases automatically once Tm is exceeded","B":"Because PAH reabsorption begins immediately above Tm and adds to total excretion","C":"Because renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate both rise automatically at high plasma PAH concentrations","D":"Because excretion above Tm levels off and stops tracking plasma PAH concentration","E":"Because the filtration component of excretion keeps climbing linearly with plasma concentration"},"correct":"E","explanation":"For a secreted substance, excretion equals the sum of filtration and secretion; once secretion saturates at Tm, only the filtration component (which is not saturable and rises linearly with plasma concentration) continues to increase excretion, producing the shallower slope seen above Tm on the PAH titration curve.","boardPrep":"Above Tm, PAH excretion still climbs, just more slowly, because filtration alone (a linear, non-saturable process) is carrying the increase once secretion maxes out.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — this same filtration-plus-saturable-transport logic applies to any secreted or reabsorbed substance with a defined Tm","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the passive transport of urea across the nephron, analyzing how water reabsorption establishes the concentration gradients that drive simple urea diffusion, and how antidiuretic hormone dynamically regulates facilitated urea transport (via UT1 transporters) in the inner medullary collecting duct.","batch":1,"stem":"What creates the driving force for passive urea reabsorption along the proximal tubule, given that the initial filtrate has the same urea concentration as blood?","choices":{"A":"Urea is actively pumped out of the tubular fluid by a dedicated, energy-requiring transporter in the proximal tubule","B":"Proximal tubule cells synthesize additional urea that dilutes the luminal concentration substantially","C":"Urea is cotransported together with glucose out of the proximal tubule lumen","D":"Reabsorbing water without reabsorbing urea raises its tubular concentration above the filtered level","E":"Bicarbonate reabsorption in the proximal tubule directly drives parallel urea reabsorption"},"correct":"D","explanation":"As water is reabsorbed along the proximal tubule, urea lags slightly behind, so its luminal concentration rises above that of blood, creating a concentration gradient that drives passive (diffusive) urea reabsorption; by the end of the proximal tubule, about 50% of the filtered urea has been reabsorbed this way.","boardPrep":"Urea reabsorption follows water reabsorption passively — no dedicated proximal tubule urea pump is needed, just the concentration gradient water reabsorption creates.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — this same principle (water reabsorption concentrates urea, driving further passive reabsorption) recurs at each downstream urea-permeable segment","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the passive transport of urea across the nephron, analyzing how water reabsorption establishes the concentration gradients that drive simple urea diffusion, and how antidiuretic hormone dynamically regulates facilitated urea transport (via UT1 transporters) in the inner medullary collecting duct.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does the percentage of filtered urea remaining in the tubular lumen rise from 50% at the end of the proximal tubule to 110% at the bend of the loop of Henle?","choices":{"A":"Urea is synthesized de novo directly by thin descending limb epithelial cells themselves","B":"Urea is secreted into the thin descending limb from the high-urea interstitial fluid of the inner medulla","C":"Water reabsorption in the thin descending limb concentrates urea without any secretion occurring","D":"A calculation artifact causes the percentage to exceed 100% despite no net addition of urea occurring","E":"The thin ascending limb actively secretes urea into the descending limb fluid further upstream"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The thin descending limb of the loop of Henle passes through the inner medulla, where a high interstitial urea concentration (established by other mechanisms) creates a gradient favoring diffusion of urea into the tubular lumen; this secretion adds more urea than was reabsorbed in the proximal tubule, so the amount present at the bend of the loop actually exceeds the original filtered load, reaching about 110%.","boardPrep":"Urea handling is not one-directional: reabsorbed in the proximal tubule (down to 50%), then secreted back in the thin descending limb (up to 110%) before ADH-dependent reabsorption downstream brings it back down.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — this urea secretion into the descending limb is part of \"urea recycling,\" which contributes to the corticopapillary osmotic gradient discussed later in this block","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the passive transport of urea across the nephron, analyzing how water reabsorption establishes the concentration gradients that drive simple urea diffusion, and how antidiuretic hormone dynamically regulates facilitated urea transport (via UT1 transporters) in the inner medullary collecting duct.","batch":1,"stem":"In the presence of antidiuretic hormone (ADH), what happens to urea handling specifically in the inner medullary collecting duct?","choices":{"A":"ADH upregulates UT1, increasing facilitated urea reabsorption down its gradient","B":"ADH inhibits UT1, preventing any further urea reabsorption at this segment","C":"ADH stimulates active, energy-requiring urea secretion into the collecting duct lumen","D":"ADH affects water permeability at this segment but leaves urea transport unchanged","E":"ADH converts urea transport in this segment from facilitated diffusion to active transport"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The inner medullary collecting duct expresses UT1, a facilitated urea transporter that is upregulated by ADH; in the presence of ADH, UT1 activity increases urea reabsorption down its concentration gradient into the inner medullary interstitium, ultimately leaving only about 40% of the original filtered urea to be excreted.","boardPrep":"ADH does double duty in the inner medullary collecting duct: it increases water permeability via aquaporins AND upregulates UT1 for urea reabsorption — both contribute to the corticopapillary gradient.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — this UT1 upregulation is a distinct ADH target separate from its aquaporin-2 water channel effect, even though both act in the same nephron segment","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the bidirectional transport mechanics (reabsorption, secretion, and post-secretory reabsorption) of uric acid in the proximal tubule, predicting how probenecid and varying doses of salicylates pharmacologically alter uric acid excretion.","batch":1,"stem":"What percentage of the filtered load of uric acid is ultimately excreted in the urine under normal conditions, after the full sequence of proximal tubule reabsorption, secretion, and post-secretory reabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 1%","B":"Approximately 25%","C":"Approximately 50%","D":"Approximately 90%","E":"Approximately 10%"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Uric acid is freely filtered (100% at Bowman space), then 99% is reabsorbed by URAT1 in the proximal convoluted tubule, then roughly half of the original filtered load is secreted back into the lumen (bringing the total present back up to about 50%), and finally most of that secreted uric acid is reabsorbed again by URAT1 in the proximal straight tubule, leaving approximately 10% of the original filtered load to be excreted.","boardPrep":"Uric acid's three-step dance (reabsorb ~99%, secrete back to ~50%, reabsorb again down to ~10%) nets out to about 10% excreted — memorize this final number, since drugs act on the individual steps.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — this final 10% figure is the baseline that probenecid and salicylates shift in opposite pharmacologic directions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the bidirectional transport mechanics (reabsorption, secretion, and post-secretory reabsorption) of uric acid in the proximal tubule, predicting how probenecid and varying doses of salicylates pharmacologically alter uric acid excretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Probenecid inhibits both uric acid reabsorption and uric acid secretion in the proximal tubule. What is its net effect on uric acid excretion, and why?","choices":{"A":"Excretion increases, because probenecid blocks more reabsorption than secretion at baseline","B":"Excretion decreases, because probenecid blocks more secretion than reabsorption at baseline","C":"Excretion is unchanged, because the two inhibited processes cancel each other out exactly","D":"Excretion increases, because probenecid selectively stimulates tubular uric acid secretion","E":"Excretion decreases, because probenecid prevents uric acid from being filtered at the glomerulus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because baseline uric acid reabsorption (about 99% of the filtered load) is quantitatively much greater than baseline uric acid secretion, blocking both processes with probenecid removes proportionally more reabsorption than secretion, so the net effect is decreased reabsorption and increased uric acid excretion, making probenecid a uricosuric drug used to treat hyperuricemia and gout.","boardPrep":"Probenecid blocks both uric acid reabsorption (URAT1) and secretion (organic anion transporters), but since reabsorption is the dominant process at baseline, the net result is always more excretion; this is why probenecid treats gout.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — contrast this dual-blockade mechanism with salicylate's dose-dependent, single-direction-predominant mechanism in the next objective's teaching point","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the bidirectional transport mechanics (reabsorption, secretion, and post-secretory reabsorption) of uric acid in the proximal tubule, predicting how probenecid and varying doses of salicylates pharmacologically alter uric acid excretion.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient takes a low dose of aspirin (salicylate) for cardioprotection. What is the expected effect on serum uric acid concentration?","choices":{"A":"Serum uric acid rises, because low-dose salicylate predominantly inhibits uric acid secretion","B":"Serum uric acid falls, because low-dose salicylate predominantly inhibits uric acid reabsorption","C":"Serum uric acid is unaffected, since salicylate has no effect on uric acid transport at any dose","D":"Serum uric acid rises, because low-dose salicylate directly stimulates hepatic uric acid production","E":"Serum uric acid falls, because low-dose salicylate increases the glomerular filtration of uric acid"},"correct":"A","explanation":"At low doses, salicylate predominantly inhibits uric acid secretion in the proximal tubule; because secretion is blocked while reabsorption remains largely intact, net uric acid reabsorption increases, decreasing uric acid excretion and raising the blood concentration of uric acid, the opposite of the effect wanted in a patient being treated for gout.","boardPrep":"Low-dose aspirin RAISES uric acid (secretion-blocking, can precipitate gout); high-dose aspirin LOWERS uric acid (reabsorption-blocking, uricosuric) — a classic dose-dependent paradox worth memorizing exactly.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — this paradox is why low-dose aspirin is used cautiously in patients with a history of gout, while high-dose salicylates historically had the opposite, uricosuric effect","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the physical principles of nonionic diffusion and pH trapping to predict the partition of weak acids and weak bases across renal tubular cell membranes, formulating a clinical rationale for manipulating urine pH (alkalinization or acidification) to accelerate the clearance of toxic substances in medical emergencies.","batch":1,"stem":"For a weak acid such as salicylate, which chemical form can diffuse across renal tubular cells by nonionic diffusion?","choices":{"A":"The charged, deprotonated A- form of the molecule","B":"Both the HA and A- forms diffuse equally well","C":"The uncharged, protonated HA form of the molecule","D":"Neither form can diffuse; salicylate requires active transport in both directions","E":"The charged form diffuses faster than the uncharged form"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Nonionic diffusion refers to the fact that only the uncharged species of a weak acid or base can cross the lipid bilayer of tubular cell membranes; for a weak acid, this is the protonated HA form, while the charged A- form cannot diffuse and remains trapped on whichever side of the membrane it currently occupies.","boardPrep":"\"Nonionic\" = uncharged = the only form that crosses the membrane — this single rule explains the entire pH-trapping phenomenon for both weak acids and weak bases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — this exact rule, applied to ammonia (NH3) versus ammonium (NH4+), reappears in the acid-base physiology covered later in this block","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the physical principles of nonionic diffusion and pH trapping to predict the partition of weak acids and weak bases across renal tubular cell membranes, formulating a clinical rationale for manipulating urine pH (alkalinization or acidification) to accelerate the clearance of toxic substances in medical emergencies.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does alkalinizing the urine increase the clearance of a weak acid such as salicylate?","choices":{"A":"Alkaline urine favors the uncharged HA form, which is actively secreted at a faster rate","B":"Alkaline urine directly increases glomerular filtration of salicylate independent of its charge state","C":"Alkaline urine denatures the salicylate molecule, preventing any tubular reabsorption","D":"Alkaline urine favors the charged A- form of salicylate, trapping it for excretion","E":"Alkaline urine decreases renal blood flow, concentrating salicylate in a smaller urine volume"},"correct":"D","explanation":"At alkaline urine pH, the equilibrium for a weak acid shifts toward the charged A- form, which cannot diffuse back across tubular cells into the blood; because this \"back-diffusion\" is minimized, more of the secreted and filtered salicylate remains trapped in the urine and is excreted, increasing its clearance.","boardPrep":"Alkalinize the urine to treat salicylate (aspirin) overdose — alkaline pH traps the charged A- form in the urine, exactly the same logic used for other weak acids like phenobarbital.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — this is a direct clinical application tested as \"how do you treat aspirin overdose,\" with the mechanistic answer being urine alkalinization via nonionic diffusion/pH trapping","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the physical principles of nonionic diffusion and pH trapping to predict the partition of weak acids and weak bases across renal tubular cell membranes, formulating a clinical rationale for manipulating urine pH (alkalinization or acidification) to accelerate the clearance of toxic substances in medical emergencies.","batch":1,"stem":"For a weak base such as morphine, how does the pH-trapping effect on excretion differ from that of a weak acid such as salicylate?","choices":{"A":"The relationship is identical: alkaline urine increases clearance of both weak acids and weak bases","B":"The relationship is mirrored: acidic urine traps the weak base for elimination","C":"Weak bases are unaffected by urine pH manipulation","D":"Acidic urine substantially decreases the overall renal clearance of weak bases","E":"Weak bases require alkaline urine specifically for effective pH-dependent trapping"},"correct":"B","explanation":"For a weak base, the charged BH+ form predominates at acidic pH and cannot diffuse back into blood, so acidic urine traps the weak base for excretion, increasing its clearance; this is the mirror image of the weak acid case, where alkaline urine (which favors the charged A- form) increases clearance instead.","boardPrep":"Weak acids: alkalinize urine to trap and excrete. Weak bases: acidify urine to trap and excrete. Opposite pH manipulations for opposite molecule classes — a frequently tested distinction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — this mirrored relationship is why amphetamine (a weak base) overdose is treated with urine acidification, the opposite intervention from salicylate overdose","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the kinetics of renal carrier-mediated transport systems, analyzing the concepts of transport maximum, threshold, and splay in saturable reabsorptive pathways (utilizing the glucose titration curve) versus saturable secretory pathways (utilizing the para-aminohippuric acid titration curve).","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman comes to the clinic because of 6 weeks of increased thirst, frequent urination, fatigue, and a 4-kg (9-lb) weight loss. She takes no medications. Blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg, and pulse is 92/min. Serum glucose is 410 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), sodium is 137 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and creatinine is 0.7 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Urinalysis shows 4+ glucose. Formal titration testing shows that her renal transport maximum (Tm) for glucose is normal at 375 mg/min and that her renal threshold for glucose is also normal. What is the most likely underlying cause of her glucosuria?","choices":{"A":"A congenitally low renal Tm for glucose that causes glucosuria even at normal plasma glucose levels","B":"An elevated plasma glucose concentration from insufficient insulin secretion","C":"A defect in the Na+-glucose cotransporter that lowers its affinity for glucose","D":"Complete absence of GLUT1 transporters in the proximal tubule basolateral membrane","E":"An acquired reduction in functioning nephron mass that lowers the whole-kidney Tm"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because her Tm and threshold are both normal, the renal glucose transport machinery itself is intact; glucosuria in this setting therefore reflects a plasma glucose concentration high enough to exceed even a normal Tm and threshold, which points to insufficient insulin secretion (uncontrolled diabetes mellitus) as the cause rather than any renal defect, distinguishing this from a primary renal glucosuria in which the Tm itself would be abnormally low. A reduced functioning nephron mass would lower the whole-kidney Tm, but her measured Tm is normal.","boardPrep":"Normal Tm + glucosuria = the problem is upstream (too much filtered glucose from hyperglycemia); low Tm + glucosuria at normal blood glucose = the problem is the renal transporter itself (primary renal glucosuria).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — this exact reasoning (checking whether Tm itself is abnormal) is the standard way board vignettes distinguish diabetic glucosuria from primary renal glucosuria","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the kinetics of renal carrier-mediated transport systems, analyzing the concepts of transport maximum, threshold, and splay in saturable reabsorptive pathways (utilizing the glucose titration curve) versus saturable secretory pathways (utilizing the para-aminohippuric acid titration curve).","batch":2,"stem":"Two patients are found to have glucosuria on routine urinalysis at a health fair. Patient A, a 58-year-old man, has a fasting plasma glucose of 260 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL) and a normal renal transport maximum (Tm) for glucose. Patient B, a 22-year-old woman, has a fasting plasma glucose of 84 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL) and an abnormally low renal Tm for glucose. Both have a serum creatinine of 0.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL) and no other urinary abnormalities. Which patient's glucosuria reflects a primary renal transport disorder?","choices":{"A":"Patient A, because an elevated plasma glucose concentration indicates a renal transport disorder","B":"Both patients have a primary renal transport disorder, since both have glucosuria","C":"Neither patient has a renal transport disorder, since glucosuria is not inherently pathologic","D":"Patient B, since glucosuria occurs without any elevation in plasma glucose","E":"Patient A, because Tm values become clinically meaningful specifically when plasma glucose is also elevated"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Patient B has glucosuria at a normal plasma glucose with a measured low Tm, which identifies a defect in the renal glucose transport system itself (primary renal glucosuria). Patient A has a normal Tm; at 260 mg/dL his plasma glucose is above the renal threshold of about 200 mg/dL, so the nephrons with the lowest Tm begin to spill glucose even though whole-kidney Tm (reached near 350 mg/dL) is not yet saturated. His glucosuria therefore reflects an increased filtered load from hyperglycemia, as in diabetes mellitus, rather than a transporter defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: read plasma glucose together with Tm: glucosuria with a normal Tm and high plasma glucose is a filtered-load problem, while glucosuria with a low Tm at normal plasma glucose is a transporter problem. COMLEX: pregnancy and SGLT2 inhibitors also cause glucosuria at normal plasma glucose.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — primary renal glucosuria is a benign, isolated proximal tubule transport abnormality and should not be mistaken for diabetes mellitus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the kinetics of renal carrier-mediated transport systems, analyzing the concepts of transport maximum, threshold, and splay in saturable reabsorptive pathways (utilizing the glucose titration curve) versus saturable secretory pathways (utilizing the para-aminohippuric acid titration curve).","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man volunteers for a glucose titration study. He is healthy, with a blood pressure of 118/74 mm Hg, a fasting plasma glucose of 86 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), and a serum creatinine of 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). During a stepwise glucose infusion, glucose first appears in his urine at a plasma concentration about twice the upper limit of normal, but reabsorption does not plateau at its maximum until the plasma concentration is considerably higher. Which factor best explains the gradual approach to the transport maximum in his titration curve?","choices":{"A":"Because nephron-to-nephron heterogeneity in Tm values naturally contributes to the resulting splay","B":"Because glucose reabsorption capacity is randomly redistributed among nephrons on a frequent rotating basis","C":"Because glucose reabsorption is concentrated in a small, fixed subset of nephrons, with the remaining nephrons contributing very little","D":"Because whole-kidney Tm is calculated by simply summing each nephron's maximum urine flow rate","E":"Because nephron heterogeneity affects PAH secretion but has no analogous effect on glucose reabsorption"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Individual nephrons vary somewhat in their own individual Tm for glucose; because the measured whole-kidney Tm is effectively an average of many individual nephron Tm values, some nephrons begin spilling glucose into the urine before others reach their own Tm, which is the second explanation (alongside low cotransporter affinity near saturation) for why splay occurs on the whole-kidney titration curve.","boardPrep":"Splay has two causes: (1) low-affinity cotransporters near Tm, and (2) nephron-to-nephron heterogeneity in individual Tm values averaging out to a smeared whole-kidney curve.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — this nephron heterogeneity concept is a favorite \"why\" question following any glucose titration curve recall question","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the passive transport of urea across the nephron, analyzing how water reabsorption establishes the concentration gradients that drive simple urea diffusion, and how antidiuretic hormone dynamically regulates facilitated urea transport (via UT1 transporters) in the inner medullary collecting duct.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old man is evaluated for passing large volumes of urine 3 days after a severe head injury from a motorcycle crash. Urine output is 8 L/day. Blood pressure is 122/80 mm Hg. Serum sodium is 150 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), serum osmolality is 308 mOsm/kg H2O (reference range: 275–295 mOsm/kg H2O), and urine osmolality is 90 mOsm/kg H2O (reference range: 50–1,200 mOsm/kg H2O). His circulating ADH concentration is essentially undetectable. Compared with a healthy person with normal ADH levels, what is the expected effect on the fraction of his filtered urea that is excreted?","choices":{"A":"A smaller fraction of filtered urea is excreted, since ADH absence substantially increases proximal tubule urea reabsorption capacity","B":"The fraction of filtered urea excreted is unchanged, since urea transport along the nephron is ADH-independent","C":"A larger fraction of filtered urea is excreted, driven by disrupted proximal tubule reabsorption of urea","D":"A larger fraction of filtered urea is excreted, since UT1-mediated reabsorption in the inner medullary collecting duct is reduced","E":"A smaller fraction of filtered urea is excreted, since thin descending limb secretion is abolished without ADH"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Without ADH, UT1 in the inner medullary collecting duct is not upregulated, so facilitated urea reabsorption at this final segment is markedly reduced compared to the ADH-present state; because less urea is reabsorbed at this last opportunity, a larger fraction of the originally filtered urea remains in the tubular fluid and is excreted, rather than the approximately 40% seen when ADH is present and UT1 is active.","boardPrep":"No ADH = no UT1 upregulation = less urea reabsorbed in the inner medullary collecting duct = more urea excreted and less urea recycled into the inner medulla, which weakens the medullary gradient.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — this ADH-dependent urea handling is distinct from, but parallel to, ADH's water-permeability effect via aquaporin-2 in the same nephron segment","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the passive transport of urea across the nephron, analyzing how water reabsorption establishes the concentration gradients that drive simple urea diffusion, and how antidiuretic hormone dynamically regulates facilitated urea transport (via UT1 transporters) in the inner medullary collecting duct.","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old man comes to the clinic after 14 hours without fluids while preparing for a medical procedure. He feels well apart from thirst. Blood pressure is 118/76 mm Hg. Serum sodium is 144 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and urine osmolality is 1,050 mOsm/kg H2O (reference range: 50–1,200 mOsm/kg H2O). What happens to urea within the lumen of his late distal tubules and cortical and outer medullary collecting ducts?","choices":{"A":"Urea becomes progressively more concentrated in the tubular fluid as its volume shrinks around it","B":"Urea impermeability prevents any further change in urea concentration in these segments","C":"Urea is actively degraded by these segments before reaching the inner medullary collecting duct","D":"Urea keeps diffusing passively out of these segments in step with the water that is being reabsorbed","E":"Urea impermeability causes these segments to secrete additional urea into the lumen"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because these segments are impermeable to urea but, in the presence of ADH, remain permeable to water (which is reabsorbed), urea is \"left behind\" as water leaves the tubular fluid, progressively concentrating the urea that remains in the lumen; this sets up a high luminal urea concentration entering the inner medullary collecting duct, where UT1 can then act on that already-concentrated urea.","boardPrep":"Urea-impermeable-but-water-permeable segments concentrate urea by exclusion, priming it for the UT1-mediated reabsorption that follows in the inner medullary collecting duct.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — this concentrating step is why UT1's downstream reabsorption can be so effective despite acting on only one nephron segment","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate the passive transport of urea across the nephron, analyzing how water reabsorption establishes the concentration gradients that drive simple urea diffusion, and how antidiuretic hormone dynamically regulates facilitated urea transport (via UT1 transporters) in the inner medullary collecting duct.","batch":2,"stem":"Urea reabsorbed via UT1 into the inner medullary interstitium is described as contributing to \"urea recycling.\" What does this term most directly refer to?","choices":{"A":"Urea is chemically converted into a different nitrogenous waste product and then re-excreted","B":"This urea moves from the medulla back through the thin descending limb to begin another loop pass","C":"Urea recycling refers to hepatic reuptake and reprocessing of urea excreted in the urine","D":"Urea recycling describes glomerular refiltration of urea that bypassed initial filtration","E":"Urea recycling refers to urea being reabsorbed and retained in the medulla without re-entering the nephron"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Urea reabsorbed by UT1 into the inner medullary interstitium raises the local interstitial urea concentration, which then drives urea back into the thin descending limb of the loop of Henle (which is permeable to urea via secretion), allowing the same urea molecules to re-enter the tubular fluid and be processed again; this cycling of urea between the interstitium and the descending limb is what \"urea recycling\" describes, and it contributes to the corticopapillary osmotic gradient.","boardPrep":"Urea recycling = interstitium to descending limb to lumen to eventual re-reabsorption downstream — a closed loop that helps build and maintain the medullary concentration gradient.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — this recycling loop is a key contributor to the countercurrent multiplication mechanism responsible for the kidney's ability to concentrate urine","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl10_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the bidirectional transport mechanics (reabsorption, secretion, and post-secretory reabsorption) of uric acid in the proximal tubule, predicting how probenecid and varying doses of salicylates pharmacologically alter uric acid excretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with gout that is well controlled on allopurinol is admitted with acute pericarditis. He has sharp chest pain that improves when he leans forward, and a friction rub is heard. Temperature is 37.9°C (100.2°F), and blood pressure is 132/80 mm Hg. Serum uric acid is 6.1 mg/dL (reference range: 3.0–8.2 mg/dL), and creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). He is started on aspirin at a total dose of 5 g/day. What is the expected effect of aspirin at this dose on his renal uric acid excretion?","choices":{"A":"High-dose aspirin increases uric acid excretion by inhibiting its reabsorption","B":"High-dose aspirin decreases uric acid excretion by inhibiting its secretion","C":"High-dose aspirin leaves uric acid excretion unchanged because its two effects cancel","D":"High-dose aspirin decreases uric acid excretion by increasing its reabsorption","E":"High-dose aspirin increases uric acid excretion by increasing its glomerular filtration"},"correct":"A","explanation":"At high doses (above about 4 g/day), salicylate inhibits URAT1-mediated urate reabsorption as well as urate secretion, and because reabsorption is the larger process the net effect is uricosuric. Low-dose aspirin does the opposite: it blocks urate secretion while leaving reabsorption intact, so it decreases uric acid excretion. Salicylate does not increase urate reabsorption or change urate filtration, and its two effects do not cancel at 5 g/day. Allopurinol lowers urate production and does not act on renal urate transport, so it does not alter this effect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: salicylate's effect on urate depends on dose: low dose (1–2 g/day) blocks secretion and raises serum uric acid, while high dose (above about 4 g/day) also blocks URAT1 reabsorption and is uricosuric (course notes; Katzung gives urate retention at ≤2.6 g/day and a uricosuric effect above 3.6 g/day). COMLEX: salicylate at 1.5 g/day or more blunts probenecid's uricosuric effect, so the two are not combined.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — salicylate's dose-dependent switch from antiuricosuric to uricosuric, set by which urate transport step it blocks","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the bidirectional transport mechanics (reabsorption, secretion, and post-secretory reabsorption) of uric acid in the proximal tubule, predicting how probenecid and varying doses of salicylates pharmacologically alter uric acid excretion.","batch":2,"stem":"Two 50-year-old men with hyperuricemia and normal kidney function, each with a serum creatinine of 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), are enrolled in a trial of two investigational drugs. Each has a serum uric acid of 9.4 mg/dL (reference range: 3.0–8.2 mg/dL). Drug X, given to Patient X, selectively blocks only URAT1-mediated reabsorption of uric acid in the proximal tubule; Drug Y, given to Patient Y, selectively blocks only the secretory step. Each drug leaves the other transport steps intact. Which drug is expected to lower serum uric acid?","choices":{"A":"Drug Y, since secretion contributes more to net uric acid handling than reabsorption does at baseline","B":"Both drugs lower serum uric acid to the same degree, since Drug X and Drug Y each remove one transport step","C":"Drug X, since removing reabsorption eliminates the dominant of the two opposing transport processes","D":"Neither drug changes serum uric acid, because the remaining unblocked step compensates for the loss","E":"Drug Y, since blocking secretion lowers the filtered load of uric acid"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Baseline uric acid reabsorption (about 99% of the filtered load) is quantitatively far greater than baseline secretion, so a drug that selectively blocks reabsorption (Drug X) removes the larger of the two opposing processes, sharply increasing the fraction of filtered uric acid that escapes reabsorption and is excreted; a drug that selectively blocks secretion instead (Drug Y) removes only the smaller process and would, if anything, tend to raise serum uric acid by leaving reabsorption unopposed, the same logic that makes low-dose salicylate antiuricosuric.","boardPrep":"Reabsorption is the dominant baseline process (~99%), so blocking it (Drug X, like probenecid) is uricosuric; blocking only secretion (Drug Y, like low-dose aspirin) leaves the dominant reabsorption process unopposed and raises uric acid instead.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — this same reabsorption-dominates-secretion logic is what explains probenecid's net uricosuric effect despite blocking both transport directions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Deconstruct the bidirectional transport mechanics (reabsorption, secretion, and post-secretory reabsorption) of uric acid in the proximal tubule, predicting how probenecid and varying doses of salicylates pharmacologically alter uric acid excretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man comes to the clinic 2 weeks after his first attack of gout, which affected the first metatarsophalangeal joint and resolved with treatment. Blood pressure is 136/84 mm Hg. Serum uric acid is 8.9 mg/dL (reference range: 3.0–8.2 mg/dL), creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and his 24-hour urinary uric acid excretion is 250 mg (reference range: 250–750 mg/24 h). Which feature of renal uric acid handling distinguishes it from glucose and para-aminohippuric acid?","choices":{"A":"Uric acid is unique among nephron solutes in being neither filtered nor reabsorbed","B":"Uric acid is reabsorbed in the distal convoluted tubule under aldosterone control","C":"Uric acid is secreted in the descending limb of Henle by passive diffusion","D":"Uric acid bypasses glomerular filtration and enters the nephron tubule through secretion","E":"Uric acid undergoes both reabsorption and secretion within the same proximal tubule segment"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Glucose is reabsorbed and PAH is secreted, but uric acid undergoes reabsorption, then secretion, then reabsorption again, all within the proximal tubule, making its handling bidirectional and sequential within a single nephron segment, a pattern not seen with the other classic titration-curve examples.","boardPrep":"Uric acid moves in both directions within one segment — reabsorbed, then secreted, then reabsorbed again, all in the proximal tubule, netting out to about 10% excreted.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 3 — recognizing this bidirectional pattern is what makes uric acid pharmacology (probenecid, salicylates) more complex than glucose or PAH pharmacology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the physical principles of nonionic diffusion and pH trapping to predict the partition of weak acids and weak bases across renal tubular cell membranes, formulating a clinical rationale for manipulating urine pH (alkalinization or acidification) to accelerate the clearance of toxic substances in medical emergencies.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department 4 hours after intentionally ingesting a bottle of aspirin tablets. She has ringing in her ears, nausea, and rapid breathing. Respirations are 30/min, pulse is 112/min, and blood pressure is 118/74 mm Hg. Arterial blood gas analysis shows a pH of 7.46 (reference range: 7.35–7.45), Pco2 of 22 mm Hg (reference range: 35–45 mm Hg), and bicarbonate of 15 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Her serum salicylate concentration is 82 mg/dL (reference range for therapeutic use: 15–30 mg/dL). Based on the principle of nonionic diffusion, which intervention best accelerates her salicylate clearance?","choices":{"A":"Administer intravenous ammonium chloride to acidify the urine, trapping the charged HA form for excretion","B":"Administer intravenous sodium bicarbonate to alkalinize the urine, trapping the charged A- form for excretion","C":"Administer a loop diuretic, since raising the urine flow rate is the main factor that drives salicylate clearance","D":"Administer intravenous probenecid to block salicylate reabsorption in the proximal tubule","E":"Administer intravenous calcium gluconate to precipitate salicylate directly in the renal tubule"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Salicylate is a weak acid, so alkalinizing the urine (with intravenous sodium bicarbonate) shifts the equilibrium toward the charged A- form, which cannot diffuse back into the blood across tubular cells and is therefore trapped in the urine for excretion, directly increasing salicylate clearance through the nonionic diffusion/pH-trapping mechanism.","boardPrep":"Salicylate overdose treatment: IV sodium bicarbonate to alkalinize the urine — a classic emergency medicine application of the nonionic diffusion principle, distinct from bicarbonate's separate role in correcting any coexisting acidemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — this is one of the most frequently tested direct clinical applications in this entire SDL, bridging basic transport physiology to emergency toxicology management","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the physical principles of nonionic diffusion and pH trapping to predict the partition of weak acids and weak bases across renal tubular cell membranes, formulating a clinical rationale for manipulating urine pH (alkalinization or acidification) to accelerate the clearance of toxic substances in medical emergencies.","batch":2,"stem":"A 23-year-old man is brought to the emergency department by friends because of agitation and chest pain after taking a large number of pills at a party. Temperature is 38.4°C (101.1°F), pulse is 138/min, and blood pressure is 176/102 mm Hg. His pupils are dilated, and he is diaphoretic. Serum creatinine is 1.1 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and a urine drug screen is positive for amphetamines. Amphetamine is a weak base. Which intervention would be expected to accelerate its renal clearance?","choices":{"A":"Acidifying the urine shifts amphetamine into its charged BH+ form, trapping it for excretion","B":"Alkalinizing the urine with intravenous sodium bicarbonate would trap it for excretion","C":"Neither acidifying nor alkalinizing the urine changes amphetamine clearance","D":"Amphetamine trapping depends on blood pH, so systemic blood acidification would be the target intervention","E":"Blocking his proximal organic cation secretory transporter would leave more drug in the lumen to trap"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Amphetamine, as a weak base, exists predominantly in its charged BH+ form at acidic pH; acidifying the urine therefore favors this charged form, which cannot diffuse back into the blood and is trapped for excretion, increasing amphetamine clearance, the mirror image of the alkalinization strategy used for the weak acid salicylate. Blocking the proximal organic base secretory pathway would put less amphetamine into the tubular fluid, slowing rather than speeding its clearance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a weak base (amphetamine, PCP) is trapped by acidic urine and a weak acid (salicylate, phenobarbital) by alkaline urine, the same physiology in mirror image. COMLEX: clinically, urine acidification is not used for amphetamine or PCP because it can worsen kidney injury from rhabdomyolysis, whereas alkalinization for salicylate is standard.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — urine acidification for weak base overdose is generally not recommended clinically, unlike alkalinization for weak acids, but the underlying physiologic principle being tested is identical","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the physical principles of nonionic diffusion and pH trapping to predict the partition of weak acids and weak bases across renal tubular cell membranes, formulating a clinical rationale for manipulating urine pH (alkalinization or acidification) to accelerate the clearance of toxic substances in medical emergencies.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with gout who takes probenecid is admitted after an accidental overdose of a weak acid drug that is normally cleared mainly by secretion through the same proximal tubule organic anion transporter that secretes para-aminohippuric acid. Blood pressure is 128/78 mm Hg, and serum creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). The team alkalinizes his urine to trap the ionized drug in the urine. What happens to the total amount of drug captured in his urine by pH trapping, compared with a patient not taking probenecid?","choices":{"A":"It would be unaffected, since transporter blockade and pH trapping act independently","B":"It would rise, since transporter blockade increases the fraction of drug in the charged form","C":"It would reverse direction, so that acidic urine now trapped the drug for excretion","D":"It would depend on whether the inhibitor itself is a weak acid or a weak base","E":"It would fall, since less drug would reach the tubular fluid to be trapped in the first place"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Nonionic diffusion and pH trapping only matter for a drug that has already been secreted (or filtered) into the tubular fluid at a high enough concentration to create a gradient favoring back-diffusion in the first place; if the secretory transporter is blocked, less of the drug reaches the urine to begin with, so even a favorable urine pH has less total drug available to trap, diminishing the overall clearance benefit that pH manipulation alone could provide. The inhibitor's own acid or base character does not matter, since its effect is to block the drug's secretion into the lumen, and probenecid is a known blocker of this organic acid secretory transporter.","boardPrep":"pH trapping amplifies clearance of a drug that is already being secreted into the urine — it does not substitute for secretion, so blocking the secretory transporter leaves less drug in the lumen to trap.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — this integration point (secretion first, pH trapping second) is why urine alkalinization alone, without adequate urine flow and ongoing secretion, is not sufficient therapy for a severe overdose","isHighYield":true}],"11":[{"id":"sdl11_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze solute concentration profiles along the length of the nephron using tubular fluid-to-plasma (TF/P) ratio curves, deconstructing what a ratio of 1.0, less than 1.0, or greater than 1.0 represents regarding net water and solute transport.","batch":1,"stem":"At the end of the proximal tubule, [TF/P]Na+ remains approximately 1.0 even though roughly two-thirds of the filtered sodium has been reabsorbed by that point. What does this illustrate?","choices":{"A":"Sodium reabsorption occurs in exact, matched proportion to water reabsorption","B":"Sodium is not being reabsorbed in the proximal tubule even though filtered load is clearly being lost","C":"Water reabsorption is lagging well behind sodium reabsorption at this point in the nephron","D":"A [TF/P] ratio of 1.0 indicates that no filtration of sodium has yet occurred","E":"The measurement reflects a laboratory error, since reabsorbed solutes should lower the ratio"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A [TF/P]x ratio of 1.0 has two possible meanings: no transport has yet occurred, or solute and water are being reabsorbed in exact proportion (isosmotic reabsorption); for sodium in the proximal tubule, the second meaning applies, since water follows sodium reabsorption closely enough that the luminal sodium concentration stays equal to plasma even as the absolute amount of sodium falls.","boardPrep":"A [TF/P] ratio of 1.0 does not always mean \"nothing happened\" — for isosmotically handled solutes like proximal tubule sodium, it means solute and water left the lumen together.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — this same isosmotic-reabsorption logic is why [TF/P]Na+ stays flat across the entire proximal tubule despite substantial sodium reabsorption","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze solute concentration profiles along the length of the nephron using tubular fluid-to-plasma (TF/P) ratio curves, deconstructing what a ratio of 1.0, less than 1.0, or greater than 1.0 represents regarding net water and solute transport.","batch":1,"stem":"In the early proximal tubule, [TF/P]Cl- rises above 1.0 while [TF/P]HCO3- falls below 1.0. What does this divergence represent?","choices":{"A":"Chloride is being actively secreted into the lumen from the peritubular capillaries at this segment","B":"Bicarbonate is being secreted into the lumen while chloride is simultaneously reabsorbed","C":"Neither solute is undergoing any net transport, and the divergence simply reflects measurement variability","D":"Water reabsorption has stopped at this point in the proximal tubule","E":"Bicarbonate is being reabsorbed preferentially relative to water at this point"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In the early proximal tubule, sodium is reabsorbed preferentially with bicarbonate (via NHE3-driven bicarbonate reabsorption), so bicarbonate leaves the lumen faster than water does, dropping [TF/P]HCO3- well below 1.0; chloride, which is not preferentially reabsorbed in this segment, is left behind as water leaves, concentrating it in the lumen and raising [TF/P]Cl- above 1.0. Ratios moving away from 1.0 in opposite directions reflect real, opposite net handling of the two anions relative to water, not measurement noise.","boardPrep":"Early proximal tubule: HCO3- ratio falls (reabsorbed faster than water), Cl- ratio rises (left behind) — this divergence is what builds the high luminal chloride concentration used downstream in the late proximal tubule.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — this same chloride-concentrating step is the direct setup for the late proximal tubule's NaCl paracellular reabsorption mechanism","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze solute concentration profiles along the length of the nephron using tubular fluid-to-plasma (TF/P) ratio curves, deconstructing what a ratio of 1.0, less than 1.0, or greater than 1.0 represents regarding net water and solute transport.","batch":1,"stem":"Tubular fluid is sampled at a point along the nephron and [TF/P]inulin is measured at 3.0. What fraction of the originally filtered water has been reabsorbed at this point?","choices":{"A":"About 33% of the filtered water","B":"About 75% of the filtered water","C":"About 67% of the filtered water","D":"More than 99% of the filtered water","E":"Very little of the filtered water, since inulin is not reabsorbed"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Because inulin is freely filtered but never reabsorbed or secreted, its rising luminal concentration reflects only water removal; the fraction of filtered water reabsorbed equals 1 minus 1 divided by [TF/P]inulin, so at [TF/P]inulin = 3.0, the fraction reabsorbed is 1 - 1/3 = 2/3, or approximately 67%, consistent with the proximal tubule's characteristic isosmotic reabsorption of about two-thirds of the filtered load.","boardPrep":"Fraction of filtered water reabsorbed = 1 - 1/[TF/P]inulin — memorize this formula, since [TF/P]inulin = 3.0 at the end of a normal proximal tubule is a classic exam value.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — this same formula, applied to [TF/P]inulin = 125 at the end of the collecting duct during antidiuresis, is how overall urinary concentrating ability gets quantified","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the secondary active transport mechanisms in the luminal membrane of the early proximal convoluted tubule, evaluating how the basolateral sodium-potassium ATPase drives apical sodium cotransport (including SGLT2 and sodium-amino acid carriers) and the sodium-hydrogen exchanger (NHE3).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the ultimate energy source that powers apical SGLT2-mediated glucose entry into early proximal tubule cells?","choices":{"A":"SGLT2 itself directly hydrolyzes ATP to power both sodium and glucose transport across the membrane","B":"Basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase because it maintains the sodium gradient that continuously drives SGLT2","C":"A separate apical H+-ATPase provides the energy for glucose entry, independent of the sodium gradient","D":"Basolateral GLUT2 transporters actively pump glucose into the cell using hydrolyzed ATP directly","E":"Mitochondrial ATP synthase located within the brush border directly energizes SGLT2 without any pump"},"correct":"B","explanation":"SGLT2 itself performs no ATP hydrolysis; it is a secondary active transporter that uses the steep inward sodium electrochemical gradient established by the basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase (which primary-actively pumps 3 Na+ out and 2 K+ in) to move glucose into the cell against its own concentration gradient, coupling one glucose molecule to the downhill entry of one sodium ion.","boardPrep":"Every apical cotransporter in the early proximal tubule (SGLT2, Na+-amino acid, Na+-phosphate) is secondary active — the only primary active pump doing the actual ATP hydrolysis is the basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — this same basolateral-pump-as-ultimate-engine logic applies to every other proximal tubule apical cotransporter, not just SGLT2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the secondary active transport mechanisms in the luminal membrane of the early proximal convoluted tubule, evaluating how the basolateral sodium-potassium ATPase drives apical sodium cotransport (including SGLT2 and sodium-amino acid carriers) and the sodium-hydrogen exchanger (NHE3).","batch":1,"stem":"Why do the SGLT2 and Na+-amino acid cotransporters generate a lumen-negative transepithelial potential difference in the early proximal tubule?","choices":{"A":"They cotransport chloride alongside sodium, making the lumen electronegative from chloride loss","B":"They pump negative ions into the lumen as a byproduct of glucose and amino acid reabsorption","C":"They bring net positive charge into the cell without any accompanying matched anion","D":"They are electroneutral exchangers that have no effect on transepithelial voltage","E":"They hyperpolarize the peritubular capillary blood while leaving the tubular lumen's potential unaffected"},"correct":"C","explanation":"SGLT2 and the Na+-amino acid cotransporters carry a sodium cation into the cell together with an uncharged organic solute (glucose or an amino acid), so no anion accompanies the sodium out of the lumen; this net movement of positive charge out of the lumen without a matched anion leaves the lumen relatively negative, generating the characteristic -4 mV lumen-negative potential difference of the early proximal tubule. They are electrogenic, not electroneutral, which is exactly why they create the lumen-negative potential.","boardPrep":"Early proximal tubule = lumen-NEGATIVE (electrogenic Na+-organic solute cotransport); late proximal tubule = lumen-POSITIVE (Cl- diffusion potential) — a paired fact worth memorizing as opposites.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — this lumen-negative potential is the electrical mirror image of the lumen-positive potential generated downstream in the late proximal tubule","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the secondary active transport mechanisms in the luminal membrane of the early proximal convoluted tubule, evaluating how the basolateral sodium-potassium ATPase drives apical sodium cotransport (including SGLT2 and sodium-amino acid carriers) and the sodium-hydrogen exchanger (NHE3).","batch":1,"stem":"How does NHE3 (the apical Na+/H+ exchanger) in the early proximal tubule ultimately achieve bicarbonate reabsorption, given that NHE3 itself transports neither bicarbonate nor carbon dioxide?","choices":{"A":"Secreted H+ converts luminal bicarbonate to CO2 that diffuses into the cell and reforms bicarbonate","B":"NHE3 directly cotransports bicarbonate together with the hydrogen ion it secretes into the lumen","C":"NHE3 creates a lumen-positive potential that pulls bicarbonate across the tight junctions paracellularly","D":"NHE3 activates a separate basolateral bicarbonate channel that reabsorbs bicarbonate independently of any exchange","E":"NHE3 converts luminal bicarbonate directly into chloride via an intraluminal exchange reaction mechanism"},"correct":"A","explanation":"NHE3 exchanges one luminal Na+ entry for one H+ secretion; the secreted H+ combines with filtered luminal HCO3- to form H2CO3, which brush-border carbonic anhydrase rapidly converts to CO2 and H2O that diffuse into the cell, where intracellular carbonic anhydrase reconstitutes H+ (recycled back out via NHE3) and HCO3- (which exits the basolateral membrane into blood); this indirect, CO2-shuttling mechanism is how an exchanger that moves no bicarbonate at all accounts for the bulk of proximal bicarbonate reabsorption.","boardPrep":"NHE3 reabsorbs bicarbonate indirectly: H+ out, combines with HCO3- to make CO2, CO2 diffuses in, gets remade into HCO3- inside the cell — the net effect is bicarbonate reabsorption without NHE3 ever touching a bicarbonate ion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — this same CO2-shuttling logic is why carbonic anhydrase inhibitors (e.g., acetazolamide) block proximal bicarbonate reabsorption even though they target neither NHE3 nor a bicarbonate transporter directly","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Contrast the cellular and paracellular driving forces for sodium chloride reabsorption in the late proximal convoluted tubule, explaining how early proximal bicarbonate reabsorption establishes the high luminal chloride concentration gradient that drives late paracellular transport.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does tubular fluid entering the late proximal tubule have an elevated chloride concentration of approximately 140 mEq/L, compared to about 105-108 mEq/L in peritubular blood?","choices":{"A":"Chloride is actively secreted into the tubular lumen throughout the early proximal tubule","B":"Preferential early proximal reabsorption of bicarbonate with sodium leaves chloride behind in the lumen","C":"The early proximal tubule actively synthesizes additional chloride ions from bicarbonate","D":"Chloride passes freely between lumen and interstitium and equals plasma throughout the tubule","E":"Aldosterone acts on the proximal tubule to reabsorb bicarbonate in exchange for chloride secretion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because early proximal sodium reabsorption is preferentially coupled to bicarbonate, glucose, and amino acids rather than chloride, and water follows these reabsorbed solutes isosmotically, chloride is left behind and becomes progressively concentrated in the remaining tubular fluid; by the start of the late proximal tubule, this concentrating effect raises luminal chloride to roughly 140 mEq/L, well above the peritubular concentration.","boardPrep":"The high luminal chloride entering the late proximal tubule is not secreted — it is left behind as bicarbonate, glucose, and amino acids (and the water that follows them) exit the lumen upstream.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — this concentrated luminal chloride is the entire driving force for both the paracellular and cellular NaCl reabsorption mechanisms that follow in the late proximal tubule","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Contrast the cellular and paracellular driving forces for sodium chloride reabsorption in the late proximal convoluted tubule, explaining how early proximal bicarbonate reabsorption establishes the high luminal chloride concentration gradient that drives late paracellular transport.","batch":1,"stem":"What generates the lumen-positive transepithelial potential difference characteristic of the late proximal tubule?","choices":{"A":"Passive paracellular diffusion of chloride down its concentration gradient out of the lumen","B":"Active secretion of sodium into the lumen through a dedicated basolateral sodium channel","C":"The same electrogenic Na+-glucose cotransport mechanism used in the early proximal tubule","D":"A dedicated apical proton pump that actively acidifies and electropositively charges the lumen","E":"Bicarbonate diffusing paracellularly out of the lumen, since very little remains by this point in the tubule"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The high luminal chloride concentration established upstream drives passive paracellular diffusion of negatively charged chloride ions through the tight junctions into the peritubular interstitium; as negative charge leaves the lumen, the lumen becomes relatively electropositive, generating the characteristic +4 mV lumen-positive potential difference that then drives passive paracellular sodium reabsorption to preserve electroneutrality.","boardPrep":"Late proximal tubule lumen-positive potential comes from chloride LEAVING the lumen (not sodium entering it) — the resulting positive lumen then repels sodium out through the tight junctions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — this same lumen-positive, chloride-diffusion-driven mechanism recurs in other nephron segments wherever a high luminal chloride gradient exists","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Contrast the cellular and paracellular driving forces for sodium chloride reabsorption in the late proximal convoluted tubule, explaining how early proximal bicarbonate reabsorption establishes the high luminal chloride concentration gradient that drives late paracellular transport.","batch":1,"stem":"In the cellular (transcellular) pathway for late proximal NaCl reabsorption, what is the role of the apical Cl-/formate exchanger operating in parallel with NHE3?","choices":{"A":"It directly cotransports chloride together with sodium across the apical membrane in a single step","B":"It actively secretes formate into the blood to generate the electrochemical gradient for chloride entry","C":"It converts luminal chloride into bicarbonate before allowing its entry into the cell","D":"It allows luminal chloride to enter the cell in exchange for intracellular formate","E":"It operates independently of NHE3, using its own ATP-hydrolysis-driven mechanism"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The apical Cl-/formate (or oxalate) exchanger uses the steep luminal-to-cell chloride gradient to drive chloride entry into the cell in exchange for intracellular formate leaving into the lumen; the secreted formate combines with H+ (also secreted by the parallel NHE3) to form uncharged formic acid, which diffuses passively back into the cell to be recycled, so the net transcellular result is one Na+ (via NHE3) and one Cl- (via the anion exchanger) entering the cell together.","boardPrep":"The Cl-/formate exchanger and NHE3 work as a parallel pair: NHE3 brings in Na+/sends out H+, the anion exchanger brings in Cl-/sends out formate, and the secreted H+ and formate recombine and diffuse back in to keep the whole cycle going.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — this same parallel-exchanger strategy (two countertransporters sharing a recycled neutral intermediate) is a recurring transport-physiology pattern worth recognizing in other epithelia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Synthesize the cellular and hemodynamic mechanisms of bulk isosmotic fluid reabsorption, tracing how active basolateral sodium extrusion drives passive water movement into the lateral intercellular spaces, and how peritubular capillary Starling forces (high oncotic and low hydrostatic pressures) draw this fluid into the systemic circulation.","batch":1,"stem":"In the proximal tubule, which event is the primary driver of isosmotic fluid reabsorption, with water movement following as a consequence?","choices":{"A":"Active pumping of water through luminal channels, followed passively by the reabsorption of sodium","B":"Hydrostatic pressure inside the tubular lumen, which forces fluid and solute across the epithelium together","C":"A standing osmotic gradient across the epithelium, which pulls water out before any solute follows","D":"Secretion of osmotically active particles into the lumen, which then draws solute and water together into the cells","E":"Active reabsorption of solute, mainly sodium with its accompanying anions, followed passively by water"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Solute reabsorption is the primary event in isosmotic reabsorption. Sodium enters the cell across the luminal membrane by several mechanisms, and because the luminal membrane is permeable to water, water follows the solute to maintain isosmolarity. The major cation is sodium, accompanied by bicarbonate in the early proximal tubule and chloride in the late proximal tubule. Water follows solute passively, so it cannot be the initiating step.","boardPrep":"In the proximal tubule, solute reabsorption is the primary event and water follows passively, which is why the reabsorbed fluid stays isosmotic to plasma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 -> Obj 1 — this same solute-led coupling of water reabsorption is what glomerulotubular balance holds proportional to filtration","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Synthesize the cellular and hemodynamic mechanisms of bulk isosmotic fluid reabsorption, tracing how active basolateral sodium extrusion drives passive water movement into the lateral intercellular spaces, and how peritubular capillary Starling forces (high oncotic and low hydrostatic pressures) draw this fluid into the systemic circulation.","batch":1,"stem":"The Na+-K+ ATPase that drives fluid reabsorption in the proximal tubule sits in which membrane, and what happens to fluid in the adjacent lateral intercellular spaces as the pump extrudes sodium?","choices":{"A":"Luminal membrane; sodium is pumped into the lumen and water follows, so fluid is drained from the spaces","B":"Basolateral membrane; sodium is pumped into the cell and water follows, so fluid is removed from the spaces","C":"Tight junction; sodium is pumped across the junction and water follows, so fluid accumulates in the lumen","D":"Basolateral membrane; sodium is pumped out of the cell and water follows, so fluid accumulates in the spaces","E":"Capillary endothelium; sodium is pumped into the blood and water follows, so the spaces become hypotonic"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Na+-K+ ATPase is located in the basolateral membranes, where basal refers to the membrane facing the peritubular capillary and lateral refers to the membrane facing the lateral intercellular spaces. As sodium is pumped out of the cell, water again follows passively, so isosmotic fluid accumulates in the lateral intercellular spaces. Electron micrographs show these spaces widening when proximal tubule reabsorption increases. Pumping sodium into the cell or into the lumen would work against reabsorption.","boardPrep":"The proximal tubule Na+-K+ ATPase is basolateral, and fluid collects in the lateral intercellular spaces before the peritubular capillary takes it up.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 -> Obj 1 — this same solute-led coupling of water reabsorption is what glomerulotubular balance holds proportional to filtration","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Synthesize the cellular and hemodynamic mechanisms of bulk isosmotic fluid reabsorption, tracing how active basolateral sodium extrusion drives passive water movement into the lateral intercellular spaces, and how peritubular capillary Starling forces (high oncotic and low hydrostatic pressures) draw this fluid into the systemic circulation.","batch":1,"stem":"Isosmotic fluid that accumulates in the lateral intercellular spaces of the proximal tubule must still be reabsorbed into the circulation. Which force is the major driver of that uptake?","choices":{"A":"Capillary hydrostatic pressure pushing fluid from the spaces into the vessel","B":"The high oncotic pressure of the protein-rich peritubular capillary blood","C":"The lumen-positive transepithelial potential of the late proximal tubule","D":"Angiotensin II stimulation of apical sodium-hydrogen exchange in the cells","E":"Increased water permeability of the luminal membrane of the tubule cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The major Starling force driving uptake of isosmotic fluid from the lateral intercellular spaces is the high oncotic pressure of peritubular capillary blood. Glomerular filtration removes fluid but leaves protein behind, raising the protein concentration and oncotic pressure of blood that leaves the glomerulus and becomes peritubular capillary blood. Capillary hydrostatic pressure opposes reabsorption, while the transepithelial potential, angiotensin II, and luminal water permeability do not describe the force that moves the fluid into the vessel.","boardPrep":"Peritubular capillary oncotic pressure is the most important force pulling isosmotic fluid out of the proximal tubule spaces, and it is high because glomerular filtration concentrates plasma protein.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 -> Obj 1 — this same solute-led coupling of water reabsorption is what glomerulotubular balance holds proportional to filtration","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze solute concentration profiles along the length of the nephron using tubular fluid-to-plasma (TF/P) ratio curves, deconstructing what a ratio of 1.0, less than 1.0, or greater than 1.0 represents regarding net water and solute transport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man undergoes nephrectomy for a small renal tumor. He consents to a research study in which tubular fluid is sampled by micropuncture from the normal pole of the kidney before the renal artery is clamped. Preoperatively, his blood pressure was 128/80 mm Hg, and his serum creatinine was 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). During the study he receives an inulin infusion. At the end of the proximal tubule, the tubular fluid-to-plasma concentration ratio ([TF/P]) for an endogenous solute x is 0.4, and [TF/P]inulin at the same point is 3.0. What does the double ratio [TF/P]x / [TF/P]inulin reveal about solute x?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 40% of the filtered load of solute x remains in the tubular fluid, meaning about 60% has been reabsorbed","B":"Approximately 75% of the filtered load of solute x has been secreted into the tubular fluid beyond the filtered amount","C":"Approximately 13% of the filtered load of solute x remains in the tubular fluid, meaning about 87% has been reabsorbed","D":"Solute x is handled identically to inulin, since both ratios are being compared directly","E":"The double ratio cannot be calculated because the two ratios were measured at different absolute concentrations"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The double ratio [TF/P]x / [TF/P]inulin corrects the raw [TF/P]x for the effect of water reabsorption, directly yielding the fraction of the filtered load remaining in the tubular fluid; here that calculation gives 0.4 / 3.0 = 0.133, or about 13% remaining, meaning approximately 87% of the filtered load of solute x has been reabsorbed by this point in the nephron.","boardPrep":"The double ratio [TF/P]x / [TF/P]inulin directly gives the fraction of filtered load remaining — do not confuse the raw [TF/P]x value (0.4 here) with this corrected fraction (0.133 here); they are very different numbers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — this same double-ratio calculation, applied to sodium at the end of the proximal tubule, is what yields the classic 33%-remaining/67%-reabsorbed figure","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze solute concentration profiles along the length of the nephron using tubular fluid-to-plasma (TF/P) ratio curves, deconstructing what a ratio of 1.0, less than 1.0, or greater than 1.0 represents regarding net water and solute transport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman undergoes nephrectomy for a renal tumor and consents to a research study in which tubular fluid is sampled by micropuncture from the normal pole of the kidney. Preoperatively, her fasting plasma glucose was 88 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), and her serum creatinine was 0.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). By the midpoint of the proximal tubule, essentially all of her filtered glucose and amino acids have been reabsorbed, and their [TF/P] ratios approach zero. A medical student reviewing the data argues that water reabsorption at this point must also have essentially stopped. Why is this reasoning flawed?","choices":{"A":"A [TF/P] ratio approaching zero requires that water reabsorption at that point has also stopped","B":"A [TF/P] ratio approaching zero reflects near-complete solute reabsorption, independent of the water reabsorption rate","C":"Glucose and amino acids are reabsorbed by solvent drag, so their uptake stops whenever water flow stops","D":"Water reabsorption in the proximal tubule waits until organic solute reabsorption is essentially complete","E":"The student is correct, since a falling [TF/P] ratio for any solute indicates falling water reabsorption as well"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The [TF/P] ratio for a given solute reflects the balance between that solute's own reabsorption rate and water's reabsorption rate; when a solute like glucose is reabsorbed essentially completely and very early (avidly, relative to water), its ratio falls toward zero, but water reabsorption continues along the proximal tubule, coupled mainly to Na+ reabsorption; at normal plasma glucose almost no glucose is left in the lumen to hold water back.","boardPrep":"A falling [TF/P] ratio for one solute (like glucose reaching near-zero) tells you about that solute's reabsorption relative to water — it does not by itself tell you whether water reabsorption sped up, slowed down, or stayed the same.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — contrast this with [TF/P]inulin, which is the one ratio that DOES directly and exclusively track water reabsorption, precisely because inulin itself is never transported","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze solute concentration profiles along the length of the nephron using tubular fluid-to-plasma (TF/P) ratio curves, deconstructing what a ratio of 1.0, less than 1.0, or greater than 1.0 represents regarding net water and solute transport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man undergoes nephrectomy for a renal tumor and consents to a research study in which tubular fluid is sampled by micropuncture from the normal pole of the kidney. Preoperatively, his blood pressure was 132/82 mm Hg, and his serum creatinine was 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). At one sampling point, the tubular fluid-to-plasma concentration ratio for an endogenous solute y ([TF/P]y) is 1.5. Reabsorption of solute y that lags behind water reabsorption and net secretion of solute y would each produce this raw value. What additional information would best distinguish these two scenarios?","choices":{"A":"The [TF/P]inulin measurement taken from the same tubular fluid sample","B":"The plasma concentration of solute y measured at the same time as the tubular fluid sample","C":"The total urine flow rate collected over a full 24-hour period","D":"The molecular weight of solute y compared with the molecular weight of inulin","E":"The distance in millimeters from Bowman's space to the sampling point"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A raw [TF/P]x value greater than 1.0 is ambiguous between two mechanisms (solute reabsorption lagging behind water reabsorption, or true net secretion); calculating the double ratio [TF/P]x / [TF/P]inulin resolves this ambiguity, because a double ratio greater than 1.0 specifically indicates net secretion (more solute is present than could be explained by water removal alone), while a double ratio below 1.0 with a raw ratio above 1.0 indicates net reabsorption that lags behind water, and a double ratio of exactly 1.0 means no net transport. A 24-hour urine flow rate and the distance from Bowman's space do not measure how much water was removed before the sampling point, which is what the [TF/P]inulin gives.","boardPrep":"Whenever a raw [TF/P] ratio above 1.0 could mean either \"lagging reabsorption\" or \"secretion,\" the double ratio (correcting for [TF/P]inulin) is the tool that tells them apart.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — this same double-ratio resolution is the standard method for confirming true tubular secretion of organic acids and bases at other nephron segments","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the secondary active transport mechanisms in the luminal membrane of the early proximal convoluted tubule, evaluating how the basolateral sodium-potassium ATPase drives apical sodium cotransport (including SGLT2 and sodium-amino acid carriers) and the sodium-hydrogen exchanger (NHE3).","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman has persistent glucosuria on routine urinalysis, with a fasting plasma glucose of 84 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL). Genetic testing shows a loss-of-function variant in the transporter that carries glucose out of early proximal tubule cells across the basolateral membrane; her luminal SGLT2 and basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase are normal. Which type of transport does the defective step normally use?","choices":{"A":"Primary active transport fueled directly by ATP hydrolysis","B":"Secondary active transport coupled to sodium entry","C":"Countertransport of glucose in exchange for hydrogen ions","D":"Paracellular diffusion through the tight junctions","E":"Facilitated diffusion down its electrochemical gradient"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In the early proximal tubule, glucose enters the cell on luminal SGLT2 against its electrochemical gradient, powered by the downhill movement of Na+; it then leaves the cell into peritubular capillary blood by facilitated diffusion through GLUT1 and GLUT2, moving down its gradient with no energy required. Primary active transport describes the basolateral Na+/K+-ATPase that maintains the Na+ gradient, not the glucose exit step. Secondary active transport coupled to Na+ entry is the luminal SGLT2 step, which is normal in her. Glucose is not exchanged for H+; the one luminal exchanger in the early proximal tubule is the Na+-H+ exchanger. Glucose is reabsorbed through the cells rather than between them, so a paracellular route does not describe it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Early proximal glucose path: luminal SGLT2 (secondary active) → basolateral GLUT1/GLUT2 (facilitated diffusion); SGLT2 inhibitors block the luminal step and increase glucose excretion. COMLEX: Glucosuria with a normal plasma glucose points to a tubular transport defect rather than diabetes mellitus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 → Obj 1 — saturable glucose carriers set the transport maximum and splay","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the secondary active transport mechanisms in the luminal membrane of the early proximal convoluted tubule, evaluating how the basolateral sodium-potassium ATPase drives apical sodium cotransport (including SGLT2 and sodium-amino acid carriers) and the sodium-hydrogen exchanger (NHE3).","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy is brought to the clinic for evaluation of short stature; his height is below the 3rd percentile. He has no polyuria and no history of rickets. Blood pressure is 98/60 mm Hg. Serum sodium is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), potassium is 3.8 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), chloride is 112 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), bicarbonate is 16 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), glucose is 86 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), and creatinine is 0.4 mg/dL (reference range: 0.3–0.7 mg/dL). Urinalysis shows no glucose or protein, and urine amino acid excretion is normal. Genetic testing identifies a variant that selectively eliminates NHE3 function in the early proximal tubule, with SGLT2 and the Na+-amino acid cotransporters unaffected. What is the most direct expected consequence for his bicarbonate handling?","choices":{"A":"Bicarbonate reabsorption is unaffected, since SGLT2 and the Na+-amino acid cotransporters take over H+ secretion","B":"A substantial fraction of filtered bicarbonate escapes reabsorption, since NHE3 drives most of that pathway","C":"Bicarbonate reabsorption increases, since NHE3 normally opposes bicarbonate reabsorption","D":"Bicarbonate is instead secreted into the lumen in the absence of NHE3 activity","E":"Glucose and amino acid reabsorption are also lost, since early proximal transporters share a single underlying mechanism"},"correct":"B","explanation":"NHE3-driven H+ secretion is the mechanism that converts luminal bicarbonate to CO2 and water for reabsorption, accounting for the great majority of early proximal bicarbonate reabsorption; because SGLT2 and the Na+-amino acid cotransporters do not secrete H+ and so do not touch bicarbonate, they cannot compensate for NHE3 loss, and a substantial fraction of filtered bicarbonate would be expected to escape reabsorption and reach more distal segments.","boardPrep":"NHE3 loss causes bicarbonate wasting specifically, while leaving glucose and amino acid reabsorption intact, since those pathways are mechanistically independent of NHE3.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — this selective bicarbonate-wasting pattern mirrors the bicarbonate loss seen in proximal (type 2) renal tubular acidosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Deconstruct the secondary active transport mechanisms in the luminal membrane of the early proximal convoluted tubule, evaluating how the basolateral sodium-potassium ATPase drives apical sodium cotransport (including SGLT2 and sodium-amino acid carriers) and the sodium-hydrogen exchanger (NHE3).","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with type 2 diabetes mellitus comes to the clinic to discuss starting an SGLT2 inhibitor. Blood pressure is 134/82 mm Hg. Hemoglobin A1c is 8.1% (reference range: 4.0–5.6%), and serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). While explaining how the drug works, his physician notes that two early proximal tubule apical transporters, SGLT2 and NHE3, both depend on the inward sodium electrochemical gradient. Why does SGLT2 generate a lumen-negative potential while NHE3 does not, even though both use the same sodium gradient?","choices":{"A":"SGLT2 uses a different sodium gradient than NHE3, one generated by a separate basolateral pump","B":"NHE3 secretes a negative ion into the lumen that exactly cancels its own electrogenic sodium entry","C":"SGLT2 is confined to the early proximal tubule, before any electrical potential develops","D":"SGLT2 moves charge in unbalanced by any anion; NHE3 swaps one cation for another, staying electroneutral","E":"NHE3 carries a second Na+ ion with each H+, which doubles its charge movement"},"correct":"D","explanation":"SGLT2 is electrogenic because it moves one positively charged sodium ion into the cell together with one uncharged glucose molecule, with no anion entering the lumen to balance the charge loss, leaving the lumen relatively negative; NHE3, by contrast, exchanges one Na+ entering the cell for one H+ leaving the cell into the lumen, a one-for-one cation-for-cation swap that carries no net charge across the membrane and is therefore electroneutral, generating no transepithelial voltage on its own.","boardPrep":"Symport of an ion with an uncharged solute (SGLT2, Na+-amino acid) is electrogenic; antiport of a cation for another cation (NHE3) is electroneutral — the transport TYPE, not just the sodium gradient involved, determines whether a potential difference is generated.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — this electrogenic-versus-electroneutral distinction is exactly why the early proximal tubule is lumen-negative despite NHE3 handling the majority of bicarbonate reabsorption there","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Contrast the cellular and paracellular driving forces for sodium chloride reabsorption in the late proximal convoluted tubule, explaining how early proximal bicarbonate reabsorption establishes the high luminal chloride concentration gradient that drives late paracellular transport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman is enrolled in a phase 1 study of an investigational drug that selectively blocks NHE3 in the early proximal tubule, preventing the normal preferential bicarbonate reabsorption there, while leaving all late proximal tubule transporters intact. She is healthy, with a blood pressure of 114/72 mm Hg, a serum chloride of 103 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), and a serum creatinine of 0.7 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). What is the most direct expected effect of the drug on her late proximal NaCl reabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Late proximal NaCl reabsorption is impaired, because the high luminal chloride gradient driving it fails to develop","B":"Late proximal NaCl reabsorption increases, because more chloride remains available for the paracellular and cellular exchange mechanisms","C":"Late proximal NaCl reabsorption is unaffected, because the late proximal transporters generate their own independent chloride gradient","D":"Late proximal NaCl reabsorption shifts from a paracellular to a cellular mechanism","E":"Late proximal NaCl reabsorption fails through the cellular pathway, while the paracellular pathway stays intact"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The main driving force for late proximal NaCl reabsorption, both paracellular (chloride diffusion and the resulting lumen-positive potential) and cellular (the Cl-/formate exchanger), depends on the high luminal chloride concentration established upstream by preferential early proximal bicarbonate (not chloride) reabsorption; if NHE3-driven bicarbonate reabsorption is blocked, chloride is not concentrated as it normally would be, and both late proximal NaCl reabsorption mechanisms lose their driving gradient together.","boardPrep":"Late proximal NaCl reabsorption depends largely on early proximal bicarbonate handling: impair the preferential bicarbonate reabsorption upstream, and you impair NaCl reabsorption late.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — this early-to-late proximal tubule dependency is a favorite integration question linking Objective 2 and Objective 3 together","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Contrast the cellular and paracellular driving forces for sodium chloride reabsorption in the late proximal convoluted tubule, explaining how early proximal bicarbonate reabsorption establishes the high luminal chloride concentration gradient that drives late paracellular transport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man is enrolled in a phase 1 study of an investigational agent that makes the tight junctions between late proximal tubule cells completely impermeable to chloride, while the cellular Cl-/formate exchanger remains fully functional. He is healthy, with a blood pressure of 122/78 mm Hg, a serum sodium of 140 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and a serum creatinine of 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). In his late proximal tubules, both a paracellular pathway and a cellular pathway normally contribute to net NaCl reabsorption. What would be the most direct consequence of the agent?","choices":{"A":"NaCl reabsorption overall would stop completely because the two pathways are fully interdependent on each other","B":"The lumen-positive potential difference would become even larger from continued chloride accumulation","C":"The lumen-positive potential difference would be lost; transcellular NaCl reabsorption would continue","D":"The cellular Cl-/formate exchanger would compensate instead by secreting chloride paracellularly as well","E":"Paracellular Na+ reabsorption would rise to replace the lost chloride-driven flux"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The lumen-positive transepithelial potential difference is generated specifically by passive paracellular chloride diffusion out of the lumen; if the tight junctions became impermeable to chloride, this paracellular diffusion (and the potential difference it generates, along with the passive paracellular sodium reabsorption that potential drives) would be lost, but the cellular pathway, which depends on the apical Cl-/formate exchanger and NHE3 rather than paracellular permeability, could continue to reabsorb some NaCl transcellularly.","boardPrep":"The paracellular and cellular NaCl reabsorption pathways in the late proximal tubule are mechanistically independent of each other — losing one does not automatically abolish the other, even though both are driven by the same underlying chloride gradient.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — recognizing that these two pathways can be dissociated experimentally is what distinguishes a mechanistic understanding from simple memorization of \"NaCl is reabsorbed here\"","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Contrast the cellular and paracellular driving forces for sodium chloride reabsorption in the late proximal convoluted tubule, explaining how early proximal bicarbonate reabsorption establishes the high luminal chloride concentration gradient that drives late paracellular transport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman with chronic kidney disease asks her nephrologist how her kidneys reabsorb salt. Blood pressure is 130/80 mm Hg, and her serum creatinine is 1.6 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). He explains that in the late proximal tubule, much of the NaCl crosses the cells through NHE3 and an apical Cl-/formate exchanger. Why is this transcellular pathway described as parallel exchangers?","choices":{"A":"NHE3 and the Cl-/formate exchanger are physically fused into one transmembrane protein complex that moves both ions simultaneously","B":"NHE3 and the Cl-/formate exchanger function as two separate antiporters that happen to recombine byproducts","C":"The two exchangers alternate their activity in sequence, each becoming active while the other is quiet","D":"Just one of the two exchangers is active at any given moment, depending on the luminal pH","E":"The two exchangers depend on different basolateral pumps to sustain their respective gradients"},"correct":"B","explanation":"NHE3 and the apical Cl-/formate exchanger are distinct, independently operating transport proteins, each simply exchanging one luminal cation or anion for an intracellular counterpart; they are termed \"parallel\" because they function simultaneously and independently, and their combined net effect (one Na+ and one Cl- entering the cell) emerges only because their secreted byproducts, H+ and formate, happen to recombine in the lumen into neutral formic acid that diffuses back in to sustain both cycles, not because the two transporters are structurally linked.","boardPrep":"\"Parallel\" exchangers means two separate transporters running side by side, not one fused transporter — their apparent coordination comes entirely from the shared recycled formic acid intermediate.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — this parallel-exchanger-plus-recycled-intermediate strategy is a template physiologic mechanism worth recognizing whenever a \"coupled\" transport process turns out to be two independent exchangers","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Synthesize the cellular and hemodynamic mechanisms of bulk isosmotic fluid reabsorption, tracing how active basolateral sodium extrusion drives passive water movement into the lateral intercellular spaces, and how peritubular capillary Starling forces (high oncotic and low hydrostatic pressures) draw this fluid into the systemic circulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man undergoes nephrectomy for a renal tumor and consents to a research study of the normal pole of the kidney. Preoperatively, his blood pressure was 130/80 mm Hg, and his serum creatinine was 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). One biopsy of proximal tubules is taken under baseline conditions, and a second is taken after an infusion that markedly increases proximal tubule reabsorption. Both are examined by electron microscopy. Which change would be expected in the second specimen?","choices":{"A":"Narrowed lateral intercellular spaces, reflecting faster draining of fluid into the tubular lumen","B":"Collapsed tubular lumens, reflecting reduced filtration of fluid into Bowman space","C":"Thickened glomerular basement membranes, reflecting greater protein retention in the filtrate","D":"Flattened brush borders, reflecting reduced surface area for sodium entry from the lumen","E":"Widened lateral intercellular spaces, reflecting isosmotic fluid awaiting peritubular uptake"},"correct":"E","explanation":"As sodium is pumped out of the cell across the basolateral membrane and water follows, isosmotic fluid accumulates in the lateral intercellular spaces. Electron micrographs show the spaces actually widening when proximal tubule reabsorption increases, because more fluid is in transit toward the peritubular capillary, where Starling forces take it up. Narrowed spaces would suggest less reabsorption, and the other findings do not describe the route that reabsorbed fluid takes.","boardPrep":"Widened lateral intercellular spaces on electron microscopy mark active isosmotic reabsorption in the proximal tubule.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — glomerulotubular balance acts on this same peritubular uptake step to keep reabsorption proportional to filtration","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Synthesize the cellular and hemodynamic mechanisms of bulk isosmotic fluid reabsorption, tracing how active basolateral sodium extrusion drives passive water movement into the lateral intercellular spaces, and how peritubular capillary Starling forces (high oncotic and low hydrostatic pressures) draw this fluid into the systemic circulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man undergoes nephrectomy for a renal cell carcinoma. Preoperatively, his serum creatinine was 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). With his consent, proximal tubule segments are dissected from the normal pole of the kidney and perfused in vitro. Ouabain, an inhibitor of the Na+-K+ ATPase, is added to the bath, and net fluid reabsorption falls toward zero even though the luminal membrane remains freely permeable to water. Which statement best explains this result?","choices":{"A":"Peritubular oncotic pressure rises sharply and overcomes the driving force for reabsorption of fluid","B":"The tight junctions seal, so fluid becomes trapped within the lateral intercellular spaces","C":"Sodium is secreted back into the tubular fluid, which draws water out of the cells and into the lumen","D":"Without the pump, solute reabsorption fails, and water movement stops because it follows solute","E":"The same ATPase pumps water directly, so its inhibition immediately halts the movement of water"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Solute reabsorption is the primary event in isosmotic reabsorption: sodium enters across the luminal membrane and is pumped out across the basolateral membrane by the Na+-K+ ATPase, and water follows passively to keep the fluid isosmotic. Blocking the pump removes the sodium gradient that supports luminal sodium entry, so solute reabsorption falls and water, which follows solute, stops with it even though the luminal membrane remains permeable. Water is not itself pumped, and an isolated perfused tubule has no peritubular blood in which oncotic pressure could change.","boardPrep":"If the basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase is blocked, proximal fluid reabsorption stops because water depends on solute reabsorption, not the reverse.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 2 — the sodium gradient maintained by this same pump also powers the apical cotransporters of the early proximal tubule","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl11_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Synthesize the cellular and hemodynamic mechanisms of bulk isosmotic fluid reabsorption, tracing how active basolateral sodium extrusion drives passive water movement into the lateral intercellular spaces, and how peritubular capillary Starling forces (high oncotic and low hydrostatic pressures) draw this fluid into the systemic circulation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman undergoes nephrectomy for a renal tumor and consents to a micropuncture study of the normal pole of the kidney. Preoperatively, her serum electrolytes were within the reference ranges, and her creatinine was 0.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). The study shows that her proximal tubules reabsorb about two-thirds of the filtered load of water and salt. Quantitatively, which solutes account for most of the solute reabsorption that the water follows?","choices":{"A":"Sodium chloride in the late segment and sodium bicarbonate in the early segment","B":"Glucose and amino acids taken up through sodium-coupled cotransporters at the luminal membrane","C":"Phosphate, lactate, and citrate taken up through sodium-coupled carriers at the luminal membrane","D":"Potassium chloride and urea moving down their concentration gradients across the epithelium","E":"Calcium and magnesium salts moving through the paracellular pathway between neighboring cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Isosmotic reabsorption couples water to solute, and the major cation is sodium with bicarbonate as the main accompanying anion in the early proximal tubule and chloride in the late proximal tubule. Quantitatively, most of the solute reabsorbed by the proximal tubule is NaCl and NaHCO3. Glucose, amino acids, phosphate, lactate, and citrate are also reabsorbed but contribute far fewer osmoles. Calcium and magnesium are present in plasma at only about 1 mEq/L each, against about 142 mEq/L for sodium, so they add few osmoles even though some move paracellularly.","boardPrep":"Most of the solute behind proximal isosmotic water reabsorption is NaCl and NaHCO3, with glucose, amino acids, and the minor anions contributing much less.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — this bulk reabsorption of sodium and water is the quantity that glomerulotubular balance holds at a constant fraction","isHighYield":false}],"12":[{"id":"sdl12_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the protective feedback mechanism of glomerulotubular (GT) balance, explaining how it maintains a constant fractional proximal tubular reabsorption of 67 percent during spontaneous fluctuations in glomerular filtration rate.","batch":1,"stem":"GFR spontaneously increases by 1% with no change in renal plasma flow (RPF). Why does the proximal tubule end up reabsorbing 67% of this larger filtered sodium load rather than the same absolute amount it reabsorbed before?","choices":{"A":"The rise in filtration fraction concentrates peritubular capillary blood, raising oncotic pressure and driving more reabsorption","B":"The proximal tubule cells sense the higher luminal sodium concentration and increase transporter expression within seconds","C":"Angiotensin II is released in direct proportion to GFR and stimulates proximal Na-H exchange independent of Starling forces","D":"A rise in GFR lowers peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure directly, without any change in filtration fraction","E":"The macula densa signals the proximal tubule directly through paracrine mediators to raise its reabsorptive rate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"With RPF unchanged, a 1% rise in GFR raises the filtration fraction (GFR/RPF), meaning a greater-than-usual fraction of plasma water leaves glomerular capillary blood; this concentrates the remaining plasma proteins, raising the oncotic pressure of the blood that becomes peritubular capillary blood, and because peritubular oncotic pressure is the dominant Starling force driving proximal reabsorption, the higher oncotic pressure increases fractional reabsorption to match the higher filtered load.","boardPrep":"Glomerulotubular balance works primarily through the filtration fraction and peritubular oncotic pressure rather than direct tubular sensing of luminal solute; memorize the chain: GFR up -> filtration fraction up -> peritubular oncotic pressure up -> reabsorption up.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — this filtration-fraction-to-oncotic-pressure chain is the entire mechanistic basis of glomerulotubular balance","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the protective feedback mechanism of glomerulotubular (GT) balance, explaining how it maintains a constant fractional proximal tubular reabsorption of 67 percent during spontaneous fluctuations in glomerular filtration rate.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has a GFR of 180 L/day and a plasma sodium concentration of 140 mEq/L. If glomerulotubular balance were completely absent (i.e., the proximal tubule reabsorbed a fixed amount rather than a fixed fraction) and GFR spontaneously rose by 1%, approximately how much additional sodium would be excreted per day?","choices":{"A":"Approximately 25 mEq/day","B":"Approximately 1960 mEq/day","C":"Approximately 2520 mEq/day","D":"Approximately 140 mEq/day","E":"Approximately 252 mEq/day"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The filtered load of sodium equals GFR times plasma sodium concentration: 180 L/day x 140 mEq/L = 25,200 mEq/day; a 1% increase in this filtered load is 252 mEq/day, and without glomerulotubular balance correcting reabsorption to match, this entire extra amount would appear in the urine, which is a large loss relative to the body's total ECF sodium content of only about 1960 mEq. About 2520 mEq/day is a 10% rise in filtered load, about 25 mEq/day is a 0.1% rise, and 140 mEq/day is the plasma sodium concentration taken as a daily amount.","boardPrep":"Filtered load = GFR x plasma concentration — this calculation is exactly why a seemingly tiny 1% GFR fluctuation would be dangerous without glomerulotubular balance, since it represents a meaningful fraction of total body sodium.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — this exact arithmetic illustrates why glomerulotubular balance is described as a protective mechanism rather than a minor physiologic curiosity","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the protective feedback mechanism of glomerulotubular (GT) balance, explaining how it maintains a constant fractional proximal tubular reabsorption of 67 percent during spontaneous fluctuations in glomerular filtration rate.","batch":1,"stem":"Glomerulotubular balance maintains a constant fractional reabsorption of 67% in the proximal tubule. What is the key distinction between \"constant fraction\" and \"constant amount\" that makes this mechanism protective?","choices":{"A":"A constant amount would also scale the reabsorbed amount with whatever load is filtered, making the two descriptions functionally identical","B":"A constant fraction automatically scales the reabsorbed amount with whatever load is filtered","C":"A constant fraction means the reabsorbed amount stays the same as the filtered load changes","D":"A constant fraction and a constant amount alternate roles depending on whether GFR is rising or falling","E":"Neither a constant fraction nor a constant amount can protect the reabsorbed amount against changes in filtered sodium load"},"correct":"B","explanation":"If the proximal tubule reabsorbed a fixed absolute amount of sodium regardless of filtered load, then any rise in GFR would send the entire extra filtered sodium straight to the urine, and any fall in GFR could even make reabsorption exceed the filtered load; because glomerulotubular balance instead keeps reabsorption at a constant fraction (67%) of whatever load is filtered, reabsorption automatically rises when the filtered load rises and falls when it falls, keeping the absolute amount delivered to more distal segments (and ultimately excreted) relatively stable despite spontaneous GFR fluctuations.","boardPrep":"The core protective logic of glomerulotubular balance: fixed fraction scales with load, fixed amount does not — this distinction is the entire conceptual point of the mechanism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — this same fixed-fraction logic is why proximal tubule reabsorption is described as \"load-dependent\" throughout renal physiology","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate how extracellular fluid (ECF) volume expansion (such as isotonic saline infusion) alters peritubular capillary Starling forces (diluting oncotic pressure and raising hydrostatic pressure) to produce decreased fractional reabsorption and luminal back-leak of fluid.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient receives a large infusion of isotonic saline. Which pair of peritubular capillary Starling force changes results, and what proximal tubule effect follows?","choices":{"A":"Oncotic pressure rises because the saline preferentially dilutes red blood cells, leaving proximal tubule reabsorption unaffected","B":"The rise in intravascular volume raises glomerular capillary pressure, leaving reabsorption in the proximal tubule unaffected","C":"Both Starling forces shift to favor increased reabsorption in the proximal tubule, since expanded plasma volume raises oncotic pressure","D":"Oncotic pressure falls and hydrostatic pressure rises, together decreasing fractional reabsorption in the proximal tubule","E":"Neither Starling force changes, so reabsorption in the proximal tubule stays fixed since isotonic saline does not alter plasma protein concentration"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Isotonic saline dilutes plasma proteins without adding protein, lowering peritubular capillary oncotic pressure, and expands intravascular volume, raising peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure; because oncotic pressure normally favors reabsorption and hydrostatic pressure normally opposes it, a fall in the former together with a rise in the latter both push in the same direction, reducing the net force favoring reabsorption and decreasing fractional proximal reabsorption of isosmotic fluid.","boardPrep":"ECF volume expansion: peritubular oncotic pressure down, peritubular hydrostatic pressure up, both changes point the same direction — toward LESS reabsorption.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — this pair of Starling force changes is the direct mechanistic explanation for why isotonic saline infusion promotes natriuresis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate how extracellular fluid (ECF) volume expansion (such as isotonic saline infusion) alters peritubular capillary Starling forces (diluting oncotic pressure and raising hydrostatic pressure) to produce decreased fractional reabsorption and luminal back-leak of fluid.","batch":1,"stem":"During ECF volume expansion, some of the isosmotic fluid that would normally be reabsorbed instead \"leaks back\" into the tubular lumen. Through which anatomic route does this back-leak occur?","choices":{"A":"Directly across the luminal apical brush border membrane of the proximal tubule cells","B":"Through the basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase operating in a reversed transport direction","C":"Across the leaky tight junctions between proximal tubule cells","D":"Through the glomerular capillary wall back into Bowman's space","E":"Through specialized aquaporin channels located in the thick ascending limb"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Isosmotic fluid accumulates in the lateral intercellular spaces between proximal tubule cells before being taken up by peritubular capillaries; when peritubular Starling forces favor reabsorption less strongly (as in ECF volume expansion), a portion of this fluid instead moves backward across the tight junctions, which are not truly \"tight\" but are permeable to small solutes and water, returning fluid to the tubular lumen rather than to peritubular capillary blood. The Na+-K+ ATPase pumps sodium out of the cell into the interstitium and does not run backward, and the thick ascending limb is impermeable to water, so it has no water route for the leak.","boardPrep":"The tight junctions between proximal tubule cells are the anatomic route for back-leak — this is the same paracellular pathway that, run in the opposite direction, normally contributes to forward reabsorption.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — this paracellular back-leak is the direct anatomic explanation for the decreased fractional reabsorption produced by unfavorable peritubular Starling forces","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate how extracellular fluid (ECF) volume expansion (such as isotonic saline infusion) alters peritubular capillary Starling forces (diluting oncotic pressure and raising hydrostatic pressure) to produce decreased fractional reabsorption and luminal back-leak of fluid.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does an increase in filtration fraction have the opposite Starling-force effect on peritubular capillaries compared with a pure ECF volume expansion from isotonic saline infusion?","choices":{"A":"A rising filtration fraction and isotonic saline both concentrate peritubular blood proteins equally","B":"Peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure is the single Starling force that changes in either scenario","C":"A rising filtration fraction affects only glomerular capillaries, sparing peritubular capillaries","D":"Isotonic saline increases the filtration fraction, making the two scenarios identical","E":"A rising filtration fraction concentrates peritubular blood proteins, while isotonic saline dilutes them"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A rising filtration fraction means a greater share of glomerular capillary plasma water is filtered out, leaving the remaining plasma proteins more concentrated in the blood that becomes peritubular capillary blood, raising peritubular oncotic pressure and favoring reabsorption; isotonic saline infusion, by contrast, adds protein-free fluid directly to the plasma, diluting plasma protein concentration throughout the circulation including peritubular capillary blood, lowering peritubular oncotic pressure and opposing reabsorption — the two scenarios drive peritubular oncotic pressure in opposite directions.","boardPrep":"Filtration fraction changes and ECF volume changes both work through peritubular Starling forces, but they are separate levers that can push oncotic pressure in opposite directions — do not conflate them.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — recognizing filtration fraction and ECF volume status as two distinct, independently variable inputs to peritubular Starling forces is a common integration point with Objective 1","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate how ECF volume contraction (such as diarrhea or vomiting) alters peritubular capillary Starling forces (concentrating oncotic pressure and lowering hydrostatic pressure) to produce a protective increase in proximal tubular fractional reabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with severe diarrhea has significant ECF volume contraction. Which pair of peritubular capillary Starling force changes results, and what proximal tubule effect follows?","choices":{"A":"Oncotic pressure falls because diarrhea dilutes plasma proteins, an effect opposite to volume contraction, sparing reabsorption in the proximal tubule","B":"The fall in intravascular volume lowers glomerular capillary pressure, leaving reabsorption in the proximal tubule unaffected","C":"Both Starling forces shift to oppose reabsorption in the proximal tubule, since contracted plasma volume lowers oncotic pressure","D":"Oncotic pressure rises and hydrostatic pressure falls, together increasing fractional reabsorption in the proximal tubule","E":"Neither Starling force changes, so reabsorption in the proximal tubule stays fixed since diarrhea affects the intestines and not plasma composition"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ECF volume contraction concentrates the remaining plasma proteins (raising peritubular capillary oncotic pressure) and lowers intravascular volume and pressure (lowering peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure); because oncotic pressure favors reabsorption and hydrostatic pressure opposes it, a rise in the former together with a fall in the latter both push toward increased reabsorption, raising fractional proximal reabsorption of isosmotic fluid as a protective response to volume loss.","boardPrep":"ECF volume contraction: peritubular oncotic pressure up, peritubular hydrostatic pressure down, both changes point the same direction — toward MORE reabsorption. This is the mirror image of volume expansion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — this pair of Starling force changes is the direct mechanistic explanation for why the kidney conserves sodium and water during volume depletion","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate how ECF volume contraction (such as diarrhea or vomiting) alters peritubular capillary Starling forces (concentrating oncotic pressure and lowering hydrostatic pressure) to produce a protective increase in proximal tubular fractional reabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Why is the increased proximal tubule reabsorption seen during ECF volume contraction described as a \"protective\" response?","choices":{"A":"It halts further glomerular filtration until ECF volume has been restored","B":"It shifts reabsorption preferentially toward potassium to protect against the specific losses caused by diarrhea","C":"It reabsorbs more of the filtered sodium and water that would otherwise have been excreted","D":"It reduces the oncotic pressure of peritubular capillary blood to compensate for the volume loss","E":"It shifts fluid from the intracellular compartment into the ECF compartment directly at the tubular level"},"correct":"C","explanation":"When ECF volume is already reduced by an external loss such as diarrhea or vomiting, excreting additional sodium and water in the urine would worsen the volume deficit; by increasing the fraction of filtered sodium and water reabsorbed rather than excreted, the proximal tubule retains more of what remains in the body, working to restore ECF volume toward normal rather than to further deplete it. Tubular reabsorption moves filtrate from the lumen into peritubular blood; it does not pull water out of cells.","boardPrep":"The \"protective\" label for increased proximal reabsorption during volume contraction simply means the kidney is conserving, not wasting, sodium and water at exactly the moment the body can least afford to lose more.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — this protective conservation response is the proximal tubule component of the broader systemic response to volume depletion that also involves the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate how ECF volume contraction (such as diarrhea or vomiting) alters peritubular capillary Starling forces (concentrating oncotic pressure and lowering hydrostatic pressure) to produce a protective increase in proximal tubular fractional reabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher artificially raises peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure in an experimental animal without changing peritubular oncotic pressure at all. What isolated effect on proximal tubule fractional reabsorption would this produce?","choices":{"A":"A decrease in fractional reabsorption, since elevated hydrostatic pressure by itself opposes reabsorption","B":"An increase in fractional reabsorption, since any rise in peritubular capillary pressure favors fluid uptake into the capillary","C":"No change in fractional reabsorption, since hydrostatic pressure has no independent effect apart from oncotic pressure","D":"A decrease in fractional reabsorption specifically if oncotic pressure is simultaneously lowered as well","E":"An increase in fractional reabsorption, restricted specifically to the late proximal tubule segment"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure and oncotic pressure act as independent, opposing Starling forces on isosmotic fluid reabsorption; hydrostatic pressure pushes fluid out of the capillary (opposing reabsorption) while oncotic pressure pulls fluid into the capillary (favoring reabsorption), so isolating a rise in hydrostatic pressure alone, with oncotic pressure unchanged, would shift the net Starling balance toward less reabsorption, independent of whatever oncotic pressure happens to be doing.","boardPrep":"Peritubular hydrostatic pressure and oncotic pressure are two separate, independently variable Starling forces — do not assume they always move together, since experimentally isolating one reveals its own independent effect.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — recognizing hydrostatic and oncotic pressure as independently manipulable variables is what allows this same Starling-force framework to explain both volume expansion and volume contraction with a single unified model","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Deconstruct the pathophysiologic cascade of contraction alkalosis (tracing how volume depletion activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system to stimulate proximal Na-H exchange) and compare this with the diuretic mechanism of poorly reabsorbed luminal osmotic agents (such as mannitol) in limiting proximal water and sodium reabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"In addition to the Starling-force mechanism, what second, hormonally driven mechanism increases proximal tubule reabsorption during ECF volume contraction?","choices":{"A":"Aldosterone directly stimulates proximal tubule Na-glucose cotransport activity","B":"Antidiuretic hormone directly stimulates proximal tubule Na-H exchange independent of angiotensin II","C":"Atrial natriuretic peptide directly stimulates proximal tubule sodium reabsorption during volume loss","D":"Parathyroid hormone directly stimulates proximal tubule bicarbonate reabsorption during volume loss","E":"Angiotensin II directly stimulates proximal tubule Na-H exchange (NHE3) activity"},"correct":"E","explanation":"ECF volume contraction lowers blood volume and arterial pressure, activating the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system; angiotensin II acts directly on proximal tubule cells to stimulate the Na-H exchanger (NHE3), which in turn stimulates coupled reabsorption of Na+, HCO3-, and water, providing a second reabsorption-boosting mechanism that operates alongside (not instead of) the peritubular Starling force changes.","boardPrep":"Two separate mechanisms increase proximal reabsorption during volume contraction: peritubular Starling forces (oncotic pressure up, hydrostatic pressure down) and angiotensin II-stimulated NHE3 — the second one is what specifically drives bicarbonate along with sodium and water.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 4 — this angiotensin II-stimulated NHE3 mechanism is the direct causal link between volume contraction and the development of contraction alkalosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Deconstruct the pathophysiologic cascade of contraction alkalosis (tracing how volume depletion activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system to stimulate proximal Na-H exchange) and compare this with the diuretic mechanism of poorly reabsorbed luminal osmotic agents (such as mannitol) in limiting proximal water and sodium reabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does angiotensin II-stimulated proximal Na-H exchange during ECF volume contraction specifically produce a metabolic alkalosis rather than simply conserving sodium in an acid-base-neutral way?","choices":{"A":"Because angiotensin II directly secretes hydrogen ions into the urine through a pathway separate from NHE3","B":"Because NHE3 activity is coupled to bicarbonate reabsorption, preferentially retaining bicarbonate","C":"Because volume contraction directly increases dietary bicarbonate absorption in the gastrointestinal tract","D":"Because angiotensin II suppresses renal ammonia production, indirectly raising plasma bicarbonate","E":"Because NHE3 stimulation causes potassium wasting, which independently raises plasma pH through a separate pathway"},"correct":"B","explanation":"NHE3 is the same exchanger responsible for early proximal bicarbonate reabsorption (secreted H+ combines with luminal HCO3- to form CO2 and water that diffuse into the cell and are reconstituted into HCO3- that exits into blood); when angiotensin II stimulates NHE3 activity during volume contraction, it does not just conserve sodium and water in isolation, it also preferentially increases bicarbonate reabsorption above baseline, raising plasma bicarbonate and producing the metabolic alkalosis known as contraction alkalosis.","boardPrep":"\"Contraction alkalosis\" is not a coincidental label — it exists specifically because the same transporter (NHE3) that angiotensin II uses to conserve volume also happens to reabsorb bicarbonate, so volume conservation and alkalosis occur together as a package.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 4 — this NHE3-mediated coupling of sodium and bicarbonate reabsorption is exactly the same early proximal mechanism introduced with acid-base disturbances in the mixed acid-base disorder objective","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Deconstruct the pathophysiologic cascade of contraction alkalosis (tracing how volume depletion activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system to stimulate proximal Na-H exchange) and compare this with the diuretic mechanism of poorly reabsorbed luminal osmotic agents (such as mannitol) in limiting proximal water and sodium reabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient receiving mannitol develops increased urinary excretion of both mannitol and sodium. What proximal tubule mechanism directly explains why unreabsorbed mannitol also causes increased sodium excretion?","choices":{"A":"Retained mannitol holds water in the lumen, diluting sodium and limiting later reabsorption","B":"Mannitol directly competes with sodium for the same luminal binding site on the Na-glucose cotransporter","C":"Mannitol is metabolized into an organic acid that directly inhibits the basolateral Na-K ATPase pump","D":"Mannitol raises peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure directly, independent of any luminal effect","E":"Mannitol binds directly to and blocks the apical Na-H exchanger throughout the proximal tubule"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because a poorly reabsorbed solute like mannitol remains in the tubular lumen, it holds water in the lumen osmotically and limits water reabsorption; as sodium is reabsorbed, the retained water dilutes the luminal sodium concentration progressively along the tubule, weakening the gradient for further sodium reabsorption and promoting backleak, so less sodium (and the water that follows it) is reabsorbed later on and more of both sodium and mannitol appear in the final urine.","boardPrep":"Osmotic diuretics work through the isosmotic-reabsorption requirement, not through direct transporter blockade: an unreabsorbed solute holds water in the lumen, dilutes luminal sodium, and indirectly drags sodium reabsorption down.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 4 — this same isosmotic-requirement logic explains why untreated diabetes mellitus, with its high filtered glucose load exceeding proximal reabsorptive capacity, produces an osmotic diuresis and ECF volume contraction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the protective feedback mechanism of glomerulotubular (GT) balance, explaining how it maintains a constant fractional proximal tubular reabsorption of 67 percent during spontaneous fluctuations in glomerular filtration rate.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man is enrolled in a renal physiology study. He is healthy, with a blood pressure of 124/78 mm Hg, a serum albumin of 4.3 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL), and a serum creatinine of 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). He receives an intrarenal infusion of a vasodilator titrated so that his renal plasma flow increases while his GFR stays constant. Which change in his filtration fraction and proximal tubule reabsorption is expected?","choices":{"A":"The filtration fraction rises, raising the peritubular oncotic pressure and increasing fractional reabsorption","B":"The filtration fraction falls, lowering peritubular oncotic pressure and decreasing fractional reabsorption","C":"The filtration fraction is unaffected by changes in RPF as long as GFR itself remains constant","D":"The filtration fraction falls, but peritubular oncotic pressure rises as a compensatory response","E":"The filtration fraction rises, but fractional reabsorption is unaffected since GFR itself did not change"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Filtration fraction equals GFR divided by RPF; if GFR is held constant while RPF rises, the filtration fraction must fall, meaning a smaller-than-usual fraction of the now-larger plasma flow is filtered out of glomerular capillary blood, so the remaining plasma proteins are diluted rather than concentrated, lowering peritubular capillary oncotic pressure and, by the same Starling-force logic used for GFR changes, decreasing fractional proximal reabsorption.","boardPrep":"Filtration fraction = GFR / RPF — changing either variable independently changes the filtration fraction and therefore peritubular oncotic pressure, even if the other variable stays fixed.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — this RPF-driven filtration fraction change demonstrates that glomerulotubular balance responds to the ratio GFR/RPF, not to GFR in isolation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the protective feedback mechanism of glomerulotubular (GT) balance, explaining how it maintains a constant fractional proximal tubular reabsorption of 67 percent during spontaneous fluctuations in glomerular filtration rate.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman with stage 3 chronic kidney disease is seen on hospital rounds. Blood pressure is 134/82 mm Hg. Serum creatinine is 1.7 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and albumin is 3.9 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). While the team discusses how her proximal tubules keep reabsorbing a constant fraction of the filtered load as her GFR varies, a medical student says that glomerulotubular balance works because the proximal tubule has its own sensor that measures GFR directly. Why is this description inaccurate?","choices":{"A":"The tubule responds to peritubular oncotic pressure, which follows filtration fraction","B":"A dedicated GFR receptor on the proximal tubule cell reports GFR itself","C":"Glomerulotubular balance is a property of the glomerulus itself","D":"The proximal tubule senses angiotensin II levels, which are independent of GFR or filtration fraction","E":"The description is accurate, since peritubular oncotic pressure is mathematically identical to GFR"},"correct":"A","explanation":"No receptor on the proximal tubule measures GFR. In the filtration-fraction model, the main link is indirect: a change in GFR relative to RPF alters the filtration fraction, which changes the protein concentration and oncotic pressure of the blood entering the peritubular capillaries, and that Starling force adjusts fractional reabsorption. Guyton notes that flow-dependent recruitment of luminal transporters such as NHE3 may also contribute, but neither mechanism is a dedicated GFR sensor. Glomerulotubular balance is a tubular, not glomerular, property; angiotensin II is not independent of filtration fraction; and peritubular oncotic pressure is not identical to GFR.","boardPrep":"Glomerulotubular balance is an indirect, Starling-force-mediated communication pathway, not a direct sensory mechanism: the proximal tubule responds mainly to peritubular Starling forces rather than sensing GFR itself.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — this distinction between direct sensing and indirect Starling-force mediation is a common source of conceptual confusion worth explicitly correcting","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Analyze the protective feedback mechanism of glomerulotubular (GT) balance, explaining how it maintains a constant fractional proximal tubular reabsorption of 67 percent during spontaneous fluctuations in glomerular filtration rate.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man volunteers for a renal physiology study. He is healthy, with a blood pressure of 118/74 mm Hg, a serum sodium of 140 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and a serum creatinine of 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Over the course of a day, his GFR fluctuates spontaneously between about 110 and 130 mL/min. If glomerulotubular balance functioned perfectly and with no delay, what would be the relationship between his filtered load of sodium and the amount of sodium delivered out of the proximal tubule to the loop of Henle?","choices":{"A":"A fixed absolute quantity would reach the loop of Henle no matter how the filtered load changes","B":"Downstream delivery would track changes in renal plasma flow, not glomerular filtration rate","C":"Delivery downstream would vanish whenever GFR rises and surge whenever GFR falls","D":"A constant 33% of the filtered load would reach the loop of Henle at each GFR","E":"A constant 67% of the filtered load would reach the loop of Henle at each GFR"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Because glomerulotubular balance keeps proximal reabsorption at a constant 67% of the filtered load, the fraction NOT reabsorbed (and therefore delivered onward to the loop of Henle) is the remaining 33%, regardless of whether GFR happens to be higher or lower than usual at a given moment; this constant-fraction delivery is precisely what limits the swings in downstream nephron sodium and water delivery that would otherwise occur with every spontaneous GFR fluctuation.","boardPrep":"If proximal reabsorption is a constant 67% of filtered load, delivery to the loop of Henle is the complementary constant 33% — both numbers move together as fixed fractions, never as fixed absolute amounts.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — this constant-fraction delivery to the loop of Henle is exactly why downstream nephron segments can be tuned to handle a relatively predictable, stable sodium and water load despite upstream GFR variability","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate how extracellular fluid (ECF) volume expansion (such as isotonic saline infusion) alters peritubular capillary Starling forces (diluting oncotic pressure and raising hydrostatic pressure) to produce decreased fractional reabsorption and luminal back-leak of fluid.","batch":2,"stem":"A 56-year-old man with cirrhosis undergoes large-volume paracentesis. Blood pressure is 106/64 mm Hg, and pulse is 88/min. Serum albumin is 2.6 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL), and creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). He then receives an infusion of concentrated albumin solution, which raises his plasma protein concentration, together with enough isotonic fluid to expand his extracellular fluid volume by the same amount as an equal volume of isotonic saline would. Compared with isotonic saline alone, how would his peritubular oncotic pressure respond, and what proximal tubule effect follows?","choices":{"A":"Peritubular oncotic pressure would still fall, since ECF volume expansion lowers oncotic pressure whatever is infused","B":"Peritubular oncotic pressure would rise, blunting or reversing the expected decrease in fractional reabsorption","C":"Peritubular oncotic pressure would be unaffected by either infusion, since hydrostatic pressure is what responds to volume changes","D":"Peritubular hydrostatic pressure would fall, canceling the entire volume expansion effect","E":"Fractional reabsorption would decrease even more sharply than with isotonic saline by itself, since two mechanisms now act together"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Isotonic saline expands ECF volume while diluting plasma proteins, lowering peritubular oncotic pressure; concentrated albumin, by contrast, adds protein along with volume, raising rather than lowering plasma (and therefore peritubular) oncotic pressure even as ECF volume expands, so the oncotic pressure component of the Starling balance would move in the opposite direction from a pure saline infusion, blunting or potentially reversing the decrease in fractional reabsorption that saline alone would produce, even though both infusions expand ECF volume equally.","boardPrep":"ECF volume expansion and peritubular oncotic pressure changes are not automatically linked — what is infused (protein-free fluid versus protein-containing fluid) determines the oncotic pressure direction independent of the volume effect itself.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — filtration fraction and infused protein both set peritubular oncotic pressure, which is what the proximal tubule responds to","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate how extracellular fluid (ECF) volume expansion (such as isotonic saline infusion) alters peritubular capillary Starling forces (diluting oncotic pressure and raising hydrostatic pressure) to produce decreased fractional reabsorption and luminal back-leak of fluid.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man undergoing a long orthopedic procedure receives 3 L of intravenous isotonic saline over 4 hours. Blood pressure is 132/80 mm Hg, and pulse is 72/min. Serum sodium is 141 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and albumin is 3.6 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL), lower than before surgery. His urine output and urinary sodium output increase over the next few hours, and the fraction of filtered sodium reabsorbed by his proximal tubules falls. Which statement best describes the contribution of this proximal tubule response to restoring his sodium balance?","choices":{"A":"The proximal tubule effect is the main mechanism, with distal segments contributing little to the excess excretion","B":"The proximal tubule effect is a minor and physiologically insignificant contributor to the excess excretion","C":"The proximal tubule effect is one of several contributors to the excess NaCl and water excretion","D":"The proximal tubule effect works mainly by raising renin release, which then increases NaCl excretion","E":"The proximal tubule mechanism reverses over time and eventually increases fractional reabsorption"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Decreased proximal fractional reabsorption is one of several mechanisms that help excrete excess NaCl and water during ECF volume expansion: it is a real, meaningful contributor, but one of several. Suppression of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, natriuretic peptide release, and altered Starling forces and transport in more distal segments act in parallel, so the proximal tubule's Starling-force-driven decrease in reabsorption is one coordinated piece of a larger response. Calling it the main mechanism overstates it, calling it insignificant understates it, it works through peritubular Starling forces rather than renin release (which is suppressed), and it persists rather than reversing while the volume expansion lasts.","boardPrep":"Renal responses to volume status changes are rarely explained by a single mechanism — recognizing the proximal tubule's Starling-force response as one contributor among several is important for not over-attributing the entire natriuretic response to this one pathway.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — ANP and BNP natriuresis acts in parallel with this proximal Starling-force response to saline loading","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Evaluate how extracellular fluid (ECF) volume expansion (such as isotonic saline infusion) alters peritubular capillary Starling forces (diluting oncotic pressure and raising hydrostatic pressure) to produce decreased fractional reabsorption and luminal back-leak of fluid.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman with long-standing malnutrition from severe dementia is evaluated in a nursing facility. She has no edema, and her mucous membranes are moist. Blood pressure is 118/72 mm Hg, and pulse is 76/min. Serum albumin is 2.3 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL), sodium is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and creatinine is 0.7 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL); her extracellular fluid volume is judged to be normal. Considering only the oncotic pressure component of peritubular Starling forces, what effect on proximal tubule reabsorption would be expected from her low albumin, independent of any change in volume status?","choices":{"A":"Decreased fractional reabsorption, since low plasma albumin lowers peritubular capillary oncotic pressure directly","B":"Increased fractional reabsorption, since low plasma albumin raises peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure directly","C":"No effect on fractional reabsorption, since plasma albumin concentration does not influence peritubular oncotic pressure","D":"Decreased fractional reabsorption, arising instead because low albumin directly raises peritubular hydrostatic pressure","E":"Increased fractional reabsorption, since the kidney compensates for low albumin by concentrating peritubular capillary blood"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Peritubular capillary oncotic pressure depends directly on the protein concentration of the blood that reaches it, which in turn depends heavily on plasma albumin, the major plasma protein; a patient with chronically low plasma albumin will have chronically low peritubular capillary oncotic pressure regardless of ECF volume status, and because oncotic pressure is the major Starling force favoring reabsorption, this lower oncotic pressure by itself would be expected to decrease fractional proximal reabsorption, independent of whatever volume-status mechanisms might also be operating.","boardPrep":"Plasma protein concentration (driven mainly by albumin) is a direct determinant of peritubular oncotic pressure on its own, separate from volume-status-driven changes in filtration fraction or ECF volume — a chronic disease state can shift this baseline independent of acute volume changes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 2 — the same low plasma oncotic pressure, acting at systemic rather than peritubular capillaries, drives edema in nephrotic syndrome","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate how ECF volume contraction (such as diarrhea or vomiting) alters peritubular capillary Starling forces (concentrating oncotic pressure and lowering hydrostatic pressure) to produce a protective increase in proximal tubular fractional reabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of 5 days of persistent vomiting from gastric outlet obstruction. Blood pressure is 98/62 mm Hg, and pulse is 108/min. His mucous membranes are dry. Serum sodium is 134 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), potassium is 3.0 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), chloride is 86 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), and bicarbonate is 36 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). He has both extracellular fluid volume contraction and ongoing loss of gastric hydrochloric acid. Which change in his proximal tubules most directly adds to his metabolic alkalosis?","choices":{"A":"The Starling-force mechanism directly removes hydrogen ions from the blood independent of any bicarbonate-related transporter","B":"RAAS activation during vomiting-induced volume contraction acts to lower plasma bicarbonate as a compensatory response to the ongoing acid loss","C":"Volume contraction from vomiting itself activates angiotensin II, which then stimulates NHE3 to reabsorb extra bicarbonate","D":"The two mechanisms act in opposing directions, so the net effect on plasma bicarbonate stays negligible despite both being active","E":"Angiotensin II during vomiting-induced volume contraction acts on the distal nephron and has no effect on proximal bicarbonate handling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vomiting causes both direct loss of gastric HCl (a primary metabolic alkalosis from acid loss) and ECF volume contraction from fluid loss; the resulting volume contraction independently activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, and angiotensin II's stimulation of proximal NHE3 reabsorbs extra bicarbonate along with sodium and water, adding a second, RAAS-driven bicarbonate-retaining mechanism on top of the original acid-loss-driven alkalosis, so the two processes compound rather than cancel, worsening the net alkalosis beyond what gastric acid loss alone would produce.","boardPrep":"In vomiting, contraction alkalosis is not just \"gastric acid was lost\" — the resulting volume contraction independently triggers angiotensin II-driven bicarbonate retention, compounding the alkalosis through a second mechanism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 2 — angiotensin II–stimulated proximal NHE3 is one of the renal mechanisms that maintain a metabolic alkalosis after vomiting","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate how ECF volume contraction (such as diarrhea or vomiting) alters peritubular capillary Starling forces (concentrating oncotic pressure and lowering hydrostatic pressure) to produce a protective increase in proximal tubular fractional reabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 23-year-old woman is admitted because of 3 days of vomiting from viral gastroenteritis. Pulse is 102/min, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. Her mucous membranes are dry. Serum creatinine is 0.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL), and bicarbonate is 31 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). In a research measurement, her GFR and renal plasma flow are both unchanged from baseline, so her filtration fraction is also unchanged, yet her fractional proximal reabsorption has increased. Does the glomerulotubular balance mechanism that operates during spontaneous GFR fluctuations explain this increase?","choices":{"A":"Yes, because glomerulotubular balance operates identically whether GFR, RPF, or ECF volume is the variable that changes","B":"Yes, because any ECF volume change necessarily alters the filtration fraction even when GFR and RPF both stay fixed","C":"No, because RAAS-driven NHE3 stimulation has replaced the Starling-force mechanism during this volume loss","D":"No, because the entire response comes from a rise in GFR itself despite RPF staying fixed","E":"No, this rise comes from ECF-volume-induced Starling-force changes, not from any filtration fraction change"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Glomerulotubular balance in response to spontaneous GFR fluctuations works specifically through changes in the filtration fraction (GFR/RPF), which alters peritubular oncotic pressure; here, however, GFR and RPF are both explicitly unchanged, so the filtration fraction cannot be the driver, yet fractional reabsorption still increases because ECF volume contraction independently concentrates plasma proteins and lowers peritubular capillary hydrostatic pressure, raising oncotic pressure and lowering hydrostatic pressure through a route separate from filtration fraction (angiotensin II-stimulated NHE3 adds a second, parallel mechanism in volume contraction); this shows that GFR-driven and ECF-volume-driven inputs converge on the same peritubular Starling forces through two mechanistically separate pathways.","boardPrep":"Glomerulotubular balance (via filtration fraction) and the ECF-volume-contraction response (via direct plasma protein concentration and hydrostatic pressure changes) are two distinct routes to the same peritubular Starling forces — one does not require the other to operate.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — this dissociation between the filtration-fraction route and the direct-volume route is the same distinction tested for ECF volume expansion versus filtration fraction changes in Objective 2","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate how ECF volume contraction (such as diarrhea or vomiting) alters peritubular capillary Starling forces (concentrating oncotic pressure and lowering hydrostatic pressure) to produce a protective increase in proximal tubular fractional reabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man with 4 days of profuse diarrhea is enrolled in a trial of an investigational agent. Pulse is 110/min, and blood pressure is 100/62 mm Hg. Serum sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and creatinine is 1.3 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). The agent selectively blocks the rise in peritubular capillary oncotic pressure that accompanies extracellular fluid volume contraction, without affecting the fall in peritubular hydrostatic pressure. What would be the most direct expected consequence for his kidneys' protective response to volume loss?","choices":{"A":"The protective increase in fractional proximal reabsorption would be blunted, since one of its two driving Starling forces would be removed","B":"The protective increase in fractional proximal reabsorption would be abolished, since the hydrostatic fall acts via the oncotic rise","C":"The protective response would be unaffected, since hydrostatic pressure changes by themselves are sufficient to drive the entire response","D":"The protective response would instead reverse into a decrease in fractional reabsorption, worsening the volume contraction","E":"The drug would have no effect on proximal reabsorption but would instead alter angiotensin II release directly"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The protective increase in fractional proximal reabsorption during ECF volume contraction results from the combined effect of two Starling forces changing in the same direction: rising peritubular oncotic pressure and falling peritubular hydrostatic pressure, both favoring reabsorption; if a drug selectively blocked only the oncotic pressure rise while leaving the hydrostatic pressure fall intact, the hydrostatic pressure component would still favor reabsorption on its own, so the overall protective response would be reduced in magnitude (blunted) rather than completely abolished or reversed, since one of the two contributing forces would still be operating normally.","boardPrep":"Because two independent Starling forces (oncotic pressure up, hydrostatic pressure down) both contribute to the volume-contraction reabsorption response, blocking only one force blunts rather than eliminates the overall effect — a useful way to test whether a concept has been understood as additive rather than all-or-none.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 4 — the same two peritubular Starling forces set bulk uptake of the isosmotic reabsorbate","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl12_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Deconstruct the pathophysiologic cascade of contraction alkalosis (tracing how volume depletion activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system to stimulate proximal Na-H exchange) and compare this with the diuretic mechanism of poorly reabsorbed luminal osmotic agents (such as mannitol) in limiting proximal water and sodium reabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl is brought to the emergency department because of 2 weeks of increasing thirst and urination, a 5-kg (11-lb) weight loss, and dizziness on standing. Pulse is 112/min, and blood pressure is 96/58 mm Hg, falling to 84/50 mm Hg on standing. Her mucous membranes are dry. Serum glucose is 540 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), sodium is 131 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), and creatinine is 1.3 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Urinalysis shows 4+ glucose. Which causal chain connects her elevated filtered glucose load to her extracellular fluid volume contraction?","choices":{"A":"Elevated filtered glucose damages proximal tubule cells, causing them to secrete sodium and water into the urine","B":"Elevated filtered glucose directly suppresses angiotensin II release, independently causing excess sodium and water excretion","C":"Elevated filtered glucose is converted into an osmotically inactive form that has no effect on luminal water handling","D":"Elevated filtered glucose competes with sodium for reabsorption via SGLT2 until it saturates, after which the effect stops","E":"Elevated filtered glucose exceeds proximal reabsorptive capacity, stays in the lumen, and limits sodium and water reabsorption"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In untreated diabetes mellitus, the filtered glucose load exceeds the proximal tubule's maximal reabsorptive capacity for glucose, so unreabsorbed glucose remains in the tubular lumen, acting like mannitol as an unreabsorbed osmotic agent for the portion above that capacity; it holds water in the lumen and limits water reabsorption, the retained water dilutes the luminal sodium concentration and weakens the gradient for further sodium reabsorption, so both glucose and excess sodium and water are excreted, and the ongoing water and sodium losses progressively contract ECF volume.","boardPrep":"Diabetic glucosuria causing volume contraction follows the same osmotic-diuresis logic as mannitol, applied to the glucose left above the reabsorptive capacity — recognizing untreated diabetes as a clinical example of endogenous osmotic diuresis links Objective 4's two component mechanisms together.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 3 — the volume contraction from osmotic diuresis then raises peritubular oncotic pressure and activates the renin-angiotensin system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Deconstruct the pathophysiologic cascade of contraction alkalosis (tracing how volume depletion activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system to stimulate proximal Na-H exchange) and compare this with the diuretic mechanism of poorly reabsorbed luminal osmotic agents (such as mannitol) in limiting proximal water and sodium reabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman is admitted because of weakness and light-headedness. She admits to taking large amounts of a diuretic that blocks the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter in the thick ascending limb, which she obtained to lose weight. Pulse is 110/min, and blood pressure is 94/60 mm Hg. Serum sodium is 133 mEq/L (reference range: 136–146 mEq/L), potassium is 2.9 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), chloride is 89 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), and bicarbonate is 34 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Does her volume contraction activate the proximal angiotensin II–NHE3 mechanism of contraction alkalosis, given that the diuretic acts in the thick ascending limb?","choices":{"A":"No, because osmotic diuretics acting specifically in the proximal tubule are what can trigger this volume-conservation mechanism","B":"Yes, because the resulting volume depletion activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system whatever its cause","C":"No, because loop diuretics directly block angiotensin II synthesis whatever the volume lost","D":"Yes, but requiring the loop diuretic to cause enough volume loss alongside an osmotic diuretic given at the same time","E":"No, because contraction alkalosis requires a primary bicarbonate loss driven by the diuretic's own site of action, separate from any volume change"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The angiotensin II-driven proximal NHE3 stimulation mechanism is triggered by the systemic consequence of ECF volume contraction (decreased blood volume and arterial pressure activating the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system), not by the specific nephron segment or mechanism responsible for causing that volume contraction; a loop diuretic causes volume contraction by blocking NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb, but the resulting drop in effective circulating volume activates RAAS just as readily as volume contraction from diarrhea, vomiting, or an osmotic diuretic would, so the same proximal angiotensin II/NHE3-driven contraction alkalosis mechanism is expected to contribute in this scenario as well (which is, in fact, why loop diuretic overuse is a recognized clinical cause of contraction alkalosis).","boardPrep":"Contraction alkalosis is triggered by the systemic fact of volume contraction and the RAAS activation it produces, not by the specific segment or drug causing the volume loss — this is why diverse causes (vomiting, loop diuretics, thiazide diuretics, heavy sweating) all converge on the same contraction alkalosis mechanism.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 4 — recognizing loop and thiazide diuretics as clinical causes of contraction alkalosis, despite acting on nephron segments far downstream of the proximal tubule, is a classic integration point tested across pharmacology and physiology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Deconstruct the pathophysiologic cascade of contraction alkalosis (tracing how volume depletion activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system to stimulate proximal Na-H exchange) and compare this with the diuretic mechanism of poorly reabsorbed luminal osmotic agents (such as mannitol) in limiting proximal water and sodium reabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with raised intracranial pressure after a head injury is enrolled in a trial comparing two osmotic agents. Blood pressure is 138/84 mm Hg, and his serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). One agent is mannitol, which is poorly reabsorbed and undergoes no cellular metabolism. The other is identical in every respect except that it is slowly taken up and metabolized by proximal tubule cells during transit through the lumen. Assuming both start at the same filtered concentration, would the slowly metabolized agent be expected to produce a weaker osmotic diuretic effect than mannitol?","choices":{"A":"No, because osmotic diuretic potency depends on the filtered concentration and is unaffected by any metabolism occurring afterward","B":"No, because metabolism of a luminal solute increases its osmotic effect by generating additional osmotically active byproducts","C":"Yes, but specifically because metabolism directly blocks the isosmotic reabsorption requirement itself","D":"Yes, because partial metabolism would lower its effective luminal concentration versus unmetabolized mannitol","E":"Yes, but specifically because metabolized solutes are actively secreted back into the lumen, which mannitol is not"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The osmotic diuretic effect depends specifically on the concentration of poorly reabsorbed solute remaining in the tubular lumen as it raises luminal osmolarity and disrupts the isosmotic reabsorption of sodium; mannitol's complete metabolic inertness means its full filtered amount stays in the lumen throughout its transit (since it is neither reabsorbed nor broken down), maximizing and sustaining its luminal osmotic contribution, whereas a solute that is partially metabolized during transit would have progressively less of itself remaining in luminal form, lowering its effective osmotic contribution compared with mannitol at the same starting filtered concentration, and therefore producing a weaker osmotic diuretic effect overall.","boardPrep":"Mannitol's effectiveness as the prototypical osmotic diuretic depends on being both unreabsorbed and unmetabolized, so that its full filtered amount stays in the lumen.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 4 — this same \"must stay in the lumen to work\" principle explains why glucose is a much less potent osmotic diuretic per filtered mole than mannitol whenever any glucose reabsorptive capacity remains intact","isHighYield":true}]};
